.CENTER
^&1: ^INTRODUCTION.\&
.SKIP 3
.SUBTITLE 1.1:  ^DESCRIPTION.
.CENTER
^&1.1:  ^DESCRIPTION.\&
.PARAGRAPH
^I^M^P IS A SIMPLE HIGHER-LEVEL LANGUAGE INTENDED PRIMARILY FOR SYSTEM
PROGRAMMING [^IRONS 1970], [^BILOFSKY 1972].  ^IT PROVIDES LANGUAGE FACILITIES
ROUGHLY AT THE LEVEL OF ^F^O^R^T^R^A^N ^I^I AND LEAVES THE PROGRAMMER THE FLEXIBILITY
OF MACHINE LANGUAGE PROGRAMMING: THE ADDRESS OF ANY VARIABLE CAN BE TAKEN,
MEMORY CAN BE ADDRESSED DIRECTLY, AND A WORD CAN BE TREATED AS BYTES AND BIT
FIELDS.  ^I^M^P72 IS THE VERSION OF ^I^M^P DEFINED BY THE COMPILER OF ^WALT ^BILOFSKY
FOR THE ^P^D^P-10.  ^THIS IMPLEMENTATION TRANSLATES THE SAME LANGUAGE TO NATIVE
CODE ON 64-BIT ^UNIX SYSTEMS, AND PROVIDES THE FOLLOWING FACILITIES:
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -3
1. ^EXTENSIBILITY.  ^THE USER MAY SPECIFY EXTENSIONS TO
THE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS OF THE LANGUAGE IN FORMS RANGING FROM
SIMPLE "MACROS" TO PRODUCTIONS WHICH GENERATE CALLS TO COMPILER
CODE-GENERATING ROUTINES.  ^MORE EFFICIENT OBJECT CODE MAY EASILY BE
SPECIFIED FOR SPECIAL CASES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^FLOATING POINT CAPABILITIES.  ^A ^R^E^A^L DATA TYPE AND FLOATING POINT
ARITHMETIC ARE PROVIDED; A ^R^E^A^L IS AN ^I^E^E^E 754 DOUBLE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^BYTE AND BIT FIELD MANIPULATION.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4. ^NO RESERVED WORDS IN THE SYNTAX.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
5. ^SYNTACTIC ERROR CORRECTION AND ADMISSIBILITY OF AMBIGUOUS SYNTAX.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
6. ^NATIVE CODE.  ^THE COMPILER WRITES ^L^L^V^M ^I^R, MAKES AN OBJECT FILE FOR THE
HOST OR FOR ANOTHER SYSTEM, AND LINKS IT WITH THE LIBRARY OF THE RUNTIME.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
7. ^CALLS TO ^[C^] FUNCTIONS, AND ROUTINES THAT ^[C^] CAN CALL, THROUGH THE ^[C^]
CALLING CONVENTION (^SECTION 2.4).
.SUBTITLE 1.2: ^IMPLEMENTATION.
.TEST PAGE 9
.SKIP 4
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.CENTER
^&1.2: ^IMPLEMENTATION.\&
.SKIP 2
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER IS A PROGRAM IN ^I^M^P72 AND COMPILES ITSELF.  ^ALMOST NONE OF THE
LANGUAGE IS BUILT INTO IT: A SMALL KERNEL (THE LEXER, THE PARSER, THE SYNTAX
GRAPH, THE CODE TREE MATCHER AND THE SEMANTIC STACK, ^SECTION 4) STARTS EMPTY,
AND THE FILE OF SYNTAX STATEMENTS ^^SYNTAX64.IMC\\ DEFINES THE LANGUAGE, EACH
STATEMENT CARRYING THE SEMANTIC ROUTINES THAT GENERATE ITS CODE (^SECTION 2.3).
^THE CODE IS GENERATED FOR AN ABSTRACT MACHINE OF 64-BIT WORDS; THE COMPILER
TRANSLATES IT TO ^L^L^V^M ^I^R AND MAKES THE OBJECT FILE WITH THE ^L^L^V^M LIBRARIES AND
LINKS IT WITH LLD, ALL IN ITS OWN PROCESS.  ^THE COMPILER NEVER RUNS A ^C
COMPILER OR AN ASSEMBLER.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER AND THE RUNTIME LIBRARY RUN ON ^LINUX, MAC^O^S, ^FREE^B^S^D, ^NET^B^S^D AND
^OPEN^B^S^D, ON THE PROCESSORS THAT ^L^L^V^M SUPPORTS THERE (X86-64, I386, AARCH64,
ARMV7, POWERPC64LE, RISCV64, S390X, LOONGARCH64).  ^PROGRAMS MAY BE COMPILED FOR
ANOTHER SYSTEM THAN THE ONE THE COMPILER RUNS ON (^SECTION 3.1.3).
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER IS PROVIDED UNDER THE LICENCE OF ^SECTION 5.  ^THE COMPILER IS
CALLED WITH THE COMMAND ^^IMP72\\, DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION 3.
.SUBTITLE 1.3: ^HOW TO ^USE ^I^M^P IN ^ONE ^EASY ^LESSON.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&1.3: ^HOW TO ^USE ^I^M^P IN ^ONE ^EASY ^LESSON\&
.SKIP 2
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS SECTION IS INTENDED TO GIVE THE EXPERIENCED PROGRAMMER THE
FLAVOR OF ^I^M^P SO THAT HE MAY WRITE SIMPLE PROGRAMS WITHOUT
HAVING TO READ THE ENTIRE MANUAL.
.PARAGRAPH
^I^M^P IS SIMILAR TO ^^FORTRAN II\\ IN POWER, AND A BIT LIKE
^^ALGOL\\ IN FLAVOR.  ^IT HAS NO BLOCK STRUCTURE OR RESERVED WORDS, AND ALL
VARIABLES ARE GLOBAL TO ALL PROGRAMS IN A COMPILATION.  ^STATEMENTS MAY BE
GROUPED, USING PARENTHESES.  ^VARIABLES NEED NOT BE DECLARED UNLESS
THEY ARE ARRAYS.  ^ARRAYS ARE DECLARED BY, E.G., ^^FOO IS 50 LONG\\
AND SUBSCRIPTS WRITTEN, E.G., ^^FOO[I+3]\\.  ^THE ASSIGNMENT
OPERATOR IS ^[:=^] (ONE TOKEN) AND THE STATEMENT SEPARATOR IS ";".  ^THE WORDS
OF THE LANGUAGE ARE WRITTEN IN CAPITAL LETTERS; NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE, SO
THAT ^^X\\ AND ^[x^] ARE TWO VARIABLES.  ^ALL BINARY
ARITHMETIC OPERATORS ARE OF EQUAL PRECEDENCE, AND OPERATIONS ARE
PERFORMED FROM RIGHT TO LEFT (AS IN ^A^P^L).  ^[:=^] IS OF THE SAME
PRECEDENCE AS THE ARITHMETIC OPERATORS AND ALSO IS
PERFORMED FROM RIGHT TO LEFT.
^RELATIONAL OPERATORS ARE EVALUATED AFTER ARITHMETIC OPERATORS.
.PARAGRAPH
^STATEMENTS MAY BE LABELED BY PREFIXING THEM WITH ^^LABEL:\\.
^THE TRANSFER OF CONTROL IS ^^GO TO LABEL\\ AND THE CONDITIONAL IS
^^A=>B\\ OR ^^A=>B ELSE C\\, WHERE ^B AND ^C ARE STATEMENTS OR GROUPS OF
STATEMENTS (IN PARENTHESES), AND ^A IS AN EXPRESSION (0 IS FALSE, NON-ZERO
IS TRUE), PERHAPS CONTAINING A RELATIONAL OPERATOR (^E^Q, ^N^E, ^G^E, ^G^T, ^L^E, ^L^T).
^THE BASIC ITERATION CONSTRUCT IS ^^A FOR B IN C,D,E\\, WHERE ^A IS A GROUP OF STATEMENTS
(IN PARENTHESES), ^B IS A VARIABLE, AND ^C, ^D AND ^E ARE EXPRESSIONS.
^A IS EXECUTED FOR VALUES OF ^B FROM ^C, INCREMENTED BY ^D, TO ^E.
.PARAGRAPH
^AN ^I^M^P SOURCE FILE CONSISTS OF A NUMBER OF STATEMENTS, IN FREE FORMAT.
^THE FILE IS TERMINATED BY "%%".  ^THE LAST STATEMENT EXECUTED SHOULD BE
^^FINI(0)\\, WHICH CLOSES THE FILES AND EXITS FROM THE PROGRAM.  ^A PROGRAM THAT
PRINTS THE SUM OF THE INTEGERS FROM 1 TO 10 IS
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^S^U^M:=0;
(^S^U^M:=^S^U^M+^I) ^F^O^R ^I ^I^N 1,1,10;
^P^R^I^N^T ^S^T^G 0,'^THE SUM IS ',^I^G^R 0,^S^U^M,/;
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^TO COMPILE THE ^I^M^P PROGRAM ON FILE ^[sum.imp^], CALL THE COMPILER BY THE COMMAND
^[imp72 sum.imp^], WHICH MAKES THE EXECUTABLE PROGRAM ^[a.out^]; ^[imp72 -o sum sum.imp^]
NAMES IT ^[sum^].  ^RUNNING IT PRINTS
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^THE SUM IS 55
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SUBTITLE 1.4: ^DIFFERENCES BETWEEN ^I^M^P72 ON ^UNIX AND THE ^ORIGINAL ^I^M^P72.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&1.4: ^DIFFERENCES BETWEEN ^I^M^P72 ON ^UNIX AND THE ^ORIGINAL ^I^M^P72.\&
.SKIP 2
.PARAGRAPH
^THE LANGUAGE IS THE LANGUAGE OF THE ORIGINAL COMPILER FOR THE ^P^D^P-10 UNDER
^T^O^P^S-10.  ^WHAT DIFFERS COMES FROM THE MACHINE AND FROM THE RUNTIME LIBRARY.
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -3
1. ^THE WORD IS 64 BITS.  ^INTEGERS ARE 64-BIT TWO'S COMPLEMENT NUMBERS, ADDRESSES ARE
BYTE ADDRESSES AND ^^A[I]\\ SCALES THE SUBSCRIPT BY 8.  ^THE ORIGINAL'S WORD WAS
36 BITS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^ASSIGNMENT IS THE TOKEN ^[:=^], WHERE THE ORIGINAL WROTE ^[_^].  ^THE CHARACTER ^[_^]
IS A LETTER OF NAMES.  ^NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE, AS THE ORIGINAL'S ARE, AND THE WORDS OF
THE LANGUAGE ARE THE CAPITAL-LETTER FORMS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^A ^R^E^A^L IS AN ^I^E^E^E 754 DOUBLE, NOT THE ^P^D^P-10'S 36-BIT FLOATING POINT NUMBER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4. ^A CHARACTER IS A BYTE AND TEXT IS ^U^T^F-8.  ^A STRING CONSTANT IS A
SEQUENCE OF BYTES ENDED BY A ZERO BYTE, NOT FIVE 7-BIT CHARACTERS TO A WORD.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
5. ^BYTE POINTERS TO CHARACTERS ARE BYTE ADDRESSES; THE HALVES OF A WORD ARE
32 BITS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
6. ^FILES ARE ^UNIX FILES, AND A FILE NAME IS AT MOST SEVEN CHARACTERS, WITH AN
EXTENSION OF AT MOST SEVEN.  ^SECTION 2.2.7.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
7. ^THE PROGRAM IS COMPILED TO NATIVE CODE AND LINKED TO AN EXECUTABLE BY
THE COMPILER.  ^THE COMPILER IS CALLED ^^IMP72\\ AND TAKES OPTIONS LIKE A ^C
COMPILER.  ^SECTION 3.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
8. ^THE REGISTERS 0^R TO 15^R ARE 64-BIT INTEGER VARIABLES OF EACH ROUTINE,
^E^X^E^C^U^T^E RUNS AN INSTRUCTION OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE, AND ^C^A^L^L^I HAS THE FEW
CALLS OF ^SECTION 2.2.9.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
9. ^C^C^A^L^L, ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L AND THE RULES FOR ^C^O^M^M^O^N LET A PROGRAM CALL ^[C^] AND BE CALLED
FROM ^[C^].  ^SECTION 2.4.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
10. ^THE SYNTAX FILE DEFINES THE SAME LANGUAGE AS THE ORIGINAL'S, WITH THE
OPERATION CODES OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE IN THE SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS INSTEAD OF
THE ^P^D^P-10'S.  ^SECTION 2.3.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SUBTITLE 2: ^PROGRAMMER'S ^GUIDE TO ^I^M^P72
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2: ^PROGRAMMER'S ^GUIDE TO ^I^M^P72\&
.SKIP 2
.SUBTITLE 2.1: ^CONVENTIONS.
.CENTER
^&2.1: ^CONVENTIONS.\&
.SKIP 2
.SUBTITLE 2.1.1: ^LEXICAL.
.CENTER
^&2.1.1: ^LEXICAL.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE LANGUAGE IS BASICALLY FREE-FORM, SUBJECT TO THE FOLLOWING
CONSTRAINTS.  ^WORDS REPRESENTING VARIABLE NAMES OR SPECIAL WORDS OF THE LANGUAGE ARE
DELIMITED BY CHARACTERS THAT ARE NOT LETTERS, DIGITS OR THE UNDERSCORE, INCLUDING SPACE.
^THEREFORE, SPACES MAY NOT APPEAR INSIDE WORDS OR NUMBERS, AND
AT LEAST ONE DELIMITER MUST APPEAR BETWEEN TWO SUCH WORDS.  ^ALL CHARACTERS
HAVING AN ^A^S^C^I^I CHARACTER CODE OF 40^B OR LESS ARE INTERPRETED AS BLANKS,
EXCEPT WHEN ENCLOSED IN !, _# OR ' SIGNS.  ^THUS, STRING CONSTANTS MAY
CONTAIN ANY CHARACTER, INCLUDING TABS AND RETURNS, AND RETURNS AND LINE FEEDS
OUTSIDE !, _# AND ' SIGNS SERVE AS DELIMITERS.  ^AS A GENERAL RULE, MAKE THE TEXT
READABLE AND YOU WILL FIND THE WORDS PROPERLY DISTINGUISHED.
.PARAGRAPH
^IDENTIFIERS CONSIST OF LETTERS, DIGITS AND THE UNDERSCORE, AND ARE CASE SENSITIVE:
^^COUNT\\, ^[Count^] AND ^[count^] ARE THREE NAMES.  ^THE WORDS OF THE LANGUAGE
(^^PRINT\\, ^^SUBR\\, ^^IS\\, ...) ARE WRITTEN IN CAPITAL LETTERS, SO THAT ^[print^] IS A NAME.
^A BYTE ABOVE 127 IS A LETTER, SO THAT THE ^U^T^F-8 TEXT OF A NAME IS PART OF IT.
^AN IDENTIFIER CONTAINING ANY SPECIAL CHARACTER (EXCEPT !) MAY BE REPRESENTED
BY ENCLOSING THE ENTIRE NAME IN ! SIGNS; E.G., !^FUNNY*ID.!
(SEE ^SECTION 2.2.1).
.PARAGRAPH
^ALL CHARACTERS APPEARING BETWEEN _# SIGNS ARE TREATED AS COMMENTS AND IGNORED BY
THE COMPILER (EXCEPT FOR _# SIGNS IN STRING CONSTANTS AND BETWEEN ! SIGNS).
^A COMMENT THAT IS NOT CLOSED BY A SECOND _# EXTENDS TO THE END OF THE FILE.
.SUBTITLE 2.1.2: ^EXPRESSIONS AND ^STATEMENTS.
.INDEX EXPRESSIONS
.INDEX STATEMENTS
.CENTER
^&2.1.2: ^EXPRESSIONS AND ^STATEMENTS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^IN ^I^M^P, NO DISTINCTION IS MADE BETWEEN EXPRESSIONS AND STATEMENTS.
^ALMOST EVERYTHING IS AN EXPRESSION, AND HAS A VALUE, ALTHOUGH A FEW EXPRESSIONS
LIKE ^G^O ^T^O ^X DO NOT HAVE A VERY USEFUL VALUE.  ^TWO CONSEQUENCES
OF THIS PHILOSOPHY ARE THAT PARENTHESES MAY BE USED FOR GROUPING
WHAT ARE USUALLY CALLED STATEMENTS IN OTHER LANGUAGES, AND THAT
THE STATEMENT SEPARATOR ';' BECOMES A BINARY OPERATOR (WHOSE
VALUE IS THE SECOND OPERAND).
.PARAGRAPH
^THE TERMS "STATEMENT" AND "EXPRESSION" WILL BE USED ALMOST
INTERCHANGEABLY HEREIN.  ^WHEN THE TERM "EXPRESSION" IS USED, IT SHOULD BE
UNDERSTOOD THAT WE MAY BE CONCERNED WITH THE VALUE OF THE EXPRESSION, WHEREAS
WHEN THE TERM "STATEMENT" APPEARS, WE ARE INTERESTED PRIMARILY IN THE EFFECT
OF EXECUTING IT, AND ONLY SECONDARILY, IF AT ALL, IN ITS VALUE.
.SUBTITLE 2.1.3: ^MACHINE ^LANGUAGE ^LEVEL ^PROGRAMMING.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.1.3: ^MACHINE ^LANGUAGE ^LEVEL ^PROGRAMMING.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R ^N^A^M^E^S
.INDEX ^M^A^C^H^I^N^E ^L^A^N^G^U^A^G^E ^P^R^O^G^R^A^M^M^I^N^G
.PARAGRAPH
^THE IDENTIFIERS 0^R,1^R,...,15^R NAME SIXTEEN REGISTERS.  ^A REGISTER IS A 64-BIT
INTEGER VARIABLE.  ^THE MAIN PROGRAM AND EACH ROUTINE HAVE A SET OF SIXTEEN
OF THEIR OWN, AND A REGISTER IS 0 WHEN THE ROUTINE IS ENTERED.  ^A CALL DOES NOT
CHANGE THE REGISTERS OF THE CALLER.  ^THE COMPILER'S OWN TEMPORARIES ARE NOT THESE
REGISTERS: THE MACHINE REGISTERS ARE ASSIGNED BY THE ^L^L^V^M CODE GENERATOR.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, THE
STATEMENTS ^[1R:=5; 2R:=1R+3; PRINT IGR 0,2R,/^] PRINT 8.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE STATEMENT ^E^X^E^C^U^T^E
(^SECTION 2.2.9) EXECUTES AN INSTRUCTION WORD OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE FOR WHICH
THE COMPILER GENERATES CODE, AND SYNTAX STATEMENTS (^SECTION 2.3) CAN USE EVERY
OPERATION OF THAT MACHINE.
.SUBTITLE 2.1.4: ^PROGRAM ^STRUCTURE AND ^SCOPE OF ^VARIABLES.
.CENTER
^&2.1.4: ^PROGRAM ^STRUCTURE AND ^SCOPE OF ^VARIABLES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^PROGRAM STRUCTURE IS MORE OR LESS LIKE THAT OF THE ^F^O^R^T^R^A^N SYSTEM.
^THUS A MAIN PROGRAM, IF ANY, COMES FIRST, THEN SUBROUTINES, IF ANY, FOLLOW.
^THE MAIN PROGRAM IS THE STATEMENTS BEFORE THE FIRST ^S^U^B^R; IT IS COMPILED TO THE FUNCTION
^[impmain^] THAT THE RUNTIME LIBRARY'S ^[main^] CALLS (^SECTION 2.4.4).
.PARAGRAPH
^IN CONTRAST TO THE ^F^O^R^T^R^A^N CONVENTION, ALL VARIABLES IN ^I^M^P ARE GLOBAL TO THE ENTIRE
SET OF SUBROUTINES COMPILED AT THE SAME TIME, EXCEPT THAT FORMAL ARGUMENTS OF A
SUBROUTINE ARE LOCAL WITHIN THAT SUBROUTINE.  ^A SET OF SUBROUTINES COMPILED AT THE SAME TIME IS
ONE SOURCE FILE.  ^THAT IS, ANY TWO REFERENCES TO THE SAME VARIABLE NAME WITHIN A
SINGLE ^I^M^P SOURCE FILE WILL REFER TO THE SAME MEMORY LOCATION, EVEN IF THE REFERENCES ARE WITHIN
DIFFERENT SUBROUTINES.  ^A VARIABLE THAT IS NOT DECLARED ^C^O^M^M^O^N BELONGS TO ITS
SOURCE FILE: ANOTHER FILE HAS A VARIABLE OF ITS OWN WITH THE SAME NAME, AND ^[C^] CANNOT NAME IT.
^THE ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLES ARE THE ONES SHARED BETWEEN FILES AND WITH ^[C^] (^SECTION 2.4.3).
^HOWEVER, REFERENCES TO FORMAL ARGUMENTS OF SUBROUTINES WILL HAVE THE EFFECT OF
REFERENCES TO THE MEMORY LOCATION CONTAINING THE ACTUAL ARGUMENT IN THE CALL OF
THE SUBROUTINE.  ^THUS REFERENCES TO FORMAL SUBROUTINE ARGUMENTS WILL NOT AFFECT VARIABLES
OF THE SAME NAME IN OTHER SUBPROGRAMS IN THE SAME COMPILATION.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE DECLARATION ^L^O^C^A^L IS PROVIDED TO DEFEAT THE FACT THAT VARIABLES ARE NORMALLY
GLOBAL TO THE ENTIRE FILE.  ^AN IDENTIFIER WHICH IS DECLARED TO BE ^L^O^C^A^L WILL BE
REPLACED THROUGHOUT THE SUBROUTINE IN WHICH IT IS SO DECLARED BY A UNIQUE NAME,
WHICH WILL NOT BE USED OUTSIDE THE SUBROUTINE.  ^THE IDENTIFIER WILL THEREFORE APPEAR
LOCAL TO THE SUBROUTINE.  ^A ^L^O^C^A^L VARIABLE IS STATIC: IT KEEPS ITS VALUE FROM ONE
CALL TO THE NEXT.
.PARAGRAPH
^BECAUSE EVERY VARIABLE IS STATIC, AND THE ARGUMENTS OF A CALL ARE EVALUATED INTO
TEMPORARIES THAT ARE ALSO STATIC, A SUBROUTINE CANNOT USEFULLY CALL ITSELF.  ^RECURSION IS
PROGRAMMED WITH A STACK OF THE PROGRAM'S OWN.
.PARAGRAPH
^I^M^P USES THE CALLING CONVENTION OF ^F^O^R^T^R^A^N, IN WHICH THE ARGUMENTS ARE ADDRESSES
(^SECTION 2.2.5), AND THE ^[C^] CALLING CONVENTION FOR THE NAMES IT EXPORTS
(^SECTION 2.4).  ^I^M^P AND ^[C^] PROGRAMS MAY CALL EACH OTHER.
.SUBTITLE 2.1.5: ^COMPILER ^VERSION.
.CENTER
^&2.1.5: ^COMPILER ^VERSION.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^V^E^R^S^I^O^N ^N^U^M^B^E^R ^O^F ^C^O^M^P^I^L^E^R
.PARAGRAPH
^THE VERSION OF THE COMPILER IS PRINTED BY THE COMMAND ^[imp72 --version^] (THE
TEXT ^[imp72 0.10.0^]) AND APPEARS, WITH THE DATE, IN THE BANNER OF THE INTERACTIVE
MODE (^SECTION 3.1.2).  ^AN EXTENDED COMPILER (^SECTION 3.4) HAS THE VERSION NUMBER OF THE
COMPILER IT WAS MADE FROM.
.SUBTITLE 2.2:  ^THE ^EXPRESSIONS.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.2:  ^THE ^EXPRESSIONS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
.INDEX ORDER OF EVALUATION
.INDEX EXPRESSIONS
^THE EXPRESSIONS OF THE LANGUAGE ARE LISTED HERE.  ^EXPRESSIONS MAY BE
GROUPED EXPLICITLY BY PARENTHESES.  ^WHERE PARENTHESES ARE OMITTED, THE FOLLOWING
IS THE ORDER IN WHICH SUBEXPRESSIONS ARE EVALUATED:
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
1. ^UNARY OPERATORS AND FUNCTIONS.
.SKIP
2. ^BINARY OPERATORS EXCEPT ";" (BUT INCLUDING ":=").
.SKIP
3. ^RELATIONAL OPERATORS.
.SKIP
4. ^CONDITIONALS.
.SKIP
5. ";".
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^WHEN TWO OPERATORS OF EQUAL PRECEDENCE APPEAR CONSECUTIVELY IN AN EXPRESSION, THE RIGHTMOST IS PERFORMED FIRST (AS IN ^A^P^L).  ^E.G., ^A*^B+^C IS EVALUATED
AS ^A*(^B+^C).  ^E^X^C^E^P^T^I^O^N: THE ORDER OF EVALUATION OF RELATIONAL OPERATORS ON THE
SAME LEVEL - E.G., ^A<^B ^G^E ^C - IS NOT DEFINED.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN "^A; ^B", ^A IS EVALUATED BEFORE ^B.  ^IN A CONDITIONAL, THE IMPLICAND WILL
BE EVALUATED AFTER THE CONDITION IS EVALUATED, IF AT ALL.  ^OTHER THAN THE
RESTRICTIONS LISTED ABOVE, ^I^M^P PLACES NO RESTRICTIONS ON THE ORDER OF EVALUATION
OF ARGUMENTS OF OPERATORS.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THE FOLLOWING TABLE, IF NO VALUE IS SPECIFIED FOR AN
EXPRESSION, THEN THE VALUE OF THAT EXPRESSION IS NOT DEFINED AND/OR
NOT USEFUL.
.SKIP 4
.TAB STOPS 20
.SUBTITLE 2.2.1:  ^VARIABLES AND ^CONSTANTS
.INDEX ^V^A^R^I^A^B^L^E^S
.INDEX ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T^S
.INDEX ^F^L^E^X^A^D^E^C^I^M^A^L
.CENTER
^&2.2.1:  ^VARIABLES AND ^CONSTANTS\&
.SKIP 2
.NOFILL
^S^A^M^P^L^E ^E^X^P^R^E^S^S^I^O^N   ^M^E^A^N^I^N^G
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP 2
.INDENT -15
19576
.BREAK
^INTEGER NUMBERS (BASE 10).  ^AN INTEGER IS A 64-BIT TWO'S COMPLEMENT NUMBER; A
CONSTANT LARGER THAN ^[2**63-1^] IS TAKEN MODULO ^[2**64^].
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
777000^B
.BREAK
^DIGITS FOLLOWED BY '^B': OCTAL CONSTANTS.  ^THE CONSTANT 1777777777777777777777^B
IS -1.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
2^A^F^B16
.BREAK
^A STRING OF DIGITS AND LETTERS BEGINNING WITH A DIGIT AND
ENDING IN '^BNN', WHERE NN IS A DECIMAL NUMBER.  ^THESE ARE
CONSTANTS IN BASE NN (CALLED "FLEXADECIMAL" CONSTANTS).
^THE LETTERS AND DIGITS TO THE LEFT OF THE LAST ^B ARE
INTERPRETED AS A CONSTANT IN BASE NN.  ^E.G., 2^A^B^B16 IS
THE BASE 16 CONSTANT 2^A^B (683 DECIMAL).  10100^B2 IS THE
BINARY CONSTANT 10100, OR 20 DECIMAL.  ^THE BASE MAY BE
ARBITRARILY LARGE, BUT ONLY DIGITS AND THE LETTERS ^A-^Z MAY
BE USED TO REPRESENT DIGITS.  (^A-^Z ARE THE DIGITS 10-35.)  ^NOTE THAT A CONSTANT STARTING WITH A LETTER
IS NOT LEGAL.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, ^A^B^B12 IS A VARIABLE NAME; THE
DESIRED CONSTANT MAY BE WRITTEN 0^A^B^B12.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
3.27"-5
.BREAK
^FLOATING POINT CONSTANT.  ^CONSISTS OF A STRING OF DIGITS CONTAINING AN EMBEDDED DECIMAL POINT
(I.E., .01 AND 3. ARE NOT LEGAL), AND FOLLOWED OPTIONALLY BY " AND A POWER-OF-TEN
SCALING FACTOR, AN INTEGER WRITTEN WITH A MINUS SIGN IF IT IS NEGATIVE AND WITH
NO PLUS SIGN.  ^THE VALUE IS THE ^R^E^A^L (AN ^I^E^E^E 754 DOUBLE) NEAREST TO THE NUMBER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A
.BREAK
^VARIABLE NAME.  ^ANY STRING OF LETTERS, DIGITS AND UNDERSCORES NOT INTERPRETABLE AS ONE OF
THE ABOVE FORMS OF CONSTANTS.  ^NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
9^R
.BREAK
^NUMBERS 0 THROUGH 15 FOLLOWED BY '^R' NAME THE CORRESPONDING REGISTERS (^SECTION 2.1.3).
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
'^A^B^CDEF'
.BREAK
^STRING CONSTANTS: A SEQUENCE OF BYTES (THE TEXT IS ^U^T^F-8) FOLLOWED BY A ZERO
BYTE, STORED IN SUCCESSIVE WORDS.  ^THE VALUE OF A STRING OF EIGHT BYTES OR FEWER IS THE WORD THAT HOLDS IT, WITH THE
FIRST BYTE IN THE LEAST SIGNIFICANT BYTE, WHICH IS THE ORDER OF THE BYTES IN MEMORY.  ^THUS AFTER ^[S:='ABC'^]
THE VARIABLE ^S HOLDS THE STRING ^A^B^C, AND ^[PRINT STG 0,S^] PRINTS IT.  ^THE VALUE OF
A LONGER STRING IS ITS FIRST EIGHT BYTES; THE ADDRESS OF THE STRING IS ^[LOC('text')^], WHICH IS
WHAT A ^C FUNCTION EXPECTS (^SECTION 2.4).  ^THE CHARACTER ' IS REPRESENTED WITHIN A STRING BY TWO
SUCCESSIVE QUOTES: ''.  ^THUS, THE STRING CONSISTING OF ONE SINGLE QUOTE WOULD BE WRITTEN '''' (BUT
WOULD PRINT AS ').
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
!^ANY THING.!
.BREAK
^SPECIAL IDENTIFIER: ANY STRING OF CHARACTERS NOT CONTAINING A '!', BRACKETED BY '!'S.  ^THE STRING
OF CHARACTERS IS CONSIDERED TO BE A SINGLE IDENTIFIER OR SYMBOL.  ^IN PARTICULAR,
THE FOLLOWING INTERPRETATIONS ARE MADE:
.BREAK
.INDENT 3
^ONE SPECIAL CHARACTER IS RECOGNIZED AS ITSELF.  ^E.G., !+! IS THE SAME AS +.
.INDENT 3
^ANY STRING STARTING WITH A SINGLE QUOTE IS RECOGNIZED AS THE STRING CONSTANT
CONTAINING THE REMAINDER OF THE STRING.  ^E.G., !'^DON'T! IS THE STRING CONSTANT "^DON'T".
.INDENT 3
^ANY OTHER STRING IS INTERPRETED AS AN IDENTIFIER WHOSE NAME IS EXACTLY THE STRING
APPEARING BETWEEN THE ! SIGNS.  ^THIS ALLOWS VARIABLE NAMES CONTAINING
SPECIAL CHARACTERS.  ^IN THE OBJECT FILE THE NAME IS A SYMBOL IN WHICH EACH BYTE THAT IS
NOT A LETTER, A DIGIT OR AN UNDERSCORE IS WRITTEN AS AN UNDERSCORE AND ITS TWO
HEXADECIMAL DIGITS: !^FUNNY*ID.! IS THE SYMBOL ^[Funny_2aid_2e^].
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A[^E]
.BREAK
^THE ^E'TH WORD OF ARRAY '^A' STARTING FROM ZERO.  ^A IS ANY VARIABLE NAME OR
CONSTANT, AND ^E IS ANY EXPRESSION.  ^THE ADDRESS OF THE WORD IS THE ADDRESS OF
^A PLUS 8 TIMES ^E.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
[^E]
.BREAK
^THE ^E'TH WORD OF MEMORY: THUS THE CONTENTS OF THE
WORD WHOSE ADDRESS IS ^E.  ^AN ADDRESS IS A BYTE ADDRESS.  ^THE EXPRESSION
[^P+8], IN WHICH ^P IS A VARIABLE AND 8 IS A CONSTANT, IS THE WORD 8 WORDS
AFTER ^P, BECAUSE THE CONSTANT IS TAKEN AS A SUBSCRIPT; ANY OTHER EXPRESSION, SUCH AS [^P+(8*^I)]
OR [^L^O^C(^W)+8], IS THE BYTE ADDRESS IT COMPUTES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
.INDEX ^L^O^C
^L^O^C(^A)
.BREAK
^THE ADDRESS OF THE MEMORY LOCATION CONTAINING ^A.  ^A MAY
BE A SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE.
.SKIP 4
