.SUBTITLE 2.2.4: ^CONTROL ^EXPRESSIONS.
.CENTER
^&2.2.4: ^CONTROL ^EXPRESSIONS.\&
.INDEX CONTROL EXPRESSIONS
.INDEX CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS

.SKIP 2
.INDENT -15
^A=>^B
.BREAK
^IF ^A IS NOT 0, EVALUATE ^B (^READ AS "^A IMPLIES ^B").  ^THUS,
^K=>^X:=1 DOES NOTHING IF ^K IS ZERO, OR SETS ^X TO 1 IF ^K IS
NON-ZERO.  ^X<^Y=>(^P:=1;^Q:=2) CHANGES ^P AND ^Q IF ^X IS
LESS THAN ^Y, OTHERWISE DOES NOTHING.  ^N^O^T^A ^B^E^N^E: ^IF ^A IS
A RELATIONAL EXPRESSION, IT NEED NOT, AND FOR BEST OBJECT CODE
SHOULD NOT, BE ENCLOSED IN PARENTHESES. ^IF ^A IS A CONSTANT,
OR AN EXPRESSION INVOLVING ONLY CONSTANTS, THEN THE CODE
GENERATED BY THE COMPILER FOR THE EXPRESSION ^A=>^B WILL
CONSIST EITHER OF ^B, IF ^A IS NON-ZERO, OR NOTHING AT ALL,
IF ^A IS ZERO.  ^THIS ALLOWS CODE TO BE COMPILED CONDITIONALLY IF, FOR EXAMPLE, ^A IS AN IDENTIFIER DEFINED BY SYNTAX TO
BE A CONSTANT.  ^IN THE CASE THAT ^B IS NOT COMPILED DUE TO
^A BEING A CONSTANT 0, ANY TAGS CONTAINED WITHIN ^B WILL NOT
BE DEFINED, BUT DECLARATIONS CONTAINED IN ^B WILL TAKE
EFFECT.
.INDEX ^E^L^S^E
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A=>^B ^E^L^S^E ^C
.BREAK
^THIS EXPRESSION HAS THE VALUE ^B IF ^A IS NONZERO, AND ^C IF
^A IS ZERO.   ^IT WILL EVALUATE ONLY ONE OF ITS OPERANDS EACH
TIME IT IS EXECUTED; THUS IT CAN BE USED TO REPLACE THE
CONSTRUCT "^A=>(^B; ^G^O ^T^O ^F^O^O); ^C; ^F^O^O:" IF THE USER DOES NOT
MIND THE VALUE BEING COMPUTED AND IGNORED (WHICH COSTS A FEW EXTRA INSTRUCTIONS OF OBJECT CODE).  ^N^O^T^A ^B^E^N^E: ^THE
EXPRESSION ^A:=^B=>^X ^E^L^S^E ^Y, E.G., WILL BE INTERPRETED AS
(^A:=^B)=>^X ^E^L^S^E ^Y.  ^TO ASSIGN THE OBVIOUS INTERPRETATION TO
IT, WRITE ^A:=(^B=>^X ^E^L^S^E ^Y).  ^IF ^C CONTAINS ANOTHER =>-^E^L^S^E
CLAUSE, THEN ^C SHOULD BE ENCLOSED IN PARENTHESES.
.INDEX ^F^O^R LOOPS
.INDEX ITERATION
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A ^F^O^R ^B ^I^N ^C,^D,^E
.INDENT -15
^A ^F^O^R ^B ^T^O ^E
.INDENT -15
^A ^F^O^R ^B ^F^R^O^M ^E
.BREAK
^THESE EXPRESSIONS PERFORM REPEATED EXECUTION OF THE
EXPRESSION ^A WITH DIFFERENT VALUES OF THE VARIABLE ^B.  ^THE ^I^N FORM
EXECUTES ^A WITH VALUES OF ^B EQUAL TO ^C, ^C+^D, ^C+2*^D,...,
^E; THE ^T^O FORM FOR ^B EQUAL TO 0,1,...,^E, AND THE ^F^R^O^M
FORM FOR ^B EQUAL TO ^E,^E-1,...,0.  ^IN EVERY CASE THE
EXPRESSION ^A IS EVALUATED AT LEAST ONCE REGARDLESS OF THE
VALUES OF ^C, ^D AND ^E.  ^IN THE ^I^N CASE WHEN ^D IS NOT A
CONSTANT, ^B MUST EXACTLY REACH THE VALUE OF ^E.  ^E.G., IN
.SKIP
.JUSTIFY OFF
.NOFILL
          ^^I:=2;\\
          ^^(A[J]:=0) FOR J IN 0,I,9\\
.SKIP
.FILL
THE LOOP WILL NEVER TERMINATE.  ^IN ALL OTHER CASES, THE LOOP
TERMINATES WHEN ^B PASSES THE TERMINAL VALUE.
^C, ^D AND ^E MAY BE ANY EXPRESSIONS, BUT IN THE ^I^N AND ^T^O
CASES ^D AND ^E WILL BE EVALUATED ONCE PER ITERATION OF THE
LOOP.  ^THE ^F^R^O^M CASE GENERATES THE MOST EFFICIENT CODE AND
IS THEREFORE TO BE PREFERRED.
^IF CONTROL IS TRANFERRED FROM WITHIN THE LOOP, THE
VALUE OF THE INDEX VARIABLE ^B IS PRESERVED.  ^THE VALUE OF ^B
FOLLOWING NORMAL TERMINATION OF THE LOOP IS UNDEFINED.
.INDENT 5
^THE SPECIAL CASES ^^(A[I]:=B[I]) FOR I TO/FROM V\\, WHERE ^A AND ^B
ARE INTEGER ARRAYS AND ^V IS A SINGLE VARIABLE OR CONSTANT, ARE COMPILED
AS A BLOCK TRANSFER INSTRUCTION.  ^THE USER IS CAUTIONED THAT IN THE
^^FROM\\ CASE, IF ^A AND ^B OVERLAP, THE CODE PRODUCED WILL BE
INCORRECT.  ^IN MORE GENERAL CASES, BLOCK TRANSFERS MAY BE
SPECIFIED BY THE ^^MOVE\\ CONSTRUCT (^SECTION 2.2.9).
.INDEX BLOCK TRANSFERS
.INDEX ^W^H^I^L^E LOOPS
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^W^H^I^L^E ^A ^D^O ^B
.BREAK
^REPEATEDLY EVALUATES THE EXPRESSION ^A, AND, IF IT
IT IS NONZERO (TRUE), EVALUATES THE EXPRESSION ^B.
^IF ^A IS ZERO (FALSE), CONTROL PASSES TO THE NEXT
STATEMENT.  ^IF ^A IS INITIALLY ZERO, ^B IS NEVER EXECUTED.
.INDEX ^^UNTIL\\
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^^A UNTIL B\\
.BREAK
^THIS EXPRESSION EVALUATES THE EXPRESSION ^A, THEN EVALUATES ^B AND IF
^B IS FALSE (ZERO) IT REPEATS.  ^EVEN IF ^B IS INITIALLY TRUE, ^A IS
ALWAYS EXECUTED AT LEAST ONCE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
((^A=^B) ^A^N^D (^C=^D)) => ^E
.INDENT -15
((^A=^B) ^O^R (^C=^D)) => ^E ^E^L^S^E ^F
.BREAK
^A CONDITION MAY BE SEVERAL RELATIONS JOINED BY ^A^N^D OR ^O^R, EACH RELATION IN PARENTHESES, IN A CONDITIONAL, AFTER ^W^H^I^L^E OR AFTER ^U^N^T^I^L.  ^THE RELATIONS
ARE EVALUATED FROM LEFT TO RIGHT AND NO MORE OF THEM THAN NECESSARY ARE EVALUATED.  ^A CONDITION OF ONE RELATION IN PARENTHESES
ALONE, AS IN ^[(A=1) => S^], IS A SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY: WRITE ^[A=1 => S^].
.INDEX ^G^O ^T^O
.INDEX ^T^R^A^N^S^F^E^R ^O^F ^C^O^N^T^R^O^L
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^G^O ^T^O ^E
.BREAK
^TRANSFER CONTROL TO THE TAG ^E.  ^A ^G^O ^T^O IN A SUBROUTINE MAY NAME A TAG OF THE
MAIN PROGRAM: CONTROL PASSES THERE, AND THE SUBROUTINES CALLED ON THE WAY ARE
LEFT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^G^O ^T^O (^L0,^L1,...,^L^N) ^E
.BREAK
^THIS EXPRESSION TRANSFERS CONTROL TO THE LABEL ^LI,
WHERE I IS THE VALUE OF EXPRESSION ^E, AND ^L0,...,^L^N
ARE IDENTIFIERS.  ^IF ^E<0 OR ^E>^N, CONTROL CONTINUES WITH THE STATEMENT AFTER THE ^G^O ^T^O.
.INDEX ^T^A^G^S
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^T:^A
.BREAK
^THE EXPRESSION ^A IS TAGGED WITH THE LABEL ^T.  ^TWO USES OF
THIS ARE: ^G^O ^T^O ^T TRANSFERS CONTROL TO THIS POINT IN THE
PROGRAM, AND, WHEN ^A IS A ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT, THE VARIABLE ^T IS THE FIRST
WORD OF THE DATA (^SECTION 2.2.8).
^TO INSERT A TAG WHERE AN EXPRESSION DOES NOT IMMEDIATELY FOLLOW,
SUCH AS BEFORE A ')', WRITE ^T^A^G:0.
.SUBTITLE 2.2.5: ^PROGRAMS, ^SUBPROGRAMS AND ^SUBPROGRAM ^CALLS.
.CENTER
^&2.2.5: ^PROGRAMS, ^SUBPROGRAMS AND ^SUBPROGRAM ^CALLS.\&
.SKIP
.SKIP 2
.INDENT -15
^A%%
.BREAK
^AN ^I^M^P PROGRAM CONSISTS OF AN ^I^M^P EXPRESSION FOLLOWED BY
TWO % SIGNS.  ^THE % SIGNS TERMINATE THE INPUT FILE AS FAR AS
THE COMPILER IS CONCERNED.  ^THE FILE ^[sum.imp^] OF ^SECTION 1.3 IS SUCH A PROGRAM.
.INDEX ^S^U^B^R
.INDEX ^S^U^B^R^O^U^T^I^N^E ^D^E^F^I^N^I^T^I^O^N^S
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^S^U^B^R ^A(^B,^C) ^I^S ^D
.BREAK
^DEFINE A SUBROUTINE (OR FUNCTION) NAMED ^A, WITH FORMAL
ARGUMENTS ^B AND ^C.  ^THE VALUE OF THE SUBROUTINE  IS ^D,
(UNLESS THE SUBROUTINE IS EXITED VIA A ^R^E^T^U^R^N STATEMENT,
Q.V.).  ^THE NORMAL EXIT FROM THE SUBROUTINE IS BY "RUNNING
OFF THE END" OF THE EXPRESSION ^D.  ^EXAMPLE:
.SKIP
^S^U^B^R ^A^B^S(^A) ^I^S (^A<0=>-^A ^E^L^S^E ^A);
.SKIP
IS A SUBROUTINE WHOSE VALUE IS THE ABSOLUTE VALUE OF ITS
ARGUMENT. ^WITH THIS DEFINITION ^[P:=ABS(Q)^] CALLS THE SUBROUTINE ^A^B^S AND SETS ^P TO THE VALUE OF ^Q.
.INDEX ^S^U^B^R^O^U^T^I^N^E ^L^I^N^K^A^G^E
^THE ARGUMENTS OF A SUBROUTINE "SHARE" THE MEMORY LOCATION OF
THE ACTUAL ARGUMENTS WITH WHICH THE SUBROUTINE IS CALLED; THAT IS,
CHANGING THE VALUE OF ONE OF THE PARAMETERS IN THE SUBROUTINE WILL
CHANGE THE VALUE OF THE VARIABLE IN THE CALLING PROGRAM WHICH
CORRESPONDS TO THAT ARGUMENT.
.INDEX ^C, ^C^O^M^P^A^T^I^B^I^L^I^T^Y ^W^I^T^H
^A SUBROUTINE IS A ^[C^] FUNCTION THAT TAKES POINTERS: THE
CALL ^F(^A1,^A2,...,^A^N) PASSES THE ADDRESS OF EACH ARGUMENT, AND AN ARGUMENT THAT
IS NOT A VARIABLE (A CONSTANT OR AN EXPRESSION) IS STORED IN A TEMPORARY
WHOSE ADDRESS IS PASSED.  ^THE SUBROUTINE REFERS TO ITS I-TH ARGUMENT THROUGH THE I-TH POINTER
AND RETURNS A 64-BIT INTEGER, SO THAT THE ^[C^] DECLARATION OF ^[SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B^]
IS ^[int64_t ADD2(int64_t *a, int64_t *b)^].
^A SUBPROGRAM CALLED WITH MORE ARGUMENTS THAN IT EXPECTS IS NOT HARMED.
^IF IT IS CALLED WITH FEWER, THE PARAMETERS THAT WERE NOT PASSED ARE UNDEFINED.
^FUNCTIONS RETURN THEIR VALUE AS THE 64-BIT RESULT; THE WORD OF A ^R^E^A^L IS THE BITS OF THE
DOUBLE.  ^THE NAME OF A SUBROUTINE IS THE SYMBOL OF THE FUNCTION IN THE OBJECT
FILE, AS WRITTEN (NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE).
^THE ROUTINES ARE NOT REENTRANT (^SECTION 2.1.4).
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^S^U^B^R ^A() ^I^S ^D
.BREAK
^DEFINE A SUBROUTINE (OR FUNCTION) NAMED ^A WITH NO FORMAL
ARGUMENTS, AND WITH VALUE ^D.
.INDEX ^R^E^T^U^R^N
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^R^E^T^U^R^N ^E
.BREAK
^RETURN FROM THE SUBROUTINE IN WHICH THIS STATEMENT APPEARS.
^THE VALUE RETURNED BY THE SUBROUTINE IS ^E.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A(^B,^C,^D)
.BREAK
^EXECUTE THE FUNCTION ^A WITH PARAMETERS ^B,^C,^D:
^THE VALUE OF THIS EXPRESSION IS THE VALUE OF THE
FUNCTION.  (^SINCE THERE IS NO DISTINCTION IN ^I^M^P BETWEEN
FUNCTIONS AND SUBROUTINES, THIS EXPRESSION IS ALSO USED
TO CALL ^A AS A SUBROUTINE WITH THE INDICATED PARAMETERS.)
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A()
.BREAK
^EXECUTE THE FUNCTION ^A WITH NO PARAMETERS.
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 2.2.6: ^BYTE ^ACCESS.
.CENTER
^&2.2.6: ^BYTE ^ACCESS.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^B^Y^T^E^S
.SKIP 2
.INDENT -15
^A<^B,^C>
.BREAK
^THIS OBJECT IS CALLED A "BYTE".  ^A IS A VARIABLE, EITHER
SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED; ^B AND ^C ARE ANY EXPRESSIONS.  ^A<^B,^C>
DESIGNATES THE PORTION OF THE WORD ^A CONSISTING OF ^B BITS, LOCATED ^C BITS
FROM THE RIGHT HAND SIDE OF THE WORD.  ^THUS, E.G., ^A<12,12> DESIGNATES THE 12 BITS
WHOSE LOWEST BIT IS BIT 12 OF ^A, AND ^A<1,63> IS THE SIGN BIT.  ^A BYTE MAY BE USED AS AN OPERAND, OR HAVE A VALUE STORED IN IT.
^EXAMPLE: THE PROGRAM FRAGMENT
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^A:=3;
(^A<9,^I+9>:=3+^A<9,^I>) ^F^O^R ^I ^I^N 0,9,18;
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
HAS THE EFFECT OF SETTING ^A TO 14011006003^B (OCTAL).
.SKIP
^THE CONSTRUCTS ^A<^R> AND ^A<^L> DESIGNATE THE RIGHT AND LEFT HALF WORDS
OF ^A.  ^A HALF WORD IS 32 BITS.  ^THEY ARE EQUIVALENT TO ^A<32,0> AND ^A<32,32> RESPECTIVELY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^B^Y^T^E^P ^A<^I,^J>
.BREAK
^THIS EXPRESSION HAS THE VALUE OF A BYTE POINTER TO THE DESIGNATED BYTE.  ^ITS USEFULNESS
LIES IN THE ABILITY TO WRITE ^X:=^B^Y^T^E^P ^A<^B,^C> AND DO BYTE ACCESS BY REFERENCING
^X, AS EXPLAINED IN THE NEXT PARAGRAPH.  ^IF ^I AND ^J ARE NOT CONSTANTS, THE BYTE POINTER WILL BE
COMPUTED WITH THEIR VALUES AT THE TIME THE STATEMENT IS EXECUTED AND WILL NOT BE
REEVALUATED IF ^I AND ^J CHANGE VALUE LATER.
.BREAK
^THERE ARE TWO KINDS OF POINTER.  ^B^Y^T^E^P ^A<7,36> AND ^B^Y^T^E^P ^A<8,64>, WITH CONSTANTS FOR THE
SIZE AND POSITION, MAKE A POINTER TO CHARACTERS: THE BYTE ADDRESS OF THE BYTE BEFORE THE FIRST
BYTE OF ^A, A BYTE BEING A CHARACTER.  ^ANY OTHER ^B^Y^T^E^P
MAKES THE POINTER TO A BIT FIELD: A WORD WITH THE SIZE OF THE FIELD IN BITS 58 TO 63, THE POSITION IN BITS 52 TO 57,
AND IN THE REMAINING BITS THE BYTE ADDRESS OF THE WORD THAT HOLDS THE FIELD.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
<^X>
.BREAK
^X MUST CONTAIN A BYTE POINTER.  (^THIS MAY BE ACCOMPLISHED BY ^X:=^B^Y^T^E^P ^A<^I,^J>.)
^THEN <^X> REFERS TO THE BYTE DESIGNATED BY THE BYTE POINTER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
<+^X>
.BREAK
^X MUST CONTAIN A BYTE POINTER.  ^WHEN <+^X> IS EVALUATED, IT INCREMENTS THE
BYTE POINTER, AND ITS VALUE IS THE BYTE THEN POINTED TO.  ^INCREMENTING A BYTE POINTER TO CHARACTERS MEANS THAT IT
POINTS TO THE NEXT BYTE OF MEMORY.  ^INCREMENTING A POINTER TO ^A<^I,^J> MEANS THAT THE POINTER WILL NOW
POINT TO ^A<^I,^J-^I> IF ^J ^G^E ^I, OR TO ^A[1]<^I,64-^I> IF ^J<^I.  ^EXAMPLE:  ^SUPPOSE THE MEMORY LOCATIONS STARTING AT
^S^T^G CONTAIN A STRING (A CHARACTER TO A BYTE, A ZERO BYTE AT THE END).  ^THEN THE FOLLOWING PROGRAM FRAGMENT UNPACKS
^S^T^G INTO THE LOCATIONS STARTING AT ^U^N^P, ONE CHARACTER PER WORD (REMEMBER, THE STRING
TERMINATES WITH A 0 CHARACTER):
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^S^T^G ^I^S 2 ^L^O^N^G; ^U^N^P ^I^S 16 ^L^O^N^G;
^S^T^G[0]:='^H^E^L^L^O';
^X:=^B^Y^T^E^P ^S^T^G<7,36>; ^I:=-1;
^L^O^O^P: (^U^N^P[^I:=^I+1]:=<+^X>)=>^G^O ^T^O ^L^O^O^P;
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
^AFTER IT ^U^N^P[0] TO ^U^N^P[5] HOLD 72, 69, 76, 76, 79 AND 0, THE CODES OF THE CHARACTERS.
.SKIP
^BYTE POINTERS MAY ALSO BE STORED INTO, WITH OR WITHOUT FIRST BEING INCREMENTED: <+^X>:=^I.
^ONLY THE LOW 8 BITS OF A CHARACTER ARE STORED.
^THE CHARACTERS OF A ^U^T^F-8 TEXT ARE ITS BYTES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^I^D^P^B(^A,^B)
.INDENT -15
^I^L^D^B(^A)
.BREAK
^I^D^P^B(^A,^B) IS THE SAME AS <+^B>:=^A, AND ^I^L^D^B(^A) IS THE SAME AS <+^A>: THEY STEP THE BYTE POINTER AND
STORE ^A IN THE BYTE, OR HAVE THE VALUE OF THE BYTE.
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 2.2.7: ^INPUT/^OUTPUT.
.CENTER
^&2.2.7: ^INPUT/^OUTPUT.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^I^N^P^U^T/^O^U^T^P^U^T
.INDEX ^F^O^R^M^A^T^T^E^D ^I/^O
.INDEX ^P^R^I^N^T
.INDEX ^R^E^A^D
.SKIP 2
.INDENT -15
^P^R^I^N^T ^A,^B,^C
.INDENT -15
^R^E^A^D ^A,^B,^C
.BREAK
^A, ^B, ^C, ETC., ARE A LIST OF EXPRESSIONS TO BE TRANSFERRED
FROM/TO INPUT/OUTPUT FILES, WITH FORMAT SPECIFIERS MIXED
IN.  ^ANY EXPRESSION MAY APPEAR IN A ^P^R^I^N^T LIST; IF AN
EXPRESSION OTHER THAN A SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE
APPEARS IN A ^R^E^A^D LIST THE EFFECT WILL BE TO READ INTO
A TEMPORARY LOCATION.  ^THE FORMAT SPECIFIERS ARE LISTED
BELOW.  ^THE ORDER IN WHICH THE ITEMS OF A LIST ARE EVALUATED IS NOT SPECIFIED;
WRITE ^[PRINT <+P>,<+P>^] AS TWO STATEMENTS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE VALUE OF A ^P^R^I^N^T STATEMENT IS THE NUMBER OF THE
LAST COLUMN ON A LINE THAT WAS PRINTED INTO.  ^IN PARTICULAR, THE VALUE OF ^P^R^I^N^T / IS 0.  ^THE VALUE OF A ^R^E^A^D
STATEMENT IS -1 IF AN ATTEMPT HAS BEEN MADE TO READ PAST
THE END OF THE FILE, 1 IF AN ATTEMPT HAS BEEN MADE TO READ
PAST THE END OF A LINE, AND 0 OTHERWISE.
.PARAGRAPH
.INDEX ^O^U^T^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E, ^D^E^F^A^U^L^T
.INDEX ^I^N^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E, ^D^E^F^A^U^L^T
^IF NO FILE IS EXPLICITLY SPECIFIED, OUTPUT IS TO
FILE ^P^O, THE STANDARD OUTPUT, AND INPUT IS FROM FILE ^P^I, THE STANDARD INPUT.
^THERE IS NO RULE AGAINST USING MANY STATEMENTS
TO PRINT OR READ ONE LINE; THE ONLY THING THAT TERMINATES
A LINE IS A /.  ^A FORMAT, ONCE SPECIFIED, REMAINS IN
EFFECT UNTIL ANOTHER SPECIFICATION IS ENCOUNTERED, EVEN
THROUGH SEVERAL DIFFERENT ^P^R^I^N^T OR ^R^E^A^D STATEMENTS.
^CHANGING THE ^P^R^I^N^T FORMAT OR FILE DOES NOT AFFECT THE ^R^E^A^D
FORMAT OR FILE, AND VICE VERSA.  ^A LINE IS HELD IN A BUFFER OF 1023
CHARACTERS; A LONGER LINE IS CUT.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN PRINTING, IF A DATA ITEM IS PRESENTED THAT
IS TOO LARGE FOR THE SPECIFIED FIELD WIDTH, A FIELD JUST
LARGE ENOUGH TO CONTAIN IT IS USED.  ^CONSEQUENTLY, IF A
FIELD WIDTH OF 0 IS SPECIFIED, A FIELD JUST LARGE ENOUGH
TO CONTAIN EACH DATA ITEM WILL BE USED.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN READING, IF THE FIELD WIDTH SPECIFICATION IS A SINGLE-CHARACTER
STRING (E.G., ','), THE INPUT FIELD WILL TERMINATE ON
THE FIRST OF A) THE SPECIFIED CHARACTER; B) THE END OF THE LINE;
C) 128 INPUT CHARACTERS.  ^A FIELD WIDTH SPECIFICATION OF 0 IS EQUIVALENT
TO ','.  ^A FIELD WIDTH THAT IS A NUMBER FROM 1 TO 31 IS A NUMBER OF COLUMNS.
(^A NUMBER FROM 32 TO 126 IS THE CODE OF THE CHARACTER THAT
ENDS THE FIELD, AS A ONE-CHARACTER STRING IS, SO THAT A FIELD OF 32 OR MORE
COLUMNS CANNOT BE ASKED FOR BY ITS NUMBER.)  ^BLANKS ARE IGNORED ON INPUT (EXCEPT IN ^S^T^G CONVERSION
AND WHEN ' ' IS THE FIELD TERMINATOR).  ^TABS ARE CONVERTED TO A
SINGLE BLANK ON INPUT.  ^ALL FIELDS ARE LIMITED TO 128 CHARACTERS ON
INPUT, SO THAT A VARIABLE THAT RECEIVES A ^S^T^G FIELD MUST BE AN ARRAY OF AT LEAST 17 WORDS.
.PARAGRAPH
^FIELD WIDTHS IN FORMAT SPECIFIERS ARE USUALLY
CONSTANTS BUT MAY BE ANY EXPRESSION.
.PARAGRAPH
^IT IS NECESSARY TO CLOSE OUT YOUR OUTPUT FILES EXPLICITLY IN YOUR PROGRAM SOME TIME BEFORE IT TERMINATES.
.INDEX ^F^I^N^I
.INDEX ^O^U^T^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E^S, ^T^E^R^M^I^N^A^T^I^N^G
.INDEX ^T^E^R^M^I^N^A^T^I^N^G ^O^U^T^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E^S
^THIS IS DONE BY THE SUBROUTINE CALL ^F^I^N^I(^I), FOR ^I=0 OR
-1.  ^THIS CLOSES ALL YOUR FILES, AND THE OUTPUT AND THE INPUT
RETURN TO THE STANDARD OUTPUT AND THE STANDARD INPUT.  ^IF ^I=0, ^F^I^N^I EXITS FROM THE PROGRAM;
IF ^I=-1, ^F^I^N^I RETURNS NORMALLY.  ^A PROGRAM
THAT ENDS WITHOUT ^F^I^N^I STILL HAS ITS COMPLETE LINES WRITTEN, SINCE THE FILES ARE CLOSED WHEN THE
PROGRAM EXITS, BUT A LINE THAT WAS NOT ENDED BY / IS LOST.
.PARAGRAPH
^AT MOST FOUR FILES MAY BE REFERRED TO IN ^I^M^P ^I/^O.  ^IF
IT IS DESIRED TO REFER TO MORE, ONE OF THE PREVIOUS FILES
MUST BE EXPLICITLY CLOSED USING ^F^I^N^I (OR CLOSE ALL OF THEM
WITH ^F^I^N^I(-1)).  ^THE STANDARD INPUT AND THE STANDARD OUTPUT ARE NOT COUNTED.  ^A FILE THAT CANNOT BE OPENED,
AND A FIFTH FILE, STOP THE PROGRAM WITH A MESSAGE ON THE STANDARD ERROR AND THE
EXIT STATUS 1.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^FORMAT ^SPECIFIERS:
.BREAK
.INDEX ^F^O^R^M^A^T ^S^P^E^C^I^F^I^C^A^T^I^O^N^S (^I/^O)
.INDENT -12
^I^G^R ^N
.BREAK
^CONVERSION IS IN BASE 10, IN A FIELD ^N POSITIONS WIDE.
^ON OUTPUT, LEADING ZEROS ARE SUPPRESSED, AND A LEADING
MINUS SIGN IS PRINTED FOR NEGATIVE NUMBERS.  ^ON INPUT, THE FIELD IS A DECIMAL NUMBER
WITH AN OPTIONAL SIGN.
.INDENT -12
^O^C^T ^N
.BREAK
^CONVERSION IS IN BASE 8, UNSIGNED (I.E., NEGATIVE NUMBERS
PRINT WITH LEADING 7'S: -1 IS 1777777777777777777777), IN A FIELD ^N POSITIONS WIDE.  ^ON
OUTPUT, LEADING ZEROS ARE PRINTED.  ^IF ^N IS GREATER THAN
12 AND THE NUMBER IS LESS THAN ^[8**12^], EXACTLY THE LEFTMOST ^N-12 POSITIONS WILL BE BLANK.
.INDENT -12
^S^T^G ^N
.BREAK
^CONVERSION IS IN BYTES, IN A FIELD ^N WIDE.  ^ON OUTPUT,
IF ^N IS 0 CHARACTERS ARE PRINTED UNTIL A 0 BYTE IS
ENCOUNTERED.  ^OTHERWISE, CHARACTERS ARE PRINTED UNTIL
A 0 BYTE IS ENCOUNTERED OR UNTIL ^N CHARACTERS HAVE BEEN
PRINTED, AND A SHORTER STRING IS PRECEDED BY BLANKS TO FILL THE FIELD.  ^ON INPUT, THE FIELD IS STORED AS BYTES
ENDED BY A ZERO BYTE.
.INDENT -12
^F^L^T ^M.^N
.BREAK
^PRINTS A FLOATING POINT NUMBER (A ^R^E^A^L) IN A FIELD ^M COLUMNS WIDE, WITH ^N DIGITS TO THE RIGHT OF
THE DECIMAL POINT.  ^IF ^N IS 0, NO DECIMAL POINT IS PRINTED.  ^IF THE NUMBER
WILL NOT FIT IN THE FIELD AS SPECIFIED, SCIENTIFIC NOTATION IS USED.  ^IF ^M IS NEGATIVE,
SCIENTIFIC NOTATION IS ALWAYS USED IN A FIELD -^M COLUMNS WIDE.  (^I^M^P USES " TO MEAN 'TIMES 10 TO THE'.)
^EXCEPTION: IF ^M IS 0, A FIELD JUST WIDE ENOUGH TO CONTAIN THE NUMBER IS USED.  ^THERE IS NO 0
BEFORE THE DECIMAL POINT: 0.5 PRINTS AS .500 FOR ^N=3.  ^THE DIGITS ARE THOSE OF THE ^I^E^E^E DOUBLE,
CORRECTLY ROUNDED.  ^THE VALUE PRINTED IS A ^R^E^A^L: AN INTEGER GIVEN TO ^F^L^T IS READ AS THE
BITS OF A DOUBLE, SO WRITE ^F^L^T(^I) FOR AN INTEGER ^I.  ^ON INPUT, ^F^L^T ^M.^N IS EQUIVALENT TO ^I^G^R ^M.
.INDENT -12
/
.BREAK
^NEW LINE.  ^ON OUTPUT, CAUSES TERMINATION OF THE CURRENT LINE.
^IF A LINE IS PRINTED BUT NOT TERMINATED WITH A /, THEN THE LINE
WILL NOT PRINT.  ^SUCCESSIVE /'S PRODUCE BLANK LINES.  ^ON INPUT, A / CAUSES
THE REMAINDER OF THE CURRENT LINE TO BE SKIPPED OVER.
.INDENT -12
^F^I^L^E ^A
.INDENT -12
^F^I^L^E ^A.^B
.BREAK
^SPECIFIES THE FILE FOR INPUT/OUTPUT.  ^A AND ^B ARE FILE NAME AND EXTENSION, AND MAY BE EITHER
STRING CONSTANTS OR UNSUBSCRIPTED VARIABLES HOLDING A STRING.  ^THE FILE IS THE ^UNIX
FILE ^[A.B^] OF THE CURRENT DIRECTORY, OR ^[A^] WHEN ^B IS ABSENT OR 0; ^A AND ^B ARE CUT TO SEVEN
CHARACTERS EACH.  ^A ^P^R^I^N^T OPENS THE FILE FOR WRITING,
AND REPLACES ITS CONTENTS; A ^R^E^A^D OPENS IT FOR READING.  ^THE FILE STAYS THE ONE FOR THE OUTPUT (OR THE
INPUT) UNTIL ANOTHER ^F^I^L^E, A ^D^E^V^I^C^E '^T^T^Y:' OR A ^F^I^N^I.  ^CAUTION: ^F^I^L^E ^F^O^R03.^D^A^T, E.G., REFERS TO VARIABLES ^F^O^R03 AND ^D^A^T.  ^HE WHO WROTE THIS PROBABLY
MEANT TO WRITE ^F^I^L^E '^F^O^R03'.'^D^A^T'.
.SKIP
^EXAMPLES: AFTER ^[A:=101B^], THE STATEMENT ^P^R^I^N^T ^S^T^G 1,^A,^I^G^R 4,^A,^O^C^T 6,^A,^A,/ PRODUCES THE LINE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
"^A  65000101000101"
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
^[(PRINT IGR 3,I)FOR I TO 2;PRINT /^] PRODUCES THE LINE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
"  0  1  2"
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
^P^R^I^N^T ^S^T^G 0, '^THIS IS A ^PAGE ^HEADING',/ PRODUCES THE LINE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
"^THIS IS A ^PAGE ^HEADING".
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
^THE PROGRAM
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^S ^I^S 8 ^L^O^N^G;
^P^R^I^N^T ^F^I^L^E 'OUT'.'TXT',^I^G^R 4,17,^S^T^G 0,' APPLES',/;
^F^I^N^I(-1);
^R^E^A^D ^F^I^L^E 'OUT'.'TXT',^I^G^R 4,^N,^S^T^G 0,^S;
^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,^N+1,^S^T^G 0,^S,/;
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
WRITES THE FILE ^[out.txt^] AND PRINTS
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
18 APPLES
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.SKIP
.INDENT -12
^D^E^V^I^C^E ^D
.BREAK
^D MAY BE EITHER A STRING CONSTANT OR A VARIABLE CONTAINING A STRING VALUE.
^D^E^V^I^C^E '^T^T^Y:' MAKES THE
STANDARD OUTPUT (FOR ^P^R^I^N^T) OR THE STANDARD INPUT (FOR ^R^E^A^D) THE FILE, WITH NO SUBSEQUENT FILE SPECIFICATION.
^THE NEXT FILE SPECIFICATION WILL CAUSE THE INPUT OR OUTPUT TO REVERT TO THAT FILE.
.INDENT -12
^I^M^A^G^E ^M^O^D^E
.BREAK
^THIS FORMAT SPECIFICATION CAUSES SUBSEQUENT DATA TRANSFER TO BE IN UNFORMATTED
MODE, TRANSFERRING 8-BYTE WORDS, IN THE BYTE ORDER OF THE MACHINE, BETWEEN THE FILE AND THE VARIABLES IN
THE ^I/^O STATEMENT LIST.  ^A / IN IMAGE MODE ENDS NOTHING.  ^MIXING ^I^M^A^G^E ^M^O^D^E AND OTHER FORMAT SPECIFICATIONS IN READING OR
WRITING THE SAME FILE MAY PRODUCE ANOMALOUS RESULTS.
.INDENT -12
^T^A^B ^N
.BREAK
^ON ^P^R^I^N^T ONLY, CAUSES THE NEXT CHARACTER OF OUTPUT TO APPEAR IN
COLUMN ^N (NUMBERING STARTING WITH 1).  ^IF PRINTING HAS ALREADY GONE
PAST COLUMN ^N, NO ACTION IS TAKEN.
.INDENT -12
^F^I^L^L 'C'
.BREAK
^ON OUTPUT ONLY, CAUSES LEADING POSITIONS IN ALL FIELDS TO BE PRINTED AS THE SPECIFIED CHARACTER
INSTEAD OF BEING LEFT BLANK.  ^THUS, FOR EXAMPLE, ^F^I^L^L '0' CAUSES LEADING ZEROS TO BE PRINTED.
^F^I^L^L ' ' (OR ^F^I^L^L 0) RESTORES BLANKS.
^THIS SPECIFICATION, UNLIKE THE OTHERS, AFFECTS ALL OUTPUT FILES, NOT JUST THE CURRENT ONE.
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 2.2.8: ^DECLARATIONS; ^D^A^T^A AND ^R^E^M^O^T^E STATEMENTS.
.CENTER
^&2.2.8: ^DECLARATIONS; ^D^A^T^A AND ^R^E^M^O^T^E STATEMENTS.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^D^E^C^L^A^R^A^T^I^O^N^S
.SKIP 2
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.INDEX ^L^E^T ^S^T^A^T^E^M^E^N^T
.INDENT -15
^L^E^T ^I=2^R,^A=3,^B^F=^B[1]
.BREAK
^THE ^L^E^T STATEMENT PROVIDES A CONVENIENT WAY OF DECLARING
SYNONYMS.  ^IT CONSISTS OF THE WORD ^L^E^T, FOLLOWED BY A LIST
OF SYNONYM DECLARATIONS OF THE FORM ^N=^V, WHERE ^N IS A NAME AND
^V IS A CONSTANT, OR A SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE.  ^THE EFFECT
IS THAT ALL SUBSEQUENT APPEARANCES OF ^N IN THE PROGRAM WILL BE
INTERPRETED AS IF ^V HAD BEEN WRITTEN INSTEAD.  ^L^E^T ^A=^B IS IN FACT JUST
"SYNTACTIC SUGARING" FOR THE SYNTAX STATEMENT ^[<ATOM> ::= A ::="B"^].
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^A,^B ^A^R^E 3 ^L^O^N^G,^C^O^M^M^O^N
.INDENT -15
1^R ^I^S ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R
.INDENT -15
^F^O^O,3^R ^I^S ^R^E^L^E^A^S^E^D
.BREAK
^DECLARATIONS:  THE GENERAL FORM FOR A DECLARATION IS
A LIST OF NAMES, THEN A LIST OF CHARACTERISTICS
TO BE ASSOCIATED WITH THESE NAMES.
^THE CHARACTERISTICS AVAILABLE ARE:
.SKIP
.INDEX ^R^E^A^L
.INDENT -12
^R^E^A^L
.BREAK
^SPECIFIES THAT THE VARIABLES ARE REAL NUMBERS, AND ALL
ARITHMETIC PERFORMED ON THEM IS TO BE FLOATING POINT.
^TYPE CONVERSION BETWEEN REAL AND INTEGER IS ALWAYS
DONE IMPLICITLY.  ^A REAL IS AN ^I^E^E^E 754 DOUBLE, HELD IN THE 64-BIT WORD.
.SKIP
.INDENT -12
^I^N^T^E^G^E^R
.BREAK
^SPECIFIES THAT THE VARIABLES ARE INTEGERS, WHICH THEY ARE UNLESS DECLARED ^R^E^A^L.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^V^E^C^T^O^R ^D^E^C^L^A^R^A^T^I^O^N^S
.INDEX ^L^O^N^G
.INDENT -12
^N ^L^O^N^G
.BREAK
^WHERE ^N IS A CONSTANT OR CONSTANT EXPRESSION.  ^THE VARIABLES ARE DECLARED TO BE ARRAYS, AND ^N WORDS ARE RESERVED
FOR EACH OF THEM.  (^THE ^N WORDS MAY BE REFERRED TO AS ^A (OR ^A[0]), ^A[1], ... ^A[^N-1].)
.SKIP
.INDEX ^C^O^M^M^O^N
.INDENT -12
^C^O^M^M^O^N
.BREAK
^VARIABLES ARE MADE COMMON: EACH IS A GLOBAL SYMBOL OF THE OBJECT FILE, SPELLED AS THE NAME, WHICH THE
FILES OF A PROGRAM AND ^[C^] SHARE.  ^EVERY FILE THAT USES A ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLE DECLARES IT, AND
THE DECLARATIONS NAME ONE VARIABLE.  ^THE SIZE OF A ^C^O^M^M^O^N ARRAY IS GIVEN BY ^N ^L^O^N^G IN THE DECLARATION.
^A VARIABLE THAT IS NOT ^C^O^M^M^O^N BELONGS TO ITS FILE (^SECTION 2.1.4).  ^A ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLE
THAT NOTHING IN A FILE USES IS NOT MADE BY THAT FILE.  ^C^O^M^M^O^N IS HOW AN ^I^M^P PROGRAM AND A ^[C^] PROGRAM
SHARE DATA (^SECTION 2.4.3).
.SKIP
.INDEX ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L
.INDENT -12
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L
.BREAK
^VARIABLES ARE DEFINED ELSEWHERE, IN THE ^[C^] LIBRARY FOR EXAMPLE: ^X ^I^S ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L
MAKES ^X THE VARIABLE OF THAT NAME WHICH THE LINKER FINDS, AND NOTHING IS DEFINED IN THE
FILE.  ^L^O^C(^X) IS ITS ADDRESS (^SECTION 2.4.3).
.SKIP
.INDENT -12
^L^O^C^A^L
.BREAK
^MAKES VARIABLES LOCAL TO THE CURRENT SUBROUTINE FROM THIS POINT TO THE END
OF THE SUBROUTINE, BUT DOES NOT AFFECT THE MEANING OF THE IDENTIFIER OUTSIDE
THE CURRENT SUBROUTINE.  ^A ^L^O^C^A^L VARIABLE KEEPS ITS VALUE FROM ONE CALL TO THE NEXT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -12
^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R
.BREAK
^THE DECLARATION ^A ^I^S ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R MAKES ^A A REGISTER: LIKE 0^R TO 15^R IT IS A VARIABLE LOCAL TO EACH ROUTINE, WHICH IS 0 WHEN THE ROUTINE IS
ENTERED AND HAS NO ADDRESS.  ^A REGISTER CANNOT BE DECLARED ^R^E^A^L.
^UNTIL THE DECLARATION ^A ^I^S ^R^E^L^E^A^S^E^D IS ENCOUNTERED, ALL REFERENCES TO ^A REFER TO THE REGISTER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -12
^R^E^L^E^A^S^E^D
.BREAK
^FOR IDENTIFIERS WHICH HAVE BEEN BOUND TO A REGISTER, THAT BINDING IS TERMINATED, AND SUBSEQUENT REFERENCES TO THAT
IDENTIFIER WILL REFER TO A MEMORY LOCATION (A VARIABLE OF THE FILE).
.SKIP
.INDEX ^R^E^M^O^T^E
.INDENT -15
^R^E^M^O^T^E ^S
.BREAK
^S IS ANY STATEMENT.  ^HAS THE EFFECT OF CAUSING THE CODE FOR STATEMENT ^S TO BE INSERTED
NOT AT THE POINT IN THE PROGRAM WHERE THE ^R^E^M^O^T^E STATEMENT APPEARS, BUT AT THE END OF
THE ROUTINE.  ^USEFUL IN QUOTED SEMANTICS WHERE
LOCAL VARIABLES ARE REQUIRED, E.G., ^L^O^C^A^L ^F^O^O ^I^N "... ^R^E^M^O^T^E ^F^O^O:0; ...".
.SKIP
.INDEX ^D^A^T^A
.INDENT -15
^D^A^T^A (^L)
.BREAK
^L IS A LIST OF CONSTANT EXPRESSIONS AND STRING CONSTANTS.  ^THE ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT PRODUCES DATA WORDS AT THAT POINT
IN THE PROGRAM CONTAINING THE VALUES OF THE CONSTANT EXPRESSIONS.  ^ONE WORD IS USED FOR EACH ITEM IN ^L, EXCEPT THAT
STRINGS ARE STORED IN AS MANY WORDS AS ARE REQUIRED.  ^D^A^T^A STATEMENTS MAY BE USED TO PRESET VARIABLES
TO A VALUE AT COMPILE TIME, VIZ.:  ^V^A^R: ^D^A^T^A (3).  ^A ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT MAY BE EXECUTED: IT DOES NOTHING.
^THE TAG OF A ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT NAMES ITS FIRST WORD, SO THAT AFTER ^T^B^L: ^D^A^T^A(10,20,30)
THE ELEMENTS ARE ^T^B^L[0], ^T^B^L[1] AND ^T^B^L[2].
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SUBTITLE 2.2.9:  ^MISCELLANEOUS ^CONSTRUCTS.
.CENTER
^&2.2.9:  ^MISCELLANEOUS ^CONSTRUCTS.\&
.SKIP
.SKIP 2
.INDEX ^E^X^E^C^U^T^E
.INDENT -15
^E^X^E^C^U^T^E ^E
.BREAK
^EXECUTES THE INSTRUCTION CONTAINED IN THE VARIABLE ^E.  ^THE INSTRUCTION IS A WORD OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE
THAT THE COMPILER GENERATES CODE FOR: THE OPERATION IN BITS 56 TO 63 (THE NUMBERS
ARE LISTED AT THE HEAD OF THE FILE ^[syntax64.imc^]: 1 ^L^O^A^D, 6 ^A^D^D, 7 ^S^U^B, ...), THE REGISTER
IN BITS 52 TO 55, AN INDEX REGISTER IN BITS 48 TO 51, BIT 47 SET FOR AN IMMEDIATE OPERAND AND THE ADDRESS OR NUMBER IN THE BITS 0 TO 46.
^THE REGISTERS ARE 0^R TO 15^R.  ^A REGISTER THAT THE INSTRUCTION NAMES MUST BE MENTIONED ELSEWHERE IN THE ROUTINE, AS 8^R
IS IN THE EXAMPLE, BECAUSE THE COMPILER KEEPS THE REGISTERS A ROUTINE MENTIONS.  ^THE PROGRAM
.SKIP
.NOFILL
8^R:=5;
^W:=(1 ^L^S 56) ^O^R (8 ^L^S 52) ^O^R (1 ^L^S 47) ^O^R 42;
^E^X^E^C^U^T^E ^W;
^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,8^R,/;
^W:=(6 ^L^S 56) ^O^R (8 ^L^S 52) ^O^R (1 ^L^S 47) ^O^R 8;
^E^X^E^C^U^T^E ^W;
^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,8^R,/;
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.SKIP
LOADS 42 INTO REGISTER 8, ADDS 8 TO IT, AND PRINTS 42 AND 50.  ^THE JUMPS AND THE FLOATING POINT OPERATIONS
CANNOT BE EXECUTED THIS WAY.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^C^A^L^L^I
.INDENT -15
^C^A^L^L^I(^C,^V)
.BREAK
^CALLS THE LIBRARY ROUTINE ^^IMPCALLI\\ WITH THE CODE ^C AND THE ARGUMENT ^V.  ^THE
CODE ^C IS A NUMBER OR AN EXPRESSION.  ^THE VALUE OF THE CONSTRUCT IS THE VALUE OF THE ROUTINE.
^THE CALLS ARE (OCTAL) 12, EXIT
(THE OUTPUT IS FLUSHED); 14, THE DATE, AS THE NUMBER ((YEAR-1964)*12+MONTH-1)*31+DAY-1; 23, THE TIME
OF DAY IN MILLISECONDS; 27, THE RUN TIME OF THE PROGRAM IN MILLISECONDS; AND 30, THE JOB NUMBER, WHICH
IS 1.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^X^W^D
.INDENT -15
^X^W^D ^A,^B
.BREAK
^USEFUL OCCASIONALLY IN SYSTEM CALLS.  ^HAS THE
VALUE OF THE LOW 32 BITS OF ^B ^O^RED WITH THE LOW 32 BITS OF ^A SHIFTED LEFT 32 BITS.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^I^O^W^D
.INDENT -15
^I^O^W^D ^A,^B
.BREAK
^HAS THE VALUE ^X^W^D -^A,^B-1.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^F^I^X(^A)
.BREAK
^HAS THE VALUE OF THE EXPRESSION ^A, CONVERTED TO TYPE INTEGER (BY TRUNCATION TOWARD ZERO) IF
IT WAS NOT ALREADY OF THAT TYPE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^F^L^T(^A)
.BREAK
^HAS THE VALUE OF THE EXPRESSION ^A, CONVERTED TO TYPE REAL IF IT WAS
NOT ALREADY OF THAT TYPE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -15
^M^O^V^E ^A ^T^H^R^O^U^G^H ^N ^T^O ^B
.BREAK
.INDEX ^B^L^O^C^K ^T^R^A^N^S^F^E^R^S
^COPIES THE ^N+1 WORDS THAT START AT ^A TO THE ^N+1 WORDS THAT START AT ^B, UPWARD, ONE WORD AT A TIME.  ^IT
PERFORMS THE OPERATION ^[(B[I]:=A[I]) FOR I TO N^].
^N IS ANY POSITIVE EXPRESSION (IF REAL, IT IS FIXED).
^A AND ^B ARE ANY EXPRESSIONS HAVING AN ADDRESS, SUCH AS
SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLES, OR [EXPRESSION].
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SUBTITLE 2.3:  ^SYNTACTIC AND ^SEMANTIC ^EXTENSION.
.PAGE
.INDEX SYNTAX EXTENSION
.INDEX EXTENSION, SYNTACTIC
.INDEX SEMANTICS
.CENTER
^&2.3:  ^SYNTACTIC AND ^SEMANTIC ^EXTENSION.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS SECTION EXPLAINS THE ^I^M^P72 FACILITIES FOR EXTENDING THE LANGUAGE.
^IT IS POSSIBLE TO ADD PRODUCTIONS TO THE SYNTAX FOR THE LANGUAGE, DEFINING THE
SEMANTICS FOR THE NEW CONSTRUCTS IN SEVERAL WAYS.  ^SEMANTICS MAY BE SPECIFIED
IN TERMS OF EXPRESSIONS WRITTEN IN THE PORTION OF THE LANGUAGE ALREADY DEFINED,
AS EXPLAINED IN ^SECTION 2.3.1.  ^ALTERNATIVELY, SEMANTICS MAY BE PERFORMED BY A
SERIES OF FUNCTION CALLS TO SEMANTIC SUBROUTINES CONTAINED WITHIN THE COMPILER,
AS DOCUMENTED IN ^SECTION 2.3.3.1.  ^THE USER MAY USE THE SUBROUTINES PROVIDED,
OR, IF HE REQUIRES OPERATIONS NOT AVAILABLE FROM THE CURRENT SET OF SEMANTIC
SUBROUTINES, HE MAY AS A LAST RESORT GO INTO THE COMPILER TO ADD NEW ONES.
^DIFFERENT SEMANTICS MAY BE SPECIFIED IN SPECIAL CASES, AS EXPLAINED IN ^SECTION
2.3.3.2, EITHER TO PERFORM DIFFERENT OPERATIONS UPON OBJECTS IN DIFFERENT
CONTEXTS, OR TO GENERATE BETTER OBJECT CODE.  ^WHEN THE SEMANTICS FOR SEVERAL
PRODUCTIONS ARE VERY SIMILAR, THEY MAY BE LUMPED TOGETHER UNDER A GENERAL
CASE, AS NOTED IN ^SECTION 2.3.3.3 AND 2.3.3.4.
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 2.3.1:  ^THE ^EASY ^WAY - ^SYNTACTIC "^MACROS"
.CENTER
^&2.3.1:  ^THE ^EASY ^WAY - ^SYNTACTIC "^MACROS"\&
.INDEX SYNTACTIC MACROS
.INDEX MACROS, SYNTACTIC
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS SECTION IS AN INTRODUCTION TO SYNTACTIC EXTENSION IN ^I^M^P72 FOR THE
CASUAL USER, AND IS INTENDED TO PROVIDE HIM WITH SUFFICIENT INFORMATION TO
UTILIZE THE BASIC FACILITY WITHOUT INFORMING TO THE POINT OF TOTAL CONFUSION.
^SOME OF THESE FEATURES ARE IN FACT LESS RESTRICTED THAN INDICATED IN THIS
SECTION.  ^THE FULL TRUTH EMERGES IN SUBSEQUENT SECTIONS.
.PARAGRAPH
^I^M^P72 CONTAINS A FACILITY FOR DEFINING SYNTACTIC MACROS, OR PATTERNS
WHICH THE COMPILER WILL RECOGNIZE IN A PROGRAM AND GENERATE SPECIFIC CODE
FOR.  ^AN EXAMPLE OF A DEFINITION FOR THE ABSOLUTE VALUE FUNCTION USING A
SYNTACTIC MACRO IS
.SKIP
.INDENT 10
<^E^X^P> ::= ^A^B^S ( <^A> ) ::= "^A<0=>-^A ^E^L^S^E ^A"
.SKIP
^THIS STATEMENT, WHEN INSERTED IN A PROGRAM, WILL CAUSE THE COMPILER TO
RECOGNIZE THE CONSTRUCT ^A^B^S, FOLLOWED BY A "(", FOLLOWED BY ANY EXPRESSION,
FOLLOWED BY A ")", AND TO SUBSTITUTE FOR IT THE CODE ENCLOSED IN " SIGNS,
INSERTING THE ACTUAL EXPRESSION FOR EACH INSTANCE OF ^A IN THE QUOTED EXPRESSION.
^THUS, WRITING
.SKIP
.CENTER
^X:=^A^B^S(^R+3)
.SKIP
LATER IN THE PROGRAM WOULD BE THE SAME AS WRITING
.SKIP
.CENTER
^X:=(^R+3<0=>-(^R+3) ^E^L^S^E ^R+3)
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
.INDEX SYNTAX STATEMENT
.INDEX SYNTAX PART
.INDEX SEMANTIC PART
^A SYNTAX STATEMENT FITS THE FOLLOWING PATTERN:
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^E^X^P> ::= SYNTAX PART ::= SEMANTIC PART
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SYNTAX PART IS THE PATTERN WHICH THE COMPILER IS TO RECOGNIZE.  ^IT
CONSISTS OF THE NAMES AND SPECIAL SYMBOLS IN THE PATTERN, AND, FOR EACH EXPRESSION IN THE PATTERN, AN IDENTIFIER IN ANGLE BRACKETS: E.G., <^F^O^O>.  ^SINGLE
CHARACTERS IN QUOTES (E.G., '_#') ARE INTERPRETED AS THAT CHARACTER, WITHOUT THE
QUOTES, AND THE CHARACTERS : ; " < AND % MUST BE QUOTED IF THEY APPEAR IN THE
SYNTAX PART.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SEMANTIC PART CONSISTS OF AN ^I^M^P EXPRESSION IN DOUBLE QUOTES, PERHAPS
.INDEX LOCAL VARIABLES
.INDEX ^L^O^C^A^L
PRECEDED BY A LIST OF LOCAL VARIABLES.  ^IT DEFINES THE CODE ^I^M^P IS TO GENERATE
WHEN IT RECOGNIZES AN INSTANCE OF THE SYNTAX PART, WITH THE ACTUAL EXPRESSIONS
IN THE INSTANCE TO BE INSERTED IN THE QUOTED EXPRESSION IN PLACE OF THE
IDENTIFIERS WHICH APPEARED IN ANGLE BRACKETS IN THE SYNTAX PART.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE QUOTED EXPRESSION MAY BE PRECEDED WITH A LIST OF LOCAL VARIABLES.
^FOR EXAMPLE, NOTICE THAT THE DEFINITION OF ^A^B^S ABOVE COMPUTES ^A TWICE, WHICH
MAY BE INEFFICIENT IF ^A IS AN EXPRESSION.  ^A MORE EFFICIENT WAY OF DOING IT
IS
.SKIP
.INDENT 5
<^E^X^P> ::= ^A^B^S ( <^A> ) ::= ^L^O^C^A^L ^R ^I^N "^R ^I^S ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R;
.INDENT 25
(^R:=^A)<0=>-^R ^E^L^S^E ^R"
.SKIP
^THIS DEFINITION COMPUTES ^A ONLY ONCE, STORING IT TEMPORARILY IN THE REGISTER ^R.
.PARAGRAPH
^ANOTHER EXAMPLE IS TAKEN FROM THE SYNTAX BUILT INTO THE COMPILER:
.SKIP
<^E^X^P> ::= <^A> ^F^O^R <^B> ^F^R^O^M <^C> ::= ^L^O^C^A^L ^F^O^R ^I^N "^B:=^C;
.INDENT 30
^F^O^R: ^A;
.INDENT 30
(^B:=^B-1) ^G^E 0=>^G^O ^T^O ^F^O^R";
.SKIP
^THE LIST OF LOCAL VARIABLES MAY CONSIST OF UP TO TEN NAMES, SEPARATED BY
COMMAS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS CONCLUDES THE INTRODUCTION TO SYNTACTIC MACROS.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.2: ^SPECIFYING ^SYNTAX.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.3.2: ^SPECIFYING ^SYNTAX.\&
.PARAGRAPH
.INDEX SYNTAX STATEMENT
^THE FORM OF THE SYNTAX SPECIFICATION STATEMENT IS A MODIFICATION OF THE FORM
OF A ^B^N^F PRODUCTION (^NAUR 1963), WITH ALTERNATIVE RIGHT-HAND SIDES NOT ALLOWED,
AND A SEMANTICS DEFINITION ADDED AT THE RIGHT.  ^THE GENERAL FORM IS:
.SKIP
.CENTER
<CLASS> ::= SYNTAX-PART ::= SEMANTIC-PART
.PARAGRAPH
^WE WILL USE THE TERM "ARGUMENTS" OF A PRODUCTION OR SYNTAX
STATEMENT TO REFER TO THE NON-TERMINALS APPEARING IN THE
SYNTAX-PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT (I.E., ON THE RIGHT-HAND
SIDE OF THE PRODUCTION).
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER INTERPRETS A PROGRAM BY RECOGNIZING INSTANCES OF CERTAIN
SYNTACTIC CLASSES, SUCH AS <^E^X^P> (EXPRESSION), <^V^B^L> (VARIABLE), AND SO ON.  ^THE
FUNCTION OF THE SYNTAX PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT IS TO TELL THE COMPILER ABOUT
A NEW CONSTRUCT THAT IT MUST RECOGNIZE AS AN INSTANCE OF A CERTAIN CLASS,
SPECIFIED BY THE IDENTIFIER IN <CLASS>.  ^THE CLASSES OF INTEREST IN THE ^I^M^P
.INDEX SYNTACTIC CLASSES
LANGUAGE ARE:
.SKIP
.INDEX ^N^A^M^E
.INDEX ^I^O^N
.INDEX ^V^B^L
.INDEX ^A^T^O^M
.INDEX ^B^Y^T^E
.INDEX ^E^X^P
.INDEX ^S^T
.INDEX ^S^T^L
.LEFT MARGIN 15
.INDENT -10
<^N^A^M>:  ^ANY VARIABLE NAME OR CONSTANT.  ^SHOULD GENERALLY BE AVOIDED IN
FAVOR OF <^V^B^L> UNLESS THE USER IS SURE OF WHAT HE IS DOING.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^V^B^L>:   ^ANY CONSTANT OR SIMPLE OR SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^I^O^N>:   ^ANY <^V^B^L>, FUNCTION CALL, OR AN <^S^T^L> ENCLOSED IN PARENTHESES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^A^T^O^M>:  ^AN <^I^O^N> OR BYTE OF AN <^I^O^N>.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^B^Y^T^E>:  ^ANY BYTE OR BYTE POINTER REFERENCE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^E^X^P>:   ^CLASS OF MOST EXPRESSIONS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^S^T>:    ^INCLUDES <^E^X^P>'S, CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS, DECLARATIONS, ETC.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
<^S^T^L>:   ^LIST OF ONE OR MORE <^S^T>S, SEPARATED BY ;'S.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FULL SET OF SYNTACTIC CLASSES IN ^I^M^P MAY BE DETERMINED BY READING THE
SYNTAX OF THE LANGUAGE (SEE ^APPENDIX ^I^I).  ^THE USER MAY
DEFINE HIS OWN SYNTACTIC CLASSES AS THE HUMOR FALLS UPON HIM.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SYNTACTIC PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT MAY CONTAIN:
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -3
1. ^IDENTIFIERS AND SPECIAL CHARACTERS, REPRESENTING THEMSELVES.
^THE CHARACTERS %, ", :, < AND ; MUST BE ENCLOSED IN SINGLE QUOTES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^SINGLE CHARACTERS ENCLOSED IN SINGLE QUOTES.  ^THEY ARE INTERPRETED
AS THE CHARACTER ALONE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^SYNTACTIC CLASSES, REPRESENTED BY <^C^L^A^S^S,NAME>, WHERE ^C^L^A^S^S IS THE
NAME OF THE CLASS AND NAME IS AN IDENTIFIER BY WHICH TO REFER TO THIS
ARGUMENT OF THE PRODUCTION IN THE SEMANTICS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4. <NAME>.  ^THIS IS INTERPRETED AS <^E^X^P,NAME> UNLESS IT IS THE VERY FIRST
THING AFTER THE ::=, IN WHICH CASE IT IS INTERPRETED AS <^A^T^O^M,NAME>.
^THIS HAS THE EFFECT OF MAKING OPERATIONS DEFINED USING <NAME> BE
RIGHT-ASSOCIATIVE, IN KEEPING WITH THE ^I^M^P CONVENTION.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^EXAMPLE: ^IN SECTION 2.3.1, AN EXPURGATED VERSION OF THE SYNTAX OF ^F^R^O^M LOOPS
WAS GIVEN.  ^THE ADULT VERSION ILLUSTRATES THE USE OF SYNTACTIC CLASSES:
.SKIP
<^S^T> ::= <^E^X^P,^A> ^F^O^R <^V^B^L,^B> ^F^R^O^M <^E^X^P,^C> ::=
.INDENT 17
^L^O^C^A^L ^F^O^R ^I^N "^B:=^C;
.INDENT 30
^F^O^R: ^A;
.INDENT 30
(^B:=^B-1) ^G^E 0=>^G^O ^T^O ^F^O^R"
.PARAGRAPH
^THE INTERPRETATION OF THE SYNTACTIC PART OF THIS STATEMENT IS THAT THE COMPILER
IS HENCEFORTH TO RECOGNIZE AS AN <^S^T> THE CONSTRUCT CONSISTING OF ANY <^E^X^P>,
FOLLOWED BY THE WORD ^F^O^R, FOLLOWED BY ANY <^V^B^L>, FOLLOWED BY THE WORD ^F^R^O^M,
FOLLOWED BY ANY <^E^X^P>.
.PARAGRAPH
.INDEX RECURSIVE SYNTAX DEFINITION
.INDEX SYNTAX DEFINITION, RECURSIVE
^EXAMPLE: ^THIS EXAMPLE ILLUSTRATES THE USE OF USER-DEFINED SYNTACTIC CLASSES
AND RECURSIVE DEFINITIONS.  ^THE CONSTRUCT TO BE DEFINED IS ^G^O ^T^O (^L0,^L1,..,^L^N) ^I
WHICH TRANSFERS CONTROL TO ^LI WHERE I IS THE VALUE OF ^I.  (^IF ^I<0 OR ^I>^N, THE
EFFECT IS UNDEFINED.)
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 5
<^S^T> ::= ^G^O ^T^O (<^G^O^L^I^S^T,^A>) <^B>  ::=
           ^L^O^C^A^L ^G^O ^I^N "^G^O ^T^O ^G^O[^B];^G^O: ^A";
<^G^O^L^I^S^T> ::= <^N^A^M,^A>             ::= "^G^O ^T^O ^A";
<^G^O^L^I^S^T> ::= <^G^O^L^I^S^T,^A>,<^N^A^M,^B>  ::= "^A; ^G^O ^T^O ^B"
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^THEN THE EXPRESSION
.SKIP
^G^O ^T^O (^A,^B,^C) ^J-3
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
PRODUCES
.SKIP
^G^O ^T^O %^G^O1[^J-3];
%^G^O1: ^G^O ^T^O ^A;
     ^G^O ^T^O ^B;
     ^G^O ^T^O ^C
.SKIP
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
(%^G^O1 IS A UNIQUE NAME GENERATED BY THE COMPILER FOR THE LOCAL VARIABLE ^G^O.)
^THIS IS THE DEFINITION OF THE CONSTRUCT IN THE SYNTAX FILE OF THE COMPILER.
^THE TOKEN ^[:=^] MUST BE WRITTEN IN SINGLE QUOTES IN THE SYNTAX PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT, AS ':=', LIKE
THE OTHER CHARACTERS THAT THE NOTATION USES (THE TOKEN ^[::=^] THAT SEPARATES THE PARTS NEEDS NONE).
.PARAGRAPH
^IT IS POSSIBLE TO WRITE A SYNTAX STATEMENT WITH NO SEMANTIC PART:
.SKIP
.CENTER
<CLASS> ::= SYNTAX-PART
.SKIP
^THE SEMANTICS IMPLIED BY THIS SYNTAX STATEMENT IS TO DISCARD ALL ARGUMENTS OF
THE PRODUCTION EXCEPT THE FIRST.  ^IF THERE ARE NO ARGUMENTS OR ONE ARGUMENT,
NO INFORMATION IS LOST.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3: ^SPECIFYING ^SEMANTICS.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.3.3: ^SPECIFYING ^SEMANTICS.\&
.SKIP 2
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.1: ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES.
.INDEX SEMANTICS
.INDEX SEMANTIC ROUTINES, ARGUMENTS OF
.INDEX SEMANTIC ROUTINES
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.1: ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^IN ^SECTION 2.3.1 WE SAW THAT THE SEMANTIC PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT COULD
CONSIST OF QUOTED SEMANTICS - I.E., AN ^I^M^P EXPRESSION ENCLOSED BY DOUBLE QUOTES.
^THERE IS ONE ALTERNATE FORMAT FOR SEMANTICS: A FUNCTIONAL EXPRESSION CONSISTING
OF CALLS TO SEMANTIC SUBROUTINES.
.PARAGRAPH
^SEMANTIC SUBROUTINES ARE NORMAL COMPILER SUBROUTINES WHICH ARE ALSO AVAILABLE TO THE SYNTAX WRITER IN A LIMITED SENSE.  ^THEY ARE CALLED WITH ARGUMENTS
OF THE FOLLOWING TYPES:
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1. ^CONSTANTS.  ^USUALLY SMALL POSITIVE NUMBERS.  ^THESE CAN SPECIFY
OPCODES, SWITCHES, OR WHATEVER, DEPENDING ON THE ROUTINE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^NEGATIVE DIRECTORY INDICES.  ^IDENTIFIERS ARE, BY CONVENTION, PASSED
TO SEMANTIC ROUTINES AS THE NEGATIVE OF THEIR INDEX IN THE
DIRECTORY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^REGISTERS.  ^THESE ARE IN THE FORM OF INDICES IN A TABLE OF SYMBOLIC
REGISTERS.  ^ASSIGNMENTS TO ACTUAL MACHINE REGISTERS ARE MADE DURING
THE ASSEMBLY PHASE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4. ^OBJECTS.
.INDEX OBJECTS (SEMANTIC ROUTINE ARGUMENTS)
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
5. ^TWO ARGUMENTS CONNECTED BY A + SIGN.  ^THE VALUE IS THE SUM OF
THE ARGUMENTS.  A SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY DIAGNOSTIC WILL RESULT FROM
AN ARGUMENT OF THE FORM ARG+ARG+ARG; THE DIAGNOSTIC MAY BE IGNORED.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^OBJECTS ARE POINTERS INTO A STACK WHICH HOLDS THE EXPRESSIONS
BEING PROCESSED BY THE SEMANTICS OF THE CURRENT PRODUCTION.  ^AN
OBJECT CORRESPONDS TO SOME <CLASS,NAME> IN THE PRODUCTION AN INSTANCE
OF WHICH IS BEING COMPILED.  ^OBJECTS EITHER ARE IDENTIFIERS FROM THE
PROGRAM BEING COMPILED, OR HAVE BEEN CONSTRUCTED BY SEMANTICS OPERATING ON AND/OR COMBINING OTHER OBJECTS.
.PARAGRAPH
^OBJECTS COME IN FIVE FLAVORS: ^NAME, ^REGISTER, ^CONSTANT, ^VARIABLE AND ^MEMORY:
^ALL BUT ^NAME WILL HAVE AN ARITHMETIC TYPE (REAL OR INTEGER), AND MAY HAVE
ASSOCIATED WITH THEM SOME OBJECT CODE WHICH COMPUTES THE EXPRESSION OF WHICH
THE OBJECT IS THE VALUE.
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^NAME - ^HAS NO PROPERTIES OTHER THAN A NAME.  ^IS TURNED INTO ANOTHER TYPE
OF OBJECT BY SEMANTIC ROUTINE ^N^A^M^E (Q.V.).  ^NAMES WILL NOT POP UP AS
OPERANDS; THEY WILL HAVE BEEN PASSED THROUGH ^N^A^M^E ON THEIR WAY TO
BECOMING <^V^B^L>S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^REGISTER - ^HAS NO PROPERTIES OTHER THAN A REGISTER.  ^A ^REGISTER OBJECT
USUALLY DESIGNATES A REGISTER HOLDING THE RESULT OF A COMPUTATION.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^CONSTANT - ^MAY BE A CONSTANT FROM THE SOURCE PROGRAM (IN WHICH CASE IT HAS
A NAME), OR A COMPUTED ONE.  ^IN ANY EVENT, IT HAS A VALUE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^VARIABLE - ^DESIGNATES A SIMPLE VARIABLE, WITH MAYBE A CONSTANT SUBSCRIPT.
           ^NO OTHER PROPERTIES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^MEMORY - ^DESIGNATES SOME ADDRESS IN MEMORY TOO COMPLICATED TO BE A
^VARIABLE.  ^MAY HAVE ANY OR ALL OF A NAME, A CONSTANT SUBSCRIPT, AN
INDEX REGISTER (CONTAINING THE VALUE OF A COMPUTED SUBSCRIPT), AND
EVEN AN INDIRECT BIT.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.1.1: ^CALLING ^CONVENTIONS FOR ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES.
.PAGE
.INDEX SEMANTIC ROUTINES, ARGUMENTS OF
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.1.1: ^CALLING ^CONVENTIONS FOR ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SEMANTIC PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT MAY CONSIST OF A CALL TO A SEMANTIC
ROUTINE, IN STANDARD ^I^M^P SUBROUTINE CALL FORMAT.  ^THE ARGUMENTS OF THE CALL MAY
BE EITHER:
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1. ^CONSTANTS, UNSIGNED, LESS THAN 30 BITS.  ^THE ARGUMENT PASSED TO THE
SEMANTIC ROUTINE IS THAT NUMBER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^IDENTIFIERS APPEARING AS NAMES OF INSTANCES OF CLASSES IN THE
PRODUCTION (E.G., ^A IN THE EXAMPLE OF ^SECTION 2.3.1).  ^THE ARGUMENT
PASSED TO THE SEMANTIC ROUTINE IS A "STACK POINTER" TO THE OBJECT
RECOGNIZED AS THAT PART OF THE PRODUCTION.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^IDENTIFIERS NOT APPEARING AS NAMES OF INSTANCES OF CLASSES.  ^THE
ARGUMENT PASSED TO THE SEMANTIC ROUTINE IS THE NEGATIVE OF THE DIRECTORY INDEX OF THE NAME.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4. ^ANOTHER CALL TO A SEMANTIC ROUTINE.  ^THE ARGUMENT PASSED IS THE VALUE
OF THE SEMANTIC ROUTINE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
5. ^ANY TWO OF THE ABOVE CONNECTED BY A + SIGN.  ^THE ARGUMENT PASSED IS
THE INDICATED SUM.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^ALTHOUGH A SEMANTIC PART CONSISTING OF CALLS ON SEMANTIC ROUTINES LOOKS LIKE
PART OF AN ^I^M^P PROGRAM, THERE ARE CERTAIN CONVENTIONS WHICH THE SEMANTICS WRITER
MAY TAKE ADVANTAGE OF HERE.  ^SEMANTIC ROUTINES MAY HAVE UP TO 10 ARGUMENTS.
^ARGUMENTS ARE EVALUATED STRICTLY LEFT TO RIGHT WITHIN ONE FUNCTION CALL.  ^IF
MORE OR FEWER ARGUMENTS ARE PROVIDED THAN THE ROUTINE DEMANDS, NO DIFFICULTY IS
ENCOUNTERED (EXCEPT THAT A REFERENCE TO A MISSING ARGUMENT WILL REFER TO A
RANDOM LOCATION IN USER CORE).
.PARAGRAPH
^THEREFORE, ALTHOUGH THE ";" OPERATOR IS NOT PROVIDED IN THE SYNTAX FOR
SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS, ITS EFFECTS OFTEN CAN BE ACHIEVED THROUGH THE USE OF
ARGUMENTS WHICH WILL BE EVALUATED BUT NOT USED.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, THE EFFECT OF
.SKIP
.CENTER
^F(^A,^B); ^G(^C,^D,^E)
.SKIP
MAY BE OBTAINED BY
.SKIP
.CENTER
^G(^C,^D,^E,^F(^A,^B))
.SKIP
IF IT DOES NOT MATTER THAT IN THE LATTER CASE ^C, ^D AND ^E ARE EVALUATED FIRST.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FOLLOWING IS AN EXAMPLE OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT USING SEMANTIC
SUBROUTINES TO SPECIFY SEMANTICS.  ^IT IS SUGGESTED THAT THE
READER INTERPRET THE SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS USING THE TABLE BELOW.  ^OPERATION 3 OF THE
ABSTRACT MACHINE IS ^C^O^M^P^L, THE ONES COMPLEMENT.
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^A^T^O^M>::= ^I^N^V ( <^A^T^O^M,^A> ) ::= ^D^E^W^O^P(3,^A^R^E^G1(1,15^B),^A);
.PARAGRAPH
^AFTER THIS STATEMENT HAS BEEN COMPILED (IN A FILE OF SYNTAX, ^SECTION 3.4), THE EXPRESSION ^I^N^V(5) HAS THE VALUE -6.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.1.2: ^TABLE OF ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES
.PAGE
.INDEX SEMANTIC ROUTINES, TABLE OF
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.1.2: ^TABLE OF ^SEMANTIC ^ROUTINES\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THIS TABLE, THE TYPES OF ARGUMENTS EXPECTED ARE INDICATED AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 30
.INDENT -20
^S, ^T, ^U:   ^OBJECTS (OTHER THAN ^NAMES EXCEPT WHERE SPECIFIED).
.INDENT -20
^R, ^P   :   ^REGISTERS.
.INDENT -20
^I, ^J, ^N:   ^CONSTANTS OR (WHEN SPECIFIED) NEGATIVE DIRECTORY INDICES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -20
* ^INDICATES A ROUTINE WHICH IS OBJECT MACHINE-DEPENDANT (I.E., WHICH MUST
BE ALTERED IF CODE FOR ANOTHER COMPUTER IS TO BE GENERATED.)
.LEFT MARGIN 18
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^N^A^M^E ^A^N^D ^A^R^G^U^M^E^N^T^S      ^E^F^F^E^C^T ^A^N^D ^R^E^S^U^L^T ^R^E^T^U^R^N^E^D.
.INDEX ^^ADDOP\\
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^A^D^D^O^P(^I,^S,^T)
.BREAK
*^PERFORMS A BINARY OPERATION ON ^S AND ^T.  ^IT IS DEFINED AS
^H^O^O^K(^S,^T, ^D^E^W^O^P(^I,^R^E^G^O^F(^F^E^T^C^H(^T)),^S)).  ^THIS COULD BE WRITTEN
IN SEMANTICS BUT USING ^A^D^D^O^P SAVES COMPILER TABLE SPACE AND
IS CLEARER BESIDES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^A^D^D^R(^S)
.BREAK
*^RESULT IS ^S.  ^THE OPERAND OF AN OPERATION OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE MAY BE A CONSTANT, SO THAT NO
OBJECT WITH THE VALUE IN THE ADDRESS FIELD IS NEEDED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^A^R^E^G(^I)
.BREAK
 ^RETURNS THE REGISTER INDEX FOR HARDWARE REGISTER ^I.
.INDEX ^^AREG1\\
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^A^R^E^G1(^I,^J)
.BREAK
 ^RETURNS A BRAND-NEW REGISTER, A TEMPORARY OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE, OF WHICH THERE IS AN UNLIMITED SUPPLY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^B^Y^T^E^P(^S,^T,^U)
.BREAK
*^S IS A ^VARIABLE-TYPE OBJECT.  ^MAKES A BYTE POINTER FOR ^S<^T,^U>, COMPUTED WHEN THE CODE RUNS (^SECTION 2.2.6).
^RESULT IS ^S, WHOSE VALUE IS NOW THE BYTE POINTER.  ^GOOD THINGS TO ^D^E^W^O^P ON ^S ARE THE BYTE OPERATIONS
24 TO 27.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^C^O^N^O^P(^S,^T,^I,^U)
.BREAK
 ^PERFORMS AN OPERATION DESIGNATED BY ^I ON TWO ^CONSTANT-TYPE
OBJECTS ^S AND ^T.  ^SEE FILE ^S^Y^N^T^A^X OR THE SOURCE FOR ^C^O^N^O^P
FOR THE CODES FOR ^I.  ^FEEL FREE TO ADD A FEW MORE OPERATIONS
IF IT WILL GENERATE BETTER CODE.  ^U IS THE IMPLICAND IN CASES
OF ^C^O^N ^R^E^L^O^P ^C^O^N=>^U.  ^RESULT IS ^S, WITH CODE FOR ^T HOOKED IN
FIRST IF THERE IS ANY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^^COPY(S)\\
.BREAK
^PRODUCES A COPY OF THE OBJECT ^S, INCLUDING A COPY OF THE CODE
ASSOCIATED WITH IT, IF ANY.  ^USEFUL IF ^S IS TO BE HOOKED IN IN
TWO DIFFERENT PLACES IN CODE TO BE GENERATED.  ^CARE MUST BE TAKEN
TO USE THE COPY OF ^S IN THE PLACE WHICH IS HOOKED IN FIRST, SINCE
TRYING TO COPY AN OBJECT THAT HAS ALREADY BEEN HOOKED IN MAY
IMPERIL THE INTERNAL TRANQUILITY OF THE COMPILER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^D^A^T^A^S^T(^S)
.BREAK
 ^FOR USE IN ^D^A^T^A STATEMENTS.  ^S IS ANY OLD OBJECT; IT GETS
CLOBBERED.  ^UNPACKS THE CURRENT LIST (SEE ^E^N^L^I^S^T), AND
GENERATES CODE FOR THE ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT WHOSE CONSTANTS AND
VARIABLES ARE IN THE LIST.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^D^E^C^L
.INDENT -18
^D^E^C^L^A^R^E
.BREAK
 ^SPECIAL SEMANTIC ROUTINES TO IMPLEMENT DECLARATIONS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^D^E^W^F^U^N(^S,^I,^J)
.BREAK
^ADDS THE CODE TO THE OBJECT ^S WHICH INSTRUCTS THE ASSEMBLY PASS
TO PERFORM SPECIAL FUNCTION ^I, WITH ARGUMENT ^J.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^D^E^W^O^P
.INDENT -18
^D^E^W^O^P(^I,^R,^S)
.BREAK
*^PERFORMS OPERATION ^I OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE (THE TABLE BELOW) UPON OBJECT ^S AND (IF NONZERO)
REGISTER ^R.  ^THIS IS A VERY SMART ROUTINE AND IS HAPPY WITH
ANY OBJECT (EXCEPT A ^NAME) AS ^S.  ^CAUTION:
^IF ^D^E^W^O^P IS USED TO IMPLEMENT A BINARY OPERATOR (AS IN
^D^E^W^O^P(^O^P,^R^E^G^O^F(^T),^S), THE CODE FOR ^T MUST BE HOOKED ON TO THE
CODE FOR ^S AFTER THE ^D^E^W^O^P IS PERFORMED, OTHERWISE IT WILL BE
LOST.  ^SEE ^A^D^D^O^P.  ^RESULT OF ^D^E^W^O^P IS ^S.  ^IF ^R IS NONZERO, ^S
IS NOW A ^REGISTER OBJECT, WITH VALUE IN THE REGISTER DESIGNATED BY ^R.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^E^N^L^I^S^T(^S)
.BREAK
 ^PLACES THE OBJECT ^S AT THE BOTTOM OF THE CURRENT LIST.  ^E^N^L^I^S^T
IS PART OF A GENERAL RECURSIVE LIST MECHANISM FOR STACKING UP
LISTS OF THINGS TO BE FED ALL AT ONCE TO SOME SEMANTIC ROUTINE.  ^N^E^W^L^I^S^T(^S) CREATES AN EMPTY LIST, AND, IF ^S IS NONZERO,
PUTS THE OBJECT ^S ON IT.  ^G^E^T^L^I^S^T(^S) IS A NON-SEMANTIC SUBROUTINE WHICH UNPACKS THE CURRENT LIST ON A FIRST-IN-FIRST-OUT
BASIS.  ^IF THE LIST IS NOT EMPTY, ^G^E^T^L^I^S^T(^S) PUTS THE TOP
OBJECT INTO THE OBJECT ^S (CLOBBERING THE OLD VALUE), AND RETURNS A NON-ZERO VALUE.  ^IF THE LIST IS EMPTY, ^G^E^T^L^I^S^T RETURNS
0, AND REOPENS THE LIST WHICH WAS CURRENT AT THE LAST CALL TO
^N^E^W^L^I^S^T.  ^SEE ^D^E^C^L^A^R^E (FILE ^I^M^P^S^E^M) FOR AN EXAMPLE OF A SEMANTIC ROUTINE WHICH USES ^G^E^T^L^I^S^T.  ^THE RESULT OF ^E^N^L^I^S^T IS 0.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^E^N^S^T^A^C^K(^I)
.BREAK
 ^CREATES AN OBJECT OF TYPE ^NAME, WHERE ^I IS THE DIRECTORY INDEX
(EITHER POSITIVE OR NEGATIVE) OF THE NAME.  ^FOR EXAMPLE TO
MAKE THE OBJECT CORRESPONDING TO THE VARIABLE ^V^A^R, WRITE
^N^A^M^E(^E^N^S^T^A^C^K(^V^A^R)).
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^E^R^R^O^R(^N,^V)
.BREAK
^PRODUCES THE COMPILER ERROR MESSAGE WHICH IS THE NAME
OF THE VARIABLE ^V.  ^N IS 0, 1 OR 2 FOR A FATAL, ORDINARY,
OR ADVISORY ERROR RESPCTIVELY.  ^FOR ERROR MESSAGES
CONTAINING SPACES, ETC., ENCLOSE THE MESSAGE IN ! SIGNS
TO MAKE IT INTO A VARIABLE NAME.  ^EXAMPLE:
^E^R^R^O^R(2,!^YOU ^GOOFED.!).
.SKIP
.INDEX ^F^E^T^C^H
.INDENT -18
^F^E^T^C^H(^S)
.BREAK
*^FORCES THE OBJECT ^S TO BE OF TYPE ^REGISTER (I.E., LOADS IT
INTO A REGISTER).  ^RESULT IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^F^I^X(^S)
.BREAK
*^GENERATES CODE TO CONVERT ^S FROM FLOATING POINT TO INTEGER.
^VALUE IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^F^L^O^A^T(^S)
.BREAK
*^GENERATES CODE TO CONVERT ^S FROM INTEGER TO FLOATING POINT.
^VALUE IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^H^O^O^K
.INDENT -18
^H^O^O^K(^U,^S,^T)
.BREAK
 ^HOOKS THE CODE FOR ^T ON AFTER THE CODE FOR ^S.  ^U GETS THIS
CODE, AND THE VALUE OF ^T.  ^U MAY BE ONE OF ^S OR ^T (AND USUALLY
IS).  ^H^O^O^K THUS PERFORMS THE ";" OPERATOR, AMONG ITS OTHER
USES.  ^IT MUST BE USED WHENEVER TWO OR MORE ARGUMENTS WHICH
MIGHT HAVE CODE ATTACHED APPEAR IN A PRODUCTION, EXCEPT IN
THE FEW CASES WHERE ANOTHER SEMANTIC ROUTINE INVOKES ^H^O^O^K
IMPLICITLY.  ^THE RESULT OF ^H^O^O^K IS ^U.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^M^A^X^W^E^L^L
.INDENT -18
^M^A^X^E^N^D
.BREAK
 ^SPECIALIZED SUBROUTINES TO HANDLE THE JOB OF SWITCHING PARSER
OUTPUT INTO A TEMPORARY ARRAY RATHER THAN FEEDING IT TO THE
CODE GENERATOR.  ^USED TO IMPLEMENT QUOTED SEMANTICS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^N^A^M^E(^S)
.BREAK
 ^S IS A ^NAME-TYPE OBJECT.  ^N^A^M^E TURNS IT INTO A VARIABLE,
ADDING AN ARITHMETIC TYPE, AND MAKING THINGS RIGHT IF ^S IS
A REGISTER OR SUBROUTINE PARAMETER.  ^RESULT IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^N^E^W^L^I^S^T(^S)
.BREAK
 ^SEE ^E^N^L^I^S^T.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^O^J^U^M^P^O^P(^I)
.BREAK
*^I IS THE CODE OF A RELATIONAL OPERATOR (1 <, 2 =, 3 ^L^E, 5 ^G^E, 6 ^N^E, 7 >).
^O^J^U^M^P^O^P RETURNS THE CODE OF THE NEGATION OF THAT OPERATOR.
^IF ^I HAS THE 10^B BIT SET, ^O^J^U^M^P^O^P RETURNS THE CODE FOR THE REVERSE NEGATION
(THE REVERSE OF >, FOR EXAMPLE, IS <.)
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^P^A^R(^I)
.BREAK
 ^USED IN ^C^A^S^E SEMANTICS TO REFER TO THE ^ITH ARGUMENT (COUNTING
FROM 0) OF THE ^C^A^S^E ARGUMENT LIST (SEE SECTION 2.3.3.3.).
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^P^R^I^N^C^A^L
.BREAK
.INDENT -18
^^PRINPAR\\
.BREAK
*^SPECIALIZED ROUTINES HANDLING SEMANTICS FOR ^P^R^I^N^T AND ^R^E^A^D
^P^R^I^N^P^A^R           STATEMENTS.
.SKIP
.INDEX ^R^E^G^O^F
.INDENT -18
^R^E^G^O^F(^S)
.BREAK
 ^RESULT IS THE REGISTER OF THE OBJECT ^S, OR 0 IF IT  DOESN'T
HAVE ONE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^R^E^G2^S(^S)
.BREAK
^IF ^S IS OF TYPE REGISTER, AND THE REGISTER WAS ASSIGNED
BY ^A^R^E^G1 SO AS TO RESERVE TWO CONSECUTIVE REGISTERS,
THEN THE VALUE OF ^S BECOMES THE CONTENTS OF THE SECOND
REGISTER.  ^S IS OTHERWISE UNCHANGED.  ^IF ^S IS NOT AS
SPECIFIED, UNDEFINED THINGS HAPPEN.  ^THE VALUE OF ^R^E^G2^S
IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^R^E^M^O^T^E(^S)
.BREAK
 ^CAUSES THE CODE ASSOCIATED WITH ^S TO BE INSERTED AT THE END
OF THE COMPILATION INSTEAD OF AT THE CURRENT POINT.  ^RESULT
IS ^S, BUT WITH NO CODE.  (^THIS MAY LEAD TO INCORRECT RESULTS
                  IF AN ATTEMPT IS MADE TO COMPUTE WITH THE VALUE OF ^S.)
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^R^E^P^V^A^L(^S,^T)
.BREAK
 ^PART OF THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX.  ^GENERALIZES THE ^V^A^L^U^E
CLASS ^S WITHIN THE QUOTED SEMANTICS ^T (SEE ^SECTION 2.3.3.4).
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^R^E^T^U^R^N(^S)
.BREAK
*^GENERATES CODE TO RETURN FROM THE CURRENT SUBROUTINE
WITH ^S AS THE VALUE OF THE SUBROUTINE.  ^RESULT IS ^S, NOT
THAT IT MATTERS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^T^A^C^K^U^P(^I)
.BREAK
 ^I IS EITHER A NEGATIVE DIRECTORY INDEX OR A CONSTANT.
^THIS ROUTINE IS USED TO CREATE OBJECTS AND PLACE THEM ON
THE STACK. (^IF ^I IS A CONSTANT, THE CONSTANT IS FIRST ENTERED
IN THE DIRECTORY, SO THAT A DIRECTORY INDEX FOR THE ITEM
EXISTS.)  ^N^A^M^E(^E^N^S^T^A^C^K(^I)) IS THEN PERFORMED TO MAKE THE
OBJECT. ^RESULT IS STACK INDEX OF THE ITEM.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^E^T^P^R^I(^I)
.BREAK
 ^PART OF THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX.  ^SETS THE DESIGNATED PRIORITY BITS IN THE SEMANTICS BEING GENERATED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^T^A^C^K(^I)
.BREAK
 ^RETURNS THE ^ITH ARGUMENT OF THE PRODUCTION.  ^USED TO REFER TO
ARGUMENTS WHICH ARE ^V^A^L^U^E CLASSES IN DEFAULT SEMANTICS (I.E.,
SEMANTICS WHICH ARE NOT CONDITIONAL.)
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^T^O^R^E(^S,^T)
.BREAK
*^STORES THE VALUE OF ^T IN ^S, HOOKING THE CODE FOR ^S ON AFTER
THAT FOR ^T.  ^RESULT IS ^T.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^U^B^B^E^G(^S)
.BREAK
*^GENERATES CODE FOR THE BEGINNING OF SUBROUTINE ^S, WHERE ^S IS
A ^NAME-TYPE OBJECT.  ^RESULT IS ^S, WITH THE CODE ATTACHED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^U^B^P^R0(^I)
.BREAK
 ^CALLED WITH ^I=0 AT THE BEGINNING, AND ^I=1 AT THE END, OF EVERY
SUBROUTINE CALL.  ^KEEPS TRACK OF THE LEVEL OF CALLS, AND
RESETS THE INDEX OF THE NEXT TEMPORARY FOR SUBROUTINE ARGUMENTS TO 0 WHENEVER IT GETS UP TO LEVEL 0.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^U^B^R^C^A^L^L(^S)
.BREAK
*^S MUST BE OF TYPE ^VARIABLE.  ^GENERATES A SUBROUTINE CALL TO ^S
(THE OPERATION ^C^A^L^L^B), AND A REFERENCE TO THE FUNCTION ^S.  ^THE CALL IS COMPLETED BY A
^D^E^W^F^U^N(^T,2,^R^E^G^O^F(^T),^S^U^B^P^R0(1)), WHERE ^T IS THE
COMPLETE SUBROUTINE CALL WITH ARGUMENTS.  ^THE RESULT OF
^S^U^B^R^C^A^L^L IS ^S, WHOSE VALUE IS NOW REGISTER 0^R, WHICH IS WHERE
SUBROUTINES RETURN VALUES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^U^B^R^P^A^R(^S,^T)
.BREAK
*^S IS A SUBROUTINE CALL OR SOME SIMILAR THING BEING BUILT, AND
^T IS AN ARGUMENT.  ^THE ADDRESS OF ^T IS PASSED AS THE NEXT ARGUMENT OF THE CALL
(THE OPERATION ^A^R^G).  ^IF ^T HAS NO ADDRESS, THE ADDRESS IS THAT OF A
TEMPORARY CONTAINING THE VALUE OF ^T.  ^THE CODE FOR ^T IS HOOKED ON TO THE BEGINNING OF
^S.  ^THE RESULT IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^U^B^S^C^R^I^P^T(^S,^T)
.BREAK
*^GENERATES CODE FOR THE ^VARIABLE- OR ^MEMORY-TYPE OBJECT ^S[^T].
^IS SMART ABOUT NOT COMPUTING CONSTANT SUBSCRIPTS, AND SO ON.  ^S
AND ^T MAY BE ANY TYPE EXCEPT NAME.  ^RESULT IS ^S, WITH THE
CODE FOR ^T HOOKED ON BEFORE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^V^A^L(^I)
.BREAK
 ^CREATES AN OBJECT WHOSE ^V^A^L IS ^I.  (^SEE ALSO ^V^A^L).
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^W^I^T^C^H(^I)
.BREAK
^TURNS ON THE COMPILER SWITCH WHICH IS THE ^ITH LETTER OF
THE ALPHABET.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^T^A^G(^S)
.BREAK
 ^S HAD BETTER BE AN OBJECT OF TYPE ^NAME, OR ^VARIABLE WITHOUT
A SUBSCRIPT.  ^ADDS TO ^S THE CODE WHICH DEFINES THE TAG ^S AT
THAT POINT.  ^RESULT IS ^S.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^V^A^L(^S)
.BREAK
 ^RETURNS THE LOW ORDER 32 BITS OF THE VALUE OF THE OBJECT ^S.
^IF ^S IS A CONSTANT, THEN THIS IS THE LOW 32 BITS OF ITS VALUE.
^IF ^S IS THE RESULT OF A ^V^A^L^U^E SEMANTICS, THEN THIS IS THE
VALUE SPECIFIED THERE.  ^IF ^S HAS HAD A VALUE SET BY ^S^V^A^L, THEN
THIS IS THAT VALUE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^V^A^L^U(^S,^T)
.BREAK
 ^PART OF THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX.  ^DEFINES THE CURRENT SEMANTICS TO BE ^V^A^L^U^E ^S ^O^F ^T, WHERE ^S IS A ^CONSTANT OBJECT AND ^T
IS A ^NAME OBJECT.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^M^O^V^E^B^L^K(^S,^N,^T)
.BREAK
^GENERATES THE CALL OF THE LIBRARY ROUTINE THAT COPIES THE ^N+1 WORDS FROM ^S TO ^T; THE SEMANTICS OF ^M^O^V^E.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^J^O^P(^C)
.BREAK
^THE JUMP OPERATION (41 TO 46) FOR THE RELATIONAL OPERATOR WITH CODE ^C; 10 MORE FOR A
COMPARISON OF REALS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^C^J^U^M^P(^O^P,^P,^R,^S)
.BREAK
^JUMP TO THE TAG ^S IF ^P AND ^R ARE RELATED BY THE JUMP OPERATION ^O^P.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^C^V^A^L(^S)
.BREAK
^MAKES THE NAME ^S THE NAME OF A ^C FUNCTION THAT IS CALLED WITH ITS ARGUMENTS BY VALUE (^SECTION 2.4.1);
THE SEMANTICS OF ^C^C^A^L^L USE IT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^C^D^E^C^L(^T,^F,^L,^V)
.BREAK
^ENTERS THE TYPES OF THE ^C FUNCTION ^F, OF THE RESULT TYPE ^T AND THE PARAMETER TYPES IN THE LIST ^L (0 FOR NONE); ^V IS THE SUM OF 1 IF THE FUNCTION
TAKES MORE ARGUMENTS, 2 FOR A CALLBACK AND 4 FOR A CALL THROUGH A POINTER (^SECTION 2.4.1, 2.4.5).  ^C^S^U^B^C^A^L^L(^S,^L) AND ^C^C^A^L^L0(^S) CALL IT, CHECKING THE NUMBER OF THE ARGUMENTS AND CONVERTING THEM
TO THE DECLARED TYPES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^C^B^A^D^D^R(^A)
.BREAK
^MAKES THE ADDRESS OF THE ^C FUNCTION THAT CALLS THE ^S^U^B^R NAMED ^A, WHICH ^C^D^E^C^L DECLARED A CALLBACK (^V IS 2) (^SECTION 2.4.5).  ^FOR A POINTER CALL ^V IS 4, OR 5 WITH THE DOTS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^T^B^E^G(^N)
.BREAK
^BEGINS THE DECLARATION OF THE STRUCTURE ^[N^] (SECTION 2.4.6); ^[STFLD(T,F,N)^] ADDS THE FIELD ^[F^] OF THE TYPE ^[T^] (AN ARRAY OF ^[N^] IF ^[N^] IS NOT 0) AND PLACES IT BY THE ALIGNMENT RULES OF THE TARGET, AND ^[STEND(H)^] ENDS THE STRUCTURE AND ROUNDS ITS SIZE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^S^T^S^I^Z^E(^S)
.BREAK
^MAKES THE CONSTANT THAT IS THE SIZE OF THE STRUCTURE OR TYPE OF ^C NAMED ^[S^]; ^[FPBEG(S,F)^] AND ^[FPFLD(P,F)^] FOLLOW A PATH OF FIELDS FROM A STRUCTURE, AND ^[STOFF(P)^] MAKES THE CONSTANT THAT IS THE OFFSET THE PATH ENDS AT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^F^L^D^P(^P,^F,^I)
.BREAK
^MAKES THE DESCRIPTOR OF THE FIELD AT THE END OF THE PATH ^[F^] OF THE STRUCTURE AT THE ADDRESS ^[P^], WITH THE ELEMENT ^[I^] (0 FOR NONE); ^[DEWOP(24,R,D)^] LOADS IT AND ^[FLDST(A,B)^] CONVERTS ^[B^] TO ITS TYPE AND STORES IT, WITH THE OPERATION 25.
.SKIP
.INDENT -18
^F^C^O^N(^A,^B,^C)
.BREAK
^MAKES THE FLOATING POINT CONSTANT ^A.^B"^C.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN ADDITION TO THE ABOVE, THERE ARE A NUMBER OF SEMANTIC ROUTINES WHICH
DEFINE THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX, ALL ON FILE ^R^S^Y^N.  ^SEE ^SECTION 4.4 FOR
DOCUMENTATION.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OPERATIONS OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE ARE THE FOLLOWING.  ^THE ACCUMULATOR ^R^D IS A REGISTER
AND ^V THE OPERAND.  ^INTEGERS ARE 64-BIT TWO'S COMPLEMENT NUMBERS; ^D^I^V TRUNCATES TOWARD ZERO AND ^M^O^D HAS THE SIGN OF THE
DIVIDEND.
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 5
1 ^L^O^A^D  ^R^D=^V      2 ^N^E^G  ^R^D=-^V      3 ^C^O^M^P^L  ^R^D=~^V      4 ^A^D^D^R  ^R^D=THE ADDRESS OF ^V
5 ^S^T^O^R^E ^V=^R^D
6 ^A^D^D  7 ^S^U^B  8 ^M^U^L  9 ^D^I^V  10 ^M^O^D                       ^R^D=^R^D OP ^V
11 ^A^N^D  12 ^A^N^D^C^A (~^R^D _& ^V)  13 ^A^N^D^C^M (^R^D _& ~^V)  14 ^A^N^D^C^B (~^R^D _& ~^V)
15 ^I^O^R  16 ^O^R^C^A (~^R^D | ^V)  17 ^O^R^C^M (^R^D | ~^V)  18 ^O^R^C^B (~^R^D | ~^V)
19 ^X^O^R  20 ^E^Q^V (~(^R^D _^ ^V))
21 ^L^S^H  ^R^D SHIFTED LOGICALLY BY THE SIGNED COUNT ^V (LEFT IF POSITIVE);
        64 PLACES OR MORE GIVE 0
22 ^A^S^H  THE SAME, ARITHMETIC    23 ^R^O^T  ^R^D ROTATED LEFT BY ^V
24 ^L^D^B  ^R^D=THE BYTE FIELD ^V NAMES    25 ^D^P^B  THE LOW BITS OF ^R^D INTO THE FIELD ^V NAMES
26 ^I^L^D^B 27 ^I^D^P^B  THE SAME AFTER STEPPING THE BYTE POINTER ^V
28 ^A^D^D^X  ^R^D=^R^D+8*^V (A WORD INDEX ADDED TO A POINTER)
30 ^F^A^D^D  31 ^F^S^U^B  32 ^F^M^U^L  33 ^F^D^I^V                      ^R^D=^R^D OP ^V, ON DOUBLES
34 ^F^L^O^A^T ^R^D=(DOUBLE)^V    35 ^F^I^X  ^R^D=(LONG)^V, TRUNCATED    36 ^F^N^E^G  ^R^D=-^V
40 ^J^U^M^P  TO THE TAG NAMED BY ^V
41 ^J^E^Q  42 ^J^L^T  43 ^J^L^E  44 ^J^G^E  45 ^J^G^T  46 ^J^N^E         JUMP TO THE TAG IF ^R^D CMP ^V
51 TO 56 ^F^J^E^Q TO ^F^J^N^E                                   THE SAME ON DOUBLES
60 ^C^A^L^L^B BEGIN A CALL    61 ^A^R^G  THE ADDRESS OF ^V IS THE NEXT ARGUMENT
62 ^C^A^L^L^E END THE CALL, ^R^D RECEIVES THE VALUE
63 ^P^I^T^E^M PRINT LIST ITEM: THE FORMAT CODE, AND ITS OPERAND
64 ^C^A^L^L^I  ^R^D=THE LIBRARY ROUTINE ^I^M^P^C^A^L^L^I(^V,^R^D)    65 ^X^C^T  DO THE INSTRUCTION IN THE WORD ^V
66 ^H^R^L^M  67 ^H^R^R^M  THE LOW 32 BITS OF ^R^D INTO THE LEFT OR RIGHT HALF OF ^V
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OPERAND ^V IS A REGISTER, A CONSTANT, A VARIABLE, OR A SUBSCRIPTED VARIABLE (A VARIABLE PLUS 8 TIMES A WORD OFFSET, PLUS 8 TIMES
A REGISTER).  ^AN ADDRESS IS A BYTE ADDRESS.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.2: ^CONDITIONAL ^SEMANTICS.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.2: ^CONDITIONAL ^SEMANTICS.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX CONDITIONAL SEMANTICS
.INDEX SEMANTICS, CONDITIONAL
.PARAGRAPH
^WE SAW ABOVE THAT SEMANTICS MAY CONSIST OF EITHER A SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALL
OR QUOTED SEMANTICS.  ^IT IS POSSIBLE TO INVOKE ONE OF A NUMBER OF SEMANTICS
FOR A GIVEN SYNTACTIC PRODUCTION, DEPENDING ON THE PARTICULAR CASE OF THAT
PRODUCTION BEING COMPILED.  ^FIRST AN EXAMPLE:
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^A^T^O^M>::= ^N^O^T <^A^T^O^M,^A>  ::= "^N^O^T ^B0^C^O^N"=>^C^O^N^O^P(^B,0,6) ^E^L^S^E
.CENTER
^D^E^W^O^P(3,^A^R^E^G1(1,15^B),^A);
.SKIP
^THE SEMANTIC PART OF THIS SYNTAX STATEMENT INVOKES DIFFERENT SEMANTICS FOR THE
SPECIAL CASE OF ^N^O^T-CONSTANT.
.PARAGRAPH
^SEMANTICS MAY CONSIST OF A NUMBER OF ALTERNATIVES, SEPARATED BY THE IDENTIFIER ^E^L^S^E.  ^ONE ALTERNATIVE MAY CONSIST SIMPLY OF A SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALL OR
QUOTED SEMANTICS: THIS IS THE SEMANTICS EXECUTED IF NONE OF THE SPECIAL CASES
OBTAIN.  ^THE OTHER ALTERNATIVES ARE OF THE FORM
.SKIP
.CENTER
CONDITION => SEMANTICS
.SKIP
WHERE THE SEMANTICS IS A SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALL OR QUOTED SEMANTICS, AND THE CONDITION IS AN ^I^M^P EXPRESSION WHICH IS A CASE OF THE PRODUCTION, ENCLOSED IN
DOUBLE QUOTES.  ^THE ARGUMENTS OF THE SEMANTICS SHOULD BE IDENTIFIERS
WHICH DESIGNATE ARGUMENTS IN THE CONDITION.  ^REFERRING TO IDENTIFIERS WHICH
DESIGNATE ARGUMENTS IN THE SYNTAX PART WILL NOT GIVE A DIAGNOSTIC BUT MIGHT
PRODUCE UNDESIRED RESULTS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE CONDITION IS SATISFIED IF THE EXPRESSION BEING PARSED MATCHES THE
EXPRESSION IN THE CONDITION.  ^CONSTANTS IN THE CONDITION WILL MATCH ANY
CONSTANT WITH THE SAME VALUE, EVEN IF THE NAMES ARE DIFFERENT.  (^BUT CONSTANT
EXPRESSIONS IN THE CONDITION ARE NOT EVALUATED.  ^THUS, "-18" IN A CONDITION
WOULD ONLY MATCH IN CASES WHERE A MINUS SIGN WAS ACTUALLY USED BEFORE A CONSTANT WITH VALUE 18.  ^ONE SHOULD TRUST THE COMPILER TO PERFORM CONSTANT
ARITHMETIC, AND WRITE 1777777777777777777756^B INSTEAD, WHICH WILL MATCH ANY CONSTANT
EXPRESSION WHICH EVALUATES TO -18.)  ^VARIABLE NAMES
WILL MATCH ANY <^E^X^P>, UNLESS THEY ARE TAGGED WITH MODIFIERS.  ^IF THE
SAME NAME APPEARS TWICE OR MORE IN THE CONDITION, THE EXPRESSIONS MATCHING
ALL OCCURRENCES MUST BE IDENTICAL.
.INDEX MODIFIERS (IN SEMANTIC CONDITIONS)
.PARAGRAPH
^MODIFIERS MAY BE ADDED TO THE FIRST OCCURRENCE OF A GIVEN VARIABLE NAME IN
A CONDITION BY ADDING THE CHARACTER '0' TO THE NAME, FOLLOWED BY THE MODIFIERS.
^MODIFIERS ALL CONSIST OF THREE LETTERS,SOMETIMES FOLLOWED BY ONE OR TWO TWODIGIT OCTAL NUMBERS.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, ^A0^C^O^N, ^F^O^O0^R^E^G0115, ^I0^I^G^R^V^A^R^M^E^M^R^E^G.  ^THE
MODIFIERS ARE:
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -10
^ARITHMETIC TYPE MODIFIERS (^IDENTIFIERS WITH NEITHER OF THESE MATCH EITHER
TYPE):
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^I^G^R:      ^IDENTIFIER MATCHES ONLY OBJECTS OF TYPE INTEGER.
.INDENT -5
^F^L^T:      ^IDENTIFIER MATCHES ONLY OBJECTS OF TYPE REAL.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
^OBJECT TYPE MODIFIERS (^IDENTIFIERS WITH NONE OF THESE MATCH ANY OBJECT TYPE;
IDENTIFIERS WITH SEVERAL MATCH ANY OF THE INDICATED TYPES):
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^R^E^G:      ^MATCHES OBJECTS OF TYPE ^REGISTER.
.INDENT -5
^R^E^GII:    ^MATCHES OBJECTS GUARANTEED TO BE IN REGISTER II.
.INDENT -5
^R^E^GIIJJ:  ^MATCHES OBJECTS GUARANTEED TO BE IN A REGISTER BETWEEN II AND
JJ.  ^RANGE FOR II AND JJ IS 0-37 (OCTAL).
.INDENT -5
^C^O^N:      ^MATCHES OBJECTS OF TYPE CONSTANT.
.INDENT -5
^C^O^NII:    ^MATCHES OBJECTS WHICH ARE CONSTANT, AND ZERO EXCEPT FOR THE II
RIGHTMOST BITS.
.INDENT -5
^C^O^NIIJJ:  ^MATCHES OBJECTS WHICH ARE CONSTANT, AND ZERO EXCEPT FOR THE II
BITS STARTING JJ FROM THE RIGHT END OF THE WORD.  ^RANGE FOR
II AND JJ IS 0-77 (OCTAL).
.INDENT -5
^C^N^G:      ^SAME AS ^C^O^N (AND MAY HAVE II OR IIJJ OR ATTACHED), BUT MATCHES
OBJECTS WHICH ARE CONSTANT IN THE DESIGNATED FIELD, WITH ALL
BITS OUTSIDE THAT FIELD SET TO 1.  ^C^O^N AND ^C^N^G SHOULD NOT
BOTH BE USED TO MODIFY THE SAME IDENTIFIER.
.INDENT -5
^V^A^R:      ^MATCHES OBJECTS OF TYPE ^VARIABLE.
.INDENT -5
^M^E^M:      ^MATCHES OBJECTS OF TYPE ^MEMORY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -10
^CODE MODIFIER:
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^W^R^D:      ^MATCHES OBJECTS WITH EXACTLY ONE WORD OF CODE ASSOCIATED WITH
THEM.  ^MAY NOT ALWAYS SUCCEED WHEN ONE MIGHT THINK IT SHOULD,
DUE TO THE ORDER IN WHICH OBJECTS ARE COMBINED BY THE CODE
GENERATOR, BUT WILL ALWAYS FAIL WHEN IT SHOULD.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^NUMEROUS EXAMPLES OF CONDITIONAL SEMANTICS MAY BE FOUND ON FILE ^S^Y^N^T^A^X,
WHICH CONTAINS THE SYNTAX FOR ^I^M^P72.
.PARAGRAPH
^CARE SHOULD BE TAKEN TO SPECIFY SPECIAL CASES AS CASES OF THE PROPER
PRODUCTION.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, SUPPOSE ONE WISHED TO REFER TO THE LOW 32 BITS
(THE RIGHT HALF) OF A VARIABLE BY THE CONSTRUCT /^V^A^R^I^A^B^L^E/.  ^THIS MIGHT BE ACCOMPLISHED BY
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^V^B^L> ::= / <^V^B^L,^A> / ::= "^A<32,0>"
.SKIP
WHICH NAMES THE BYTE OF ^A THAT IS ITS RIGHT HALF.  ^IF ONE WANTED ALSO TO BE ABLE TO WRITE
.SKIP
.CENTER
/^V^A^R^I^A^B^L^E/ := ^E^X^P^R^E^S^S^I^O^N
.SKIP
AND STORE THE VALUE OF THE EXPRESSION IN THE RIGHT HALF OF VARIABLE, ONE MIGHT
THEN WRITE
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^V^B^L> ::= / <^V^B^L,^A> / ::= "^A<32,0>"
.CENTER
   ^E^L^S^E "/^A/:=^B" => "^A<32,0>:=^B"
.SKIP
^BUT /^A/:=^B IS A SPECIAL CASE NOT OF THIS PRODUCTION BUT OF THE PRODUCTION FOR
":=".  ^THE ABOVE SYNTAX STATEMENT WOULD PRODUCE AN ERROR DIAGNOSTIC.  ^THE
CORRECT DEFINITION WOULD BE (THE TOKEN ^[:=^] IS WRITTEN IN QUOTES IN THE SYNTAX PART OF A
SYNTAX STATEMENT, LIKE THE OTHER CHARACTERS THAT THE NOTATION USES)
.SKIP
.NOFILL
<^V^B^L> ::= / <^V^B^L,^A> / ::= "^A<32,0>";
<^E^X^P>  ::= <^V^B^L,^A> ':=' <^E^X^P,^B> ::= "/^A/:=^B" => "^A<32,0>:=^B"
.FILL
.INDEX ^D^U^P^L^I^C^A^T^E ^P^R^O^D^U^C^T^I^O^N^S
.SKIP
^THIS WOULD PRODUCE AN ADVISORY DIAGNOSTIC, SINCE THE SYNTAX IN THE SECOND STATEMENT DUPLICATES A PRODUCTION ALREADY IN THE LANGUAGE, BUT THIS IS PERMITTED IN
ORDER TO ALLOW DEFINING ADDITIONAL SPECIAL CASES OF SEMANTICS, EXACTLY AS IS
DONE HERE.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.3: ^C^A^S^ES.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^C^A^S^E
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.3: ^C^A^S^ES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^IF TWO OR MORE PRODUCTIONS HAVE EXACTLY THE SAME SYNTAX, EXCEPT FOR
TERMINAL SYMBOLS (I.E., THEY CONTAIN THE SAME SYNTACTIC CLASSES IN THE SAME
ORDER, AND ARE INSTANCES OF THE SAME CLASS), AND IF THEIR SEMANTICS ARE
DEFINED BY IDENTICAL SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS, WITH PERHAPS ONLY A FEW CONSTANTS
CHANGED (E.G., DIFFERENT OPCODES), THEN IT IS POSSIBLE TO DEFINE A GENERAL CASE,
AND DEFINE THE SEMANTICS OF EACH PRODUCTION AS A SPECIAL CASE OF IT.
.PARAGRAPH
^EXAMPLE:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.TEST PAGE 10
<^E^X^P> ::= <^A^T^O^M,^I> ^L^S <^E^X^P,^J> ::= ^C^A^S^E (21,0,0,5) ^O^F
      ^S^H^I^F^T^S  (^A^D^D^O^P(^P^A^R(0),^J,^I)
      ^E^L^S^E "^A0^C^O^N ^L^S ^B0^C^O^N"=>^C^O^N^O^P(^A,^B,^P^A^R(3)));
<^E^X^P> ::= <^A^T^O^M,^A>  ^A^L^S <^E^X^P,^B> ::=
       ^C^A^S^E (22,0,0,16) ^O^F ^S^H^I^F^T^S;
<^E^X^P> ::= <^A^T^O^M,^A> ^L^R^O^T <^E^X^P,^B> ::=
       ^C^A^S^E (23,0,0,17) ^O^F ^S^H^I^F^T^S;
.FILL
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FIRST STATEMENT DEFINES THE SYNTAX FOR ^I ^L^S ^J.  ^THE SEMANTIC PART DEFINES
THE CASE ^S^H^I^F^T^S TO CONSIST OF THE SET OF SEMANTIC ALTERNATIVES WITHIN THE PARENTHESES FOLLOWING THE IDENTIFIER ^S^H^I^F^T^S.  (^NOTICE THAT THE ^A0^C^O^N ^L^S ^B0^C^O^N ALTERNATIVE IS NOT PART OF THE ^S^H^I^F^T^S DEFINITION BUT IS AN ALTERNATIVE TO IT.)
^THE LIST OF CONSTANTS FOLLOWING THE IDENTIFIER ^C^A^S^E IS THE "PARAMETER LIST" FOR
THIS PARTICULAR INSTANCE (^L^S) OF THE CASE ^S^H^I^F^T^S.  ^THESE CONSTANTS ARE REFERRED
TO IN THE SEMANTICS FOR ^S^H^I^F^T^S BY ^P^A^R(0) AND ^P^A^R(3); THE FIRST IS THE OPERATION OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE (21 IS ^L^S^H) AND THE FOURTH IS THE CODE OF THE OPERATION FOR ^C^O^N^O^P.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OTHER TWO STATEMENTS DEFINE THE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS FOR TWO OTHER KINDS
OF SHIFTS.  ^THE SEMANTICS IS DEFINED
BY INVOKING ^S^H^I^F^T^S WITH DIFFERENT PARAMETER LISTS.  ^THE
SEMANTICS ARE THE SAME, BUT ^P^A^R(I) WILL REFER TO THE I-TH ELEMENT OF THE APPROPRIATE PARAMETER LIST.
.PARAGRAPH
^A CASE, SUCH AS ^S^H^I^F^T^S, MAY NOT BE DEFINED IN MORE THAN ONE PLACE.  ^IT IS
NOT NECESSARY TO HAVE PARAMETER LISTS BE THE SAME LENGTH FOR DIFFERENT INSTANCES
OF A CASE, BUT IT IS THE USER'S RESPONSIBILITY TO INSURE THAT PARAMETERS THAT
ARE NOT SUPPLIED ARE NOT REFERRED TO.  ^IN ANY EVENT, PARAMETER LISTS MUST ALWAYS
CONTAIN AT LEAST ONE CONSTANT.
.PARAGRAPH
^SEVERAL ^C^A^S^E SEMANTICS MAY APPEAR AS SOME OR ALL OF THE ALTERNATIVES IN A
SEMANTIC PART.  ^A ^C^A^S^E SEMANTICS MAY NOT BE THE SEMANTICS PART OF A CONDITIONAL
SEMANTICS ALTERNATIVE (I.E., "CONDITION"=>^C^A^S^E (...) ^O^F ^F^O^O IS ILLEGAL.)
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.4: ^THE ^V^A^L^U^E ^KLUDGE.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^V^A^L^U^E
.INDEX KLUDGE
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.4: ^THE ^V^A^L^U^E ^KLUDGE.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^OCCASIONALLY, IT IS NECESSARY TO REFER IN CONDITIONAL SEMANTICS TO AN ARGUMENT OF THE PRODUCTION WHICH CAN NOT BE WRITTEN AS AN IDENTIFIER.  ^THIS IS NOT
POSSIBLE USING THE MECHANISMS DEFINED SO FAR.  ^THE ^V^A^L^U^E KLUDGE IS A WAY TO
ACCOMPLISH THIS.  ^TAKE FOR EXAMPLE THE SYNTAX FOR CONDITIONALS.  ^RELATIONAL
OPERATORS ARE DEFINED AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP
.CENTER
<^R^E^L^O^P> ::= ^N^E  ::= ^V^A^L^U^E 6 ^O^F ^E^Q;
.CENTER
<^R^E^L^O^P> ::= '<' ::= ^V^A^L^U^E 1 ^O^F ^E^Q;
.SKIP
AND SO ON.  ^THE
SEMANTICS SPECIFIES THAT THESE PRODUCTIONS ARE SPECIAL CASES OF A
CLASS OF PRODUCTIONS LABELED ^E^Q, AND GIVES A 32-BIT VALUE TO BE ASSOCIATED
WITH THE PARTICULAR PRODUCTION.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN SYNTAX STATEMENTS INVOLVING THE CLASS <^R^E^L^O^P>, IT IS POSSIBLE TO SPECIFY
THAT ANY CASE OF ^E^Q MAY BE RECOGNIZED AS MATCHING A PARTICULAR ELEMENT IN A
SEMANTIC CONDITION.  ^THIS IS DONE AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -3
1. ^IN QUOTED UNCONDITIONAL SEMANTICS:  PREFIX THE QUOTED PART BY  ^E^Q/.
^THE CORRESPONDING INSTANCE OF ^R^E^L^O^P IN THE SYNTAX PART MUST BE
NAMED ^E^Q.  (^LAST LINE IN THE EXAMPLE BELOW).
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^IN SEMANTIC CONDITIONS:  ^PREFIX THE CONDITION BY ^E^Q/.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^IN SEMANTICS: ^THE ELEMENT
 MAY BE REFERRED TO IN QUOTED SEMANTICS BY PREFIXING THE QUOTED PART
BY ^E^Q/ AS IN (1).  ^IN SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS, THE ELEMENT MAY BE REFERENCED BY ^S^T^A^C^K(I), WHERE THE ELEMENT COMES TO THE RIGHT OF EXACTLY I
IDENTIFIERS IN THE CONDITION (OR, IN UNCONDITIONAL SEMANTICS, I ARGUMENTS OF THE
PRODUCTION).
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE PARTICULAR INSTANCE OF ^E^Q MAY BE DETERMINED BY REFERRING TO
^V^A^L(^S^T^A^C^K(I)), WITH I AS IN (2) ABOVE.  ^THE VALUE OF THIS SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALL
WILL BE THE VALUE SPECIFIED IN THE ^V^A^L^U^E SEMANTICS DEFINING THE INSTANCE OF
<^R^E^L^O^P> IN THE EXPRESSION BEING COMPILED.
.PARAGRAPH
^EXAMPLE:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 5
<^S^T>    ::= <^E^X^P,^A> <^R^E^L^O^P,^E^Q> <^E^X^P,^B> => <^S^T,^C> ::=
  ^E^Q/"^P=^R=>^G^O ^T^O ^S0^V^A^R"=>^C^J^U^M^P(^J^O^P(^V^A^L(^S^T^A^C^K(1))),^P,^R,^S) ^E^L^S^E
  ^L^O^C^A^L ^I^F ^I^N ^E^Q/"^N^O^T(^A=^B)=>^G^O ^T^O ^I^F; ^C; ^I^F: 0";
.FILL
.SUBTITLE 2.3.3.5: ^PRIORITY ^SEMANTICS.
.PAGE
.INDEX PRIORITY SEMANTICS
.CENTER
^&2.3.3.5: ^PRIORITY ^SEMANTICS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^USUALLY, THE SEMANTICS FOR AN EXPRESSION IS NOT EXECUTED UNTIL ENOUGH OF THE
CONTEXT OF THE EXPRESSION HAS BEEN PARSED IN ORDER TO DETERMINE THE PARTICULAR
SPECIAL CASE INVOLVED.  ^IF IT IS KNOWN THAT NO SPECIAL CASES INVOLVING SUBEXPRESSIONS OF A PRODUCTION EXIST, OR IF IT IS DESIRED FOR OTHER REASONS TO
DEFEAT THE SPECIAL CASE MATCHING MECHANISM AND INVOKE THE SEMANTICS FOR A
PRODUCTION IMMEDIATELY IT IS RECOGNIZED, THE ^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y SEMANTICS IS USED.
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y SEMANTICS IS A SEMANTIC PART CONSISTING ENTIRELY OF
.SKIP
.CENTER
^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y N S
.SKIP
WHERE N IS A DIGIT IN THE RANGE 0-7, AND S IS A SEMANTIC PART CONTAINING NO
CONDITIONAL SEMANTICS OR ^E^L^S^E ALTERNATIVES.
.PARAGRAPH
N IS INTERPRETED AS A THREE-BIT MASK, AND THE SEMANTICS S FOR THE PRODUCTION
IS EXECUTED IMMEDIATELY, BYPASSING THE SPECIAL CASE MATCHING PROCESS, IN THE
EVENT THAT THE CURRENT PRIORITY MASK OF THE MATCHING ROUTINE AND N HAVE ANY
BITS SET IN THE SAME POSITION.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE PRIORITY MASK OF THE MATCHING ROUTINE IS SET TO K BY THE SEMANTIC
ROUTINE ^S^E^T^P^R^I(K).  ^THE INTERPRETATIONS OF THE BITS ARE:
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1.  ^NORMALLY SET.  ^USED TO FORCE IMMEDIATE SEMANTIC EXECUTION UNDER NORMAL
CIRCUMSTANCES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2.  ^SET DURING INTERPRETATION OF DOUBLE-QUOTED EXPRESSIONS.  ^IN THIS MODE,
SEMANTICS ARE NOT INTERPRETED UNLESS THEY ARE ^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y 2.  ^THE OUTPUT
OF THE PARSER IS STORED INSTEAD, FOR USE AS DATA BY THE COMPILER.
^HOWEVER, ^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y 2 SEMANTICS ARE EXECUTED, PROVIDING A WAY TO TERMINATE THIS STATE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
4.  ^SET DURING SYNONYM PROCESSING (^^LET\\ STATEMENT).
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SUBTITLE 2.3.4: ^SYNTACTIC ^AMBIGUITY AND ^HOW TO ^MAKE IT ^WORK FOR ^YOU.
.PAGE
.INDEX SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY
.INDEX AMBIGUITY, SYNTACTIC
.CENTER
2.3.4: ^SYNTACTIC ^AMBIGUITY AND ^HOW TO ^MAKE IT ^WORK FOR ^YOU.
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^I^M^P72 PARSER ALLOWS AMBIGUITY IN THE SYNTAX.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, NOTICE THAT
IN MANY EXAMPLES ABOVE, WE PRESENT DIFFERENT DEFINITIONS OF THE CONSTRUCT
^A^B^S(^E).  ^THE SYNTAX ALLOWS TWO INTERPRETATIONS OF THIS CONSTRUCT: THE SPECIAL
CONSTRUCT WE ARE DEFINING, OR A CALL ON THE SUBROUTINE ^A^B^S.  ^THE PARSER NOTES
THE TWO INTERPRETATIONS, AND CHOOSES BETWEEN THEM, AS FOLLOWS:
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1. ^IF AN IDENTIFIER IS AN EXPRESSION IN ONE INTERPRETATION, AND A TERMINAL
SYMBOL (I.E., APPEARS AS ITSELF IN THE PRODUCTION, SUCH AS ^A^B^S IN THE
EXAMPLE OF ^SECTION 2.3.1), IN THE OTHER INTERPRETATION,
THE INTERPRETATION WITH THE TERMINAL SYMBOL IS CHOSEN.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^OTHERWISE, THE INTERPRETATION INVOLVING THE SMALLEST NUMBER OF DIFFERENT
SYNTAX RULES IS CHOSEN.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^OTHERWISE, THE COMPILER MAKES AN ARBITRARY CHOICE.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THUS, REFERRING TO THE EXAMPLE IN ^SECTION 2.3.3.2 OF THE CONSTRUCT
/VARIABLE/, THE SPECIAL CASE /VARIABLE/:=EXPRESSION WAS DEFINED BY A SPECIAL CASE
IN THE SEMANTICS.  ^IT WOULD ALTERNATIVELY BE POSSIBLE TO DEFINE THE CASE BY
INTRODUCING A DELIBERATE SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY, AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
<^V^B^L> ::= / <^V^B^L,^A> /
                    ::= "^A<32,0>";
<^E^X^P>  ::= / <^V^B^L,^A> / ':=' <^E^X^P,^B>
                    ::= "^A<32,0>:=^B"
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE EXPRESSION /^X/:=5, E.G., COULD BE RECOGNIZED IN TWO WAYS, BY USING THE FIRST
SYNTAX STATEMENT ABOVE PLUS THE RULE FOR <^V^A^R>:=<^E^X^P>, OR BY USING THE SECOND
SYNTAX STATEMENT ABOVE.  ^BUT ACCORDING TO RULE 2, THE PARSER CHOOSES THE SECOND
INTERPRETATION, AS INVOLVING ONE SYNTAX RULE AS OPPOSED TO TWO RULES FOR THE
FIRST INTERPRETATION.  ^THIS IS PRECISELY THE CHOICE THAT IS DESIRED.
.SUBTITLE 2.3.5: ^PEACEFUL ^CO-^EXISTENCE WITH ^YOUR ^EXTENSIBLE ^COMPILER.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&2.3.5: ^PEACEFUL ^CO-^EXISTENCE WITH ^YOUR ^EXTENSIBLE ^COMPILER.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS SECTION PRESENTS THE RESULTS OF SOME EXPERIENCE WITH SYNTAX AND SEMANTIC DEFINITION IN ^I^M^P72, IN ORDER TO HELP OTHERS AVOID SOME PROBLEMS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE PARSER WORKS BY CARRYING ALONG ALL POSSIBLE PARSES OF THE PROGRAM UP
TO THE SYMBOL IT IS READING AT THE MOMENT.  ^IT USUALLY MANAGES TO RESOLVE ALL
AMBIGUITY EVERY COUPLE OF SYMBOLS AT MOST.  ^IF A SYNTACTIC PRODUCTION IS ENTERED
WHICH REQUIRES IT TO READ A LARGE NUMBER OF SYMBOLS BEFORE IT CAN CHOOSE WHICH
SYNTAX RULE IT IS FOLLOWING, THE PARSER MAY REQUIRE INORDINATE AMOUNTS OF SPACE
AND TIME.  ^THIS CONDITION MAY BE DIAGNOSED BY LOOKING AT THE COMPILER STATISTICS
AT THE BOTTOM OF THE SOURCE PROGRAM LISTING.  ^IF THE ^MAX. ^PARSE ^SPACE GOES
OVER 1500+500, OR THE ^MAX. ^OUTPUT ^SPACE IS OVER 150, THE CONDITION MAY
EXIST.
.PARAGRAPH
^AN EXAMPLE OF SYNTAX WHICH MAY CAUSE THE PROBLEM IS:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
<^E^X^P> ::= <^E^X^P,^A> => <^S^T,^B>;
<^E^X^P> ::= <^E^X^P,^A> => <^E^X^P,^B> ^E^L^S^E <^S^T,^C>
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.FILL
.SKIP
^WHEN THE COMPILER SEES ^A=>(.......), IT IS NOT ABLE TO DECIDE UNTIL THE RIGHT
PARENTHESIS WHETHER IT IS TRYING TO FORM AN ^S^T OR AN ^E^X^P.  ^THUS IT MUST CARRY
ALONG TWO PARSES WHILE IT IS PARSING THE ENTIRE CONTENTS OF THE PARENTHESES,
WHICH MAY BE AN ARBITRARILY LONG EXPRESSION.  ^THE SYNTAX ^I^M^P USES IS
.SKIP
.NOFILL
          <^E^X^P> ::= <^E^X^P,^A> => <^S^T,^B>;
          <^E^X^P> ::= <^E^X^P,^A> => <^S^T,^B> ^E^L^S^E <^S^T,^C>
.FILL
.SKIP
WHICH AVOIDS THE PARSER PROBLEM (ALTHOUGH IT INTRODUCES THE NECESSITY FOR PARENTHESIZING THE ^E^L^S^E CLAUSE).  ^THE METHOD FOR STORING SYNTAX RULES COMBINES ALL
RULES WHOSE RIGHT-HAND PARTS BEGIN IDENTICALLY, UP TO THE POINT AT WHICH THEY
DIFFER.  ^THUS, THE PARSER STORES THESE TWO RULES AS:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
                        =:: <^E^X^P>
                       /
      <^E^X^P,^A> => <^S^T,^B>
                       ^\
                        ^E^L^S^E <^S^T,^C> =:: <^E^X^P>
.SKIP
.FILL
AND ONLY CARRIES ALONG ONE PARSE UNTIL IT HAS FINISHED PARSING THE <^S^T,^B>.
.INDEX SPECIAL CASES, ORDER OF RECOGNIZING
.PARAGRAPH
^ANOTHER DIFFICULTY WHICH MAY ARISE IS THAT SPECIAL SEMANTIC CASES MAY BE
SPECIFIED IN A SYNTAX STATEMENT, BUT THE COMPILER WILL REFUSE TO RECOGNIZE THEM.
^THIS MAY ARISE FROM THE WAY IN WHICH THE PATTERNS FOR SPECIAL CASES ARE RECOGNIZED.  ^SEMANTICS ARE PERFORMED FOR SUBEXPRESSIONS OF A SYNTAX PRODUCTION FROM
LEFT TO RIGHT.  ^FOR AN ELEMENT IN A SYNTACTIC CONDITIONAL TO BE RECOGNIZED, IT
MUST ALREADY HAVE BEEN REDUCED BY SEMANTICS TO ONE OBJECT.  ^AN EXAMPLE WILL
ILLUSTRATE THE PITFALL:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
       <^E^X^P> ::= <^A^T^O^M,^A> ^N^E <^E^X^P,^B> ::= .....;
       <^S^T>  ::= <^E^X^P,^A> => <^S^T,^B> ::=
              "^A ^N^E 0=>^B" => ..... ^E^L^S^E .....
.FILL
.SKIP
WHERE THE ..... REPRESENTS SEMANTICS WHOSE EXACT FORM IS NONESSENTIAL TO THE
POINT BEING ILLUSTRATED.  ^IF THE STATEMENT
.SKIP
.CENTER
^A ^N^E 0=>^X:=^X+4
.SKIP
IS ENCOUNTERED BY THE COMPILER, AN ATTEMPT TO MATCH THE SPECIAL CASE "^A ^N^E 0=>^B"
IS MADE, BUT FAILS SINCE ^X:=^X+4 IS NOT A SINGLE OBJECT; THE SEMANTICS FOR := AND +
HAVE NOT YET BEEN PERFORMED.  ^SINCE THE SPECIAL CASE FAILS, THE SEMANTICS FOR
THE SUBEXPRESSIONS OF THE STATEMENT ARE INVOKED, FROM LEFT TO RIGHT.  ^FIRST, THE
SEMANTICS FOR ^A ^N^E ^B IS PERFORMED.  ^CHECKS ARE MADE AT EVERY STEP TO SEE IF THE
CURRENT EXPRESSION MATCHES SOME SPECIAL CASE, BUT NOW ^A ^N^E 0 IS A SINGLE OBJECT,
AND WILL ONLY MATCH PATTERNS OF THE FORM "^X=>...".  ^THUS THE COMPILER WILL FAIL
TO RECOGNIZE THE SPECIAL CASE "^A ^N^E 0=>^B".
.PARAGRAPH
^IT IS IN ORDER TO AVOID THIS PROBLEM THAT ^I^M^P HAS SEPARATE PRODUCTIONS FOR
^A=>^B AND ^A <^R^E^L^O^P> ^B=>^C (WHERE <^R^E^L^O^P> IS THE CLASS OF RELATIONAL OPERATORS).
.SUBTITLE 2.4:  ^CALLING ^C AND ^BEING ^CALLED FROM ^C.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^C ^I^N^T^E^R^F^A^C^E
.CENTER
^&2.4:  ^CALLING ^C AND ^BEING ^CALLED FROM ^C\&
.SKIP 2
.PARAGRAPH
^AN ^I^M^P ROUTINE IS A FUNCTION OF THE ^[C^] CALLING CONVENTION OF THE SYSTEM, AND A ^[C^] FUNCTION CAN BE CALLED
FROM ^I^M^P.  ^THE FILES ARE IN THE OBJECT FORMAT OF THE SYSTEM (^E^L^F OR ^MACH-^O), SO THAT THEY LINK WITH
^C OBJECTS AND LIBRARIES.  ^THE RUNTIME LIBRARY OF ^I^M^P PROGRAMS, ^[libimp72.a^], MUST BE LINKED WITH THEM.
.SUBTITLE 2.4.1: ^CALLING ^C: ^C^C^A^L^L.
.INDEX ^C^C^A^L^L
.CENTER
^&2.4.1: ^CALLING ^C: ^C^C^A^L^L.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^[CCALL NAME(A,B,...)^] CALLS THE ^[C^] FUNCTION ^[NAME^] PASSING THE VALUES OF ITS ARGUMENTS, AS ^C DOES, AND HAS THE
VALUE THAT THE FUNCTION RETURNS. ^AN ORDINARY CALL ^[NAME(A,B)^] OF AN ^I^M^P ROUTINE PASSES ADDRESSES; A CALL OF A ^C
FUNCTION IS ALWAYS WRITTEN WITH ^C^C^A^L^L. ^THE ARGUMENTS GO IN THE REGISTERS AND ON THE STACK THAT THE ^[C^] ^A^B^I
OF THE SYSTEM USES, SO THAT A CALL MAY HAVE MORE THAN SIX (SIXTEEN AT MOST). ^A FUNCTION THAT IS NOT DECLARED (SEE
BELOW) IS CALLED WITH A FIXED NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS, EACH ONE WORD (TYPE ^[long^]): THE ARGUMENTS ARE INTEGERS OR POINTERS,
AND THE RESULT IS THE INTEGER OF A POINTER'S SIZE. ^A ^C FUNCTION THAT TAKES A VARIABLE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS (^[printf^],
^[snprintf^], ^[open^], ^[fcntl^], ^[ioctl^], ^[syscall^]) MUST BE DECLARED WITH ^[...^], BECAUSE THE ^A^B^I OF SEVERAL
SYSTEMS CALLS A VARIADIC FUNCTION DIFFERENTLY FROM A FIXED ONE. ^THE ADDRESS OF A VARIABLE OR OF A STRING CONSTANT, WHICH A ^[C^] FUNCTION EXPECTS FOR A POINTER,
IS WRITTEN ^L^O^C(^X) OR ^L^O^C('TEXT'): A STRING CONSTANT IS BYTES ENDED BY A ZERO BYTE. ^FOR EXAMPLE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^N:=^C^C^A^L^L STRLEN(^L^O^C('HELLO, WORLD'));
^P^R^I^N^T ^S^T^G 0,'LENGTH ',^I^G^R 0,^N,/;
^C^C^A^L^L PUTS(^L^O^C('PUTS ADDS A NEWLINE'));
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
CALL ^[strlen^] AND ^[puts^]. (AFTER ^[EXTERNAL INT printf(PTR, ...);^], ^[CCALL printf(LOC('%ld'),N)^] PRINTS WITH THE FORMATS OF ^C.) ^A ^C ^[int^] RESULT OF
A FUNCTION THAT IS NOT DECLARED COMES BACK IN HALF A REGISTER, SO THAT ITS UPPER BITS ARE UNDEFINED: MASK IT WITH
^[AND 4294967295^] BEFORE COMPARING IT WITH A NEGATIVE NUMBER. ^A FUNCTION THAT IS NOT DECLARED CANNOT TAKE OR RETURN
A ^[double^] OR A ^[float^]. ^A DECLARATION REMOVES THESE LIMITS.
.PARAGRAPH
^A DECLARATION GIVES THE TYPES OF A ^C FUNCTION ONCE, AS A STATEMENT:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PUTS(^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^D^O^U^B^L^E LDEXP(^D^O^U^B^L^E, ^I^N^T);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^P^T^R MALLOC(^S^I^Z^E);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^V^O^I^D FREE(^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PRINTF(^P^T^R, ...);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^L^O^N^G WRITE(^I^N^T, ^P^T^R, ^S^I^Z^E);
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE TYPES ARE THOSE OF ^C, WRITTEN IN CAPITALS (NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE):
^[CHAR UCHAR SHORT USHORT INT UINT LONG ULONG LLONG ULLONG SIZE PTR DOUBLE FLOAT^], AND ^[VOID^] FOR A RESULT OR AS
THE WHOLE LIST OF PARAMETERS (^[(VOID)^] IS NO PARAMETER). ^[LONG^], ^[ULONG^] AND ^[SIZE^] HAVE THE WIDTH OF A ^C
^[long^], WHICH IS 32 BITS WHERE POINTERS ARE 32 BITS; ^[LLONG^] AND ^[ULLONG^] ARE 64 BITS EVERYWHERE, AND ^[PTR^] IS
THE WIDTH OF A POINTER. ^THE NAME IS THE ^C NAME AS WRITTEN; A NAME THAT IS NOT AN ^I^M^P NAME IS A SPECIAL IDENTIFIER
(^SECTION 2.2.1). ^THREE DOTS AFTER THE LAST TYPE DECLARE A FUNCTION OF A VARIABLE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS. ^A DECLARATION
HOLDS TO THE END OF THE FILE, AND A FUNCTION THAT SEVERAL FILES CALL IS DECLARED IN EACH. ^A FUNCTION DECLARED TWICE
WITH DIFFERENT TYPES, DECLARED AFTER A CALL OF IT THAT HAD NO DECLARATION, OR DECLARED WITH A TYPE THAT IS NOT ONE OF
THOSE, IS AN ERROR.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN A CALL ^[CCALL NAME(ARGS)^] OF A DECLARED FUNCTION THE COMPILER COUNTS THE ARGUMENTS (A WRONG NUMBER IS AN ERROR,
AND A VARIADIC FUNCTION TAKES AT LEAST THE DECLARED ONES) AND CONVERTS EACH AS ^C DOES. ^AN INTEGER IS TRUNCATED OR
EXTENDED TO THE TYPE OF THE PARAMETER. ^A ^[PTR^] TAKES A WORD AS ITS VALUE, OR A STRING CONSTANT DIRECTLY:
^[CCALL puts('hello')^] PASSES THE ADDRESS OF THE BYTES, ENDED BY A ZERO BYTE, WITH NO ^L^O^C. ^A ^[DOUBLE^] OR A
^[FLOAT^] TAKES A ^R^E^A^L, WHICH IS THE BITS OF A DOUBLE (^SECTION 2.4.3), OR AN INTEGER, WHICH IS CONVERTED TO REAL;
A ^R^E^A^L GIVEN TO AN INTEGER TYPE OR A ^[PTR^] IS CONVERTED TO AN INTEGER. ^THE ARGUMENTS OF A VARIADIC CALL THAT
HAVE NO DECLARED TYPE GET THE PROMOTIONS OF ^C: A ^R^E^A^L IS PASSED AS A ^[double^], AND AN INTEGER AS A ^[long^];
THE ADDRESS OF A STRING CONSTANT THERE IS WRITTEN ^L^O^C('TEXT'). ^THE RESULT IS THE WORD THE PROGRAM SEES: THE SIGNED
TYPES ARE SIGN-EXTENDED, THE UNSIGNED TYPES AND ^[PTR^] ARE ZERO-EXTENDED, A ^[DOUBLE^] OR A ^[FLOAT^] IS A ^R^E^A^L,
AND ^[VOID^] GIVES 0. ^A FUNCTION DECLARED ^[DOUBLE^] OR ^[FLOAT^] HAS A ^R^E^A^L AS ITS VALUE, WHICH MIXES WITH OTHER
REALS; A VARIABLE OR A FUNCTION THAT HOLDS ONE IS DECLARED ^R^E^A^L. ^FOR EXAMPLE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PUTS(^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PRINTF(^P^T^R, ...);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^D^O^U^B^L^E LDEXP(^D^O^U^B^L^E, ^I^N^T);
^X ^I^S ^R^E^A^L;
^C^C^A^L^L PUTS('LDEXP SCALES BY A POWER OF TWO');
^X:=^C^C^A^L^L LDEXP(1.5,4);
^C^C^A^L^L PRINTF('%.1F%C',^X,10);
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[ldexp scales by a power of two^] AND ^[24.0^]. ^[CCALL puts('a','b')^] IS AN ERROR:
^[CCALL puts TAKES 1 ARGUMENTS, NOT 2.^] ^THE COMPILER LINKS THE ^C LIBRARY AND, WHERE THE SYSTEM KEEPS THE MATH FUNCTIONS IN A LIBRARY OF THEIR OWN (^[libm^] OF
GLIBC), THAT LIBRARY TOO, SO THAT ^[sqrt^], ^[pow^] AND ^[sin^] CAN BE DECLARED AND CALLED.
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^[C^] FUNCTION MAY CALL AN ^I^M^P ROUTINE BACK: ^[LOC(ROUTINE)^] IS ITS ADDRESS.  ^AN ^I^M^P ROUTINE RECEIVES ITS
ARGUMENTS AS ADDRESSES, WHICH IS WHAT ^[qsort^] GIVES ITS COMPARISON FUNCTION:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^V ^I^S 6 ^L^O^N^G;
^V[0]:=5; ^V[1]:=2; ^V[2]:=9; ^V[3]:=1; ^V[4]:=7; ^V[5]:=3;
^C^C^A^L^L QSORT(^L^O^C(^V),6,8,^L^O^C(^C^M^P));
(^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 3,^V[^I]) ^F^O^R ^I ^T^O 5;
^P^R^I^N^T /;
^F^I^N^I(0);
^S^U^B^R ^C^M^P(^A,^B) ^I^S ^A-^B
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
SORTS THE ARRAY AND PRINTS ^[  1  2  3  5  7  9^].  ^A ROUTINE THAT A ^[C^] FUNCTION
CALLS WITH ARGUMENTS BY VALUE (A SIGNAL HANDLER) RECEIVES EACH VALUE AS THE ADDRESS IN ITS
PARAMETER: ^L^O^C(^A) IS THE VALUE THAT ^C PASSED.  ^TO CALL A FUNCTION THROUGH AN ADDRESS, ^[CCALL impcallp(F,A,...)^] CALLS THE
FUNCTION AT ^F WITH THE ARGUMENTS BY VALUE AND HAS THE RESULT AS A ^C ^[long^], AND ^[CCALL impcallw(F,A,...)^]
HAS THE RESULT AS AN ^I^M^P WORD OF 64 BITS; ^[CCALL impcallw(LOC(TWICE),LOC(Y))^] CALLS THE ^I^M^P ROUTINE ^T^W^I^C^E WITH THE ADDRESS OF ^Y.
.SUBTITLE 2.4.2: ^BEING ^CALLED FROM ^C.
.INDEX ^C, ^C^A^L^L^I^N^G ^I^M^P ^F^R^O^M
.CENTER
^&2.4.2: ^BEING ^CALLED FROM ^C.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^S^U^B^R IS A ^[C^] FUNCTION THAT TAKES POINTERS TO 64-BIT WORDS AND RETURNS A 64-BIT INTEGER.  ^[SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B^]
IS DECLARED IN ^[C^] AS ^[int64_t ADD2(int64_t *, int64_t *)^].  ^THE HEADER ^[imp72.h^], INSTALLED IN THE ^[include^] DIRECTORY,
HAS THE ^[C^] TYPES OF THE WORDS.  ^THE SYMBOL IS THE NAME AS WRITTEN; THE ROUTINE IS CALLED WITH THE ADDRESS OF EACH ARGUMENT.
^FOR EXAMPLE, WITH THE FILE ^[lib.imp^]
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^C^O^U^N^T ^I^S ^C^O^M^M^O^N;
^T^A^B^L^E ^I^S ^C^O^M^M^O^N,4 ^L^O^N^G;
^S^U^B^R ^A^D^D2(^A,^B) ^I^S ^A+^B;
^S^U^B^R ^B^U^M^P(^N) ^I^S (^C^O^U^N^T:=^C^O^U^N^T+^N; ^C^O^U^N^T);
^S^U^B^R ^G^E^T^T(^I) ^I^S ^T^A^B^L^E[^I]
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
AND THE ^[C^] PROGRAM ^[main.c^]
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
_#INCLUDE <STDIO.H>
_#INCLUDE <STDINT.H>
EXTERN INT64__T ^C^O^U^N^T;
EXTERN INT64__T ^T^A^B^L^E[4];
INT64__T ^A^D^D2(INT64__T *, INT64__T *);
INT64__T ^B^U^M^P(INT64__T *);
INT64__T ^G^E^T^T(INT64__T *);
INT MAIN(VOID) {
    INT64__T A = 3, B = 4, N = 10, I = 2;
    PRINTF("%LLD_\N", (LONG LONG)^A^D^D2(_&A, _&B));
    ^B^U^M^P(_&N); ^B^U^M^P(_&N);
    PRINTF("%LLD_\N", (LONG LONG)^C^O^U^N^T);
    ^T^A^B^L^E[2] = 7;
    PRINTF("%LLD_\N", (LONG LONG)^G^E^T^T(_&I));
    RETURN 0;
}
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
THE COMMANDS ^[imp72 -c lib.imp^] AND ^[cc -no-pie -o m main.c lib.o libimp72.a^] MAKE A PROGRAM THAT PRINTS 7, 20 AND 7
(^[-no-pie^] IS FOR A ^[C^] COMPILER THAT MAKES POSITION-INDEPENDENT EXECUTABLES BY DEFAULT).
^A FILE WITH NO MAIN PROGRAM (NO STATEMENTS BEFORE THE FIRST ^S^U^B^R) DEFINES NO ^[impmain^], SO THAT IT IS
LINKED INTO A ^[C^] PROGRAM BUT NOT INTO A PROGRAM THAT USES THE MAIN OF THE LIBRARY (^SECTION 2.4.4).
.SUBTITLE 2.4.3: ^C^O^M^M^O^N AND ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^VARIABLES.
.INDEX ^C^O^M^M^O^N ^V^A^R^I^A^B^L^E^S
.CENTER
^&2.4.3: ^C^O^M^M^O^N AND ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^VARIABLES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^A VARIABLE DECLARED ^C^O^M^M^O^N IS A GLOBAL SYMBOL OF THE OBJECT FILE, WITH THE NAME AS WRITTEN, SO THAT
^[C^] DECLARES IT ^[extern int64_t X;^] (AN ARRAY ^[A IS COMMON,8 LONG^] IS ^[extern int64_t A[8];^]).  ^EVERY
FILE OF THE PROGRAM THAT USES IT DECLARES IT ^C^O^M^M^O^N.  ^A VARIABLE THAT IS NOT ^C^O^M^M^O^N BELONGS TO ITS FILE AND ^[C^] CANNOT NAME IT.
^A ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLE THAT NO ROUTINE OF A FILE USES IS NOT MADE BY THAT FILE, AS THE EXAMPLE ABOVE USES ^T^A^B^L^E IN ^G^E^T^T.
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^R^E^A^L IS AN ^I^E^E^E 754 DOUBLE IN A WORD THAT IS THE DOUBLE'S OWN BITS, SO THAT ^[C^] DECLARES A
^[COMMON REAL^] AS ^[extern double X;^].  ^[C^] CODE THAT HANDLES THE WORD OF A ^R^E^A^L AS AN INTEGER CONVERTS IT WITH
^[imp_real_to_double^] AND ^[imp_double_to_real^] OF ^[imp72.h^].
.PARAGRAPH
^[X IS EXTERNAL^] DECLARES A VARIABLE THAT IS DEFINED ELSEWHERE, IN THE ^[C^] LIBRARY FOR EXAMPLE.  ^NOTHING IS DEFINED IN
THE FILE, AND ^L^O^C(^X) IS THE ADDRESS OF THE VARIABLE THAT THE LINKER FINDS.  ^THE NAMES THAT ARE NOT ^I^M^P NAMES ARE WRITTEN AS
SPECIAL IDENTIFIERS (^SECTION 2.2.1):
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
!ENVIRON! ^I^S ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L;
^P^R^I^N^T ^S^T^G 0,'ENVIRON IS SET: ',^I^G^R 0,(!ENVIRON! ^N^E 0),/;
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[environ is set: -1^].  ^A ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLE OR A ^S^U^B^R WITH THE NAME ^[!a-b!^] IS THE SYMBOL ^[a_2db^]: A
BYTE THAT IS NOT A LETTER, A DIGIT OR AN UNDERSCORE IS WRITTEN AS AN UNDERSCORE AND TWO HEXADECIMAL DIGITS.
.SUBTITLE 2.4.4: ^NAMES, ^PROGRAMS AND ^COMMAND ^LINE.
.CENTER
^&2.4.4: ^NAMES, ^PROGRAMS AND ^COMMAND ^LINE.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE AND A SYMBOL IS THE NAME AS WRITTEN; ^[SUBR Add^] AND ^[SUBR ADD^] ARE TWO FUNCTIONS.  ^THE
^I^M^P NAMES ARE THE NAMES OF ^C (LETTERS, DIGITS AND UNDERSCORES), AND THE SYMBOLS OF THE RUNTIME
LIBRARY AND THE COMPILER ARE LOWER CASE (^[impmain^], ^[imppr^], ^[imprd^]) OR BEGIN WITH ^[RTL^]; A PROGRAM SHOULD NOT
USE THEM FOR ITS OWN.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE STATEMENTS BEFORE THE FIRST ^S^U^B^R OF A FILE ARE THE FUNCTION ^[impmain^] OF THAT FILE.  ^THE LIBRARY HAS A ^[main^] THAT
CALLS ^[impmain^] WITH THE COMMAND LINE KEPT FOR THE PROGRAM.  ^A PROGRAM WITH A ^[main^] OF ITS OWN (IN ^[C^], OR A ^S^U^B^R CALLED ^[main^])
DOES NOT LINK THE ^[main^] OF THE LIBRARY, AND CALLS ^[impmain^] ITSELF IF IT HAS ONE.
^THE COMMAND LINE IS READ WITH ^[CCALL impargc(0)^], THE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS, AND ^[CCALL impargvp(0)^], THE ADDRESS OF THE
ARRAY OF POINTERS; THE ROUTINE ^I^M^P^A^R^G^E^T(^O^U^T,^V,^I) STORES ELEMENT ^I OF SUCH AN ARRAY IN THE WORD AT ^O^U^T, SO THAT
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^N:=^C^C^A^L^L IMPARGC(0);
^I^M^P^A^R^G^E^T(^L^O^C(^W),^C^C^A^L^L IMPARGVP(0),0);
^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,^N,^S^T^G 0,[^W],/;
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS THE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS AND THE PROGRAM'S NAME.  ^EXACTLY ONE OF THE FILES OF A PROGRAM HAS STATEMENTS BEFORE ITS FIRST ^S^U^B^R.
.SUBTITLE 2.4.5: ^CALLBACKS AND ^FUNCTION ^POINTERS.
.INDEX ^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K
.INDEX ^F^N^P^T^R
.CENTER
^&2.4.5: ^CALLBACKS AND ^FUNCTION ^POINTERS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^[C^] FUNCTION THAT TAKES A FUNCTION POINTER (^[qsort^], ^[bsearch^], ^[signal^], ^[atexit^]) CALLS IT WITH THE
ARGUMENTS OF ^[C^], BY VALUE AND WITH THE TYPES OF ^[C^]; AN ^[IMP^] ROUTINE TAKES ADDRESSES. ^A ^[CALLBACK^]
DECLARATION GIVES A ^[SUBR^] THE SIGNATURE OF THE CALL, AND ^[CALLBACK(NAME)^] IS THE ADDRESS OF A ^[C^] FUNCTION THAT
MAKES THE CALL:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K ^I^N^T ^C^M^P(^P^T^R, ^P^T^R);
^V ^I^S 6 ^L^O^N^G;
^V[0]:=5; ^V[1]:=2; ^V[2]:=9; ^V[3]:=1; ^V[4]:=7; ^V[5]:=3;
^C^C^A^L^L QSORT(^L^O^C(^V),6,8,^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K(^C^M^P));
(^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 3,^V[^I]) ^F^O^R ^I ^T^O 5;
^P^R^I^N^T /;
^F^I^N^I(0);
^S^U^B^R ^C^M^P(^A,^B) ^I^S (
  [^A]<[^B] => ^R^E^T^U^R^N 0-1;
  [^A]=[^B] => ^R^E^T^U^R^N 0;
  1)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
SORTS THE ARRAY AND PRINTS THE NUMBERS 1 2 3 5 7 9, EACH IN A FIELD OF THREE. ^THE DECLARATION HAS THE FORM
^[CALLBACK result NAME(types)^] WITH THE RESULT AND THE TYPES OF ^SECTION 2.4.1 (^[CHAR^] TO ^[FLOAT^], ^[VOID^] FOR
THE RESULT OR AS THE WHOLE LIST OF PARAMETERS); IT COMES BEFORE THE ^[SUBR^], AND A ^[SUBR^] WITH ANOTHER NUMBER OF
PARAMETERS THAN THE DECLARATION IS AN ERROR. ^EACH PARAMETER REACHES THE ROUTINE AS A WORD IN A VARIABLE OF ITS OWN,
CONVERTED AS THE RESULT OF A DECLARED FUNCTION IS: A SIGNED TYPE IS SIGN-EXTENDED, AN UNSIGNED TYPE AND ^[PTR^] ARE
ZERO-EXTENDED, ^[DOUBLE^] AND ^[FLOAT^] ARE A ^R^E^A^L. ^A PARAMETER DECLARED ^[PTR^] IS THE ADDRESS THAT ^[C^]
PASSED, SO ^[A^] IS THE ADDRESS OF AN ELEMENT OF THE ARRAY AND ^[[A]^] IS THE ELEMENT. ^THE VALUE OF THE ROUTINE IS
CONVERTED TO THE TYPE OF THE RESULT: AN INTEGER TYPE TAKES THE LOW BITS OF THE WORD (^[RETURN 0-1^] IS THE ^[int^]
-1), ^[DOUBLE^] AND ^[FLOAT^] TAKE A ^R^E^A^L, AND ^[VOID^] IGNORES IT. ^[CALLBACK(NAME)^] OF A NAME THAT IS NOT
DECLARED A CALLBACK IS AN ERROR, AND SO IS ^[CCALL NAME(...)^] OF ONE: A CALLBACK IS CALLED WITHOUT ^[CCALL^], WITH
ADDRESSES, AS ANY ^[SUBR^] IS.
.PARAGRAPH
^A CALLBACK IS A THUNK THAT THE COMPILER WRITES INTO THE OBJECT FILE OF THE FIRST ^[CALLBACK(NAME)^] IN IT; THE
ROUTINE ITSELF CAN BE IN ANOTHER FILE. ^THE VARIABLES OF AN ^[IMP^] ROUTINE ARE STATIC, SO A CALLBACK THAT IS ENTERED
AGAIN WHILE IT RUNS (A SIGNAL HANDLER THAT INTERRUPTS THE SAME ROUTINE, A COMPARISON THAT SORTS) SHARES ITS VARIABLES
WITH THE FIRST ENTRY. ^FOR EXAMPLE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K ^V^O^I^D ^C^A^T^C^H(^I^N^T);
^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K ^V^O^I^D ^B^Y^E(^V^O^I^D);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^P^T^R SIGNAL(^I^N^T, ^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T RAISE(^I^N^T);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T ATEXIT(^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PUTS(^P^T^R);
^C^C^A^L^L SIGNAL(2,^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K(^C^A^T^C^H));
^C^C^A^L^L ATEXIT(^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K(^B^Y^E));
^C^C^A^L^L RAISE(2);
^C^C^A^L^L PUTS('AFTER THE SIGNAL');
^F^I^N^I(0);
^S^U^B^R ^C^A^T^C^H(^S) ^I^S ^C^C^A^L^L PUTS('CAUGHT A SIGNAL');
^S^U^B^R ^B^Y^E() ^I^S ^C^C^A^L^L PUTS('BYE')
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[caught a signal^], ^[after the signal^] AND ^[bye^].
.PARAGRAPH
^TO CALL A ^[C^] FUNCTION THROUGH A POINTER THAT THE PROGRAM HAS (FROM ^[dlsym^], FROM A STRUCTURE, OR A
^[CALLBACK(NAME)^]), ^[FNPTR result NAME(types)^] DECLARES THE SIGNATURE, AND ^[CCALL NAME(F, A, ...)^] CALLS THE
FUNCTION AT THE ADDRESS ^[F^] WITH THE ARGUMENTS ^[A^], ... CONVERTED TO THE TYPES AS FOR A DECLARED FUNCTION. ^THE
FUNCTION IS THE FIRST ARGUMENT AND COUNTS: A DECLARATION OF ONE TYPE TAKES TWO ARGUMENTS. ^THREE DOTS AFTER THE LAST
TYPE DECLARE A FUNCTION OF A VARIABLE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS. ^FOR EXAMPLE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K ^D^O^U^B^L^E ^H^A^L^V^E(^D^O^U^B^L^E);
^F^N^P^T^R ^D^O^U^B^L^E ^H^A^L^V^E^C^A^L^L(^D^O^U^B^L^E);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^I^N^T PRINTF(^P^T^R, ...);
^X ^I^S ^R^E^A^L;
^X:=^C^C^A^L^L ^H^A^L^V^E^C^A^L^L(^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K(^H^A^L^V^E),5.0);
^C^C^A^L^L PRINTF('%.2F%C',^X,10);
^F^I^N^I(0);
^S^U^B^R ^H^A^L^V^E(^A) ^I^S (
  ^Y ^I^S ^R^E^A^L;
  ^Y:=^A;
  ^Y:=^Y/2.0;
  ^Y)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[2.50^]. ^A CALLBACK HAS AT MOST 16 PARAMETERS AND IS NOT VARIADIC; A STRUCTURE BY VALUE IS NOT A TYPE (^SECTION 2.4.6 HAS THE LAYOUTS OF STRUCTURES IN MEMORY).
.SUBTITLE 2.4.6: ^STRUCTURE ^LAYOUTS.
.INDEX ^S^T^R^U^C^T
.INDEX ^F^I^E^L^D
.INDEX ^S^I^Z^E^O^F
.INDEX ^O^F^F^S^E^T^O^F
.CENTER
^&2.4.6: ^STRUCTURE ^LAYOUTS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^A ^C STRUCTURE IS MEMORY AT AN ADDRESS. ^A ^[STRUCT^] STATEMENT DECLARES ITS LAYOUT, THE NAMES AND THE ^C TYPES OF ITS FIELDS, AND ^[FIELD(P, NAME.FIELD)^] READS AND WRITES A FIELD OF THE STRUCTURE AT THE ADDRESS ^[P^] WITH THE TYPE AND AT THE OFFSET THAT ^C GIVES IT, IN PLACE OF BYTE OFFSETS WRITTEN BY HAND:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^S^T^R^U^C^T ^P^A^I^R (^L^O^N^G ^S^E^C, ^L^O^N^G ^N^S^E^C);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^P^T^R CALLOC(^S^I^Z^E, ^S^I^Z^E);
^T:=^C^C^A^L^L CALLOC(1,^S^I^Z^E^O^F(^P^A^I^R));
^F^I^E^L^D(^T,^P^A^I^R.^S^E^C):=5;
^F^I^E^L^D(^T,^P^A^I^R.^N^S^E^C):=0-7;
^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,^F^I^E^L^D(^T,^P^A^I^R.^S^E^C),^S^T^G 0,' ',^I^G^R 0,^F^I^E^L^D(^T,^P^A^I^R.^N^S^E^C),^S^T^G 0,' ',^I^G^R 0,^O^F^F^S^E^T^O^F(^P^A^I^R,^N^S^E^C),/;
^F^I^N^I(0)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[5 -7 8^]. ^THE 8 IS THE OFFSET OF ^[NSEC^]: A ^[long^] IS 8 BYTES ON A MACHINE WHOSE LONGS ARE 64 BITS AND 4 BYTES ON ONE WHOSE LONGS ARE 32 BITS, AND THE LAYOUT IS THAT OF THE MACHINE THE PROGRAM IS COMPILED FOR (^[--target^] INCLUDED).
.PARAGRAPH
^THE DECLARATION IS ^[STRUCT name (type field, type field, ...)^]. ^A TYPE IS ONE OF THE TYPES OF SECTION 2.4.1 (^[CHAR^] TO ^[FLOAT^], NOT ^[VOID^]) OR A STRUCTURE DECLARED EARLIER IN THE SAME FILE, WHICH MAKES THE FIELD A NESTED STRUCTURE. ^[type field[N]^] DECLARES AN ARRAY OF ^[N^] ELEMENTS, ^[N^] A CONSTANT. ^THE COMPILER PLACES EACH FIELD AT THE NEXT MULTIPLE OF ITS ALIGNMENT, AS A ^C COMPILER DOES: ^[CHAR^] AND ^[UCHAR^] TAKE 1 BYTE, ^[SHORT^] AND ^[USHORT^] 2, ^[INT^], ^[UINT^] AND ^[FLOAT^] 4, ^[LLONG^], ^[ULLONG^] AND ^[DOUBLE^] 8, AND ^[LONG^], ^[ULONG^], ^[SIZE^] AND ^[PTR^] THE WIDTH OF A ^C ^[long^], 8 BYTES OR 4 WHERE THE POINTERS ARE 32 BITS. ^EACH TYPE ALIGNS TO ITS SIZE, EXCEPT THAT THE 8 BYTE TYPES ALIGN TO 4 ON THE I386, AS THE ^SYSTEM ^V ^A^B^I HAS IT. ^A STRUCTURE ALIGNS AS ITS MOST ALIGNED FIELD AND ITS SIZE IS ROUNDED UP TO THAT ALIGNMENT. ^NAMES OF STRUCTURES, LIKE NAMES OF FIELDS, ARE CASE SENSITIVE, AND A DECLARATION HOLDS TO THE END OF THE FILE.
.PARAGRAPH
^[SIZEOF(name)^] IS THE SIZE IN BYTES OF A STRUCTURE OR OF A TYPE OF ^C (^[SIZEOF(PTR)^]), AND ^[OFFSETOF(name, field)^] THE OFFSET OF A FIELD; BOTH ARE CONSTANTS. ^A FIELD OF A NESTED STRUCTURE IS NAMED BY THE PATH OF ITS FIELDS, SEPARATED BY DOTS: ^[OFFSETOF(name, outer.inner)^], ^[FIELD(P, name.outer.inner)^].
.PARAGRAPH
^[FIELD(P, name.field)^] IS A VALUE OR A PLACE TO STORE TO, IN AN EXPRESSION, ON THE LEFT OF ^[:=^], AS AN ARGUMENT OF ^[CCALL^]. ^AS A VALUE IT HAS THE TYPE OF THE FIELD CONVERTED AS THE RESULT OF A DECLARED FUNCTION IS: A SIGNED TYPE IS SIGN-EXTENDED, AN UNSIGNED TYPE AND ^[PTR^] ARE ZERO-EXTENDED, ^[DOUBLE^] AND ^[FLOAT^] ARE A ^[REAL^]. ^A STORE CONVERTS TO THE TYPE OF THE FIELD: AN INTEGER FIELD TAKES THE LOW BITS OF AN INTEGER AND THE INTEGER PART OF A ^[REAL^], A ^[DOUBLE^] OR ^[FLOAT^] FIELD TAKES A ^[REAL^] OR AN INTEGER CONVERTED TO REAL. ^A STORE CHANGES THE BYTES OF ITS FIELD AND NO OTHERS. ^[FIELD(P, name.field, I)^] IS ELEMENT ^[I^] OF A FIELD THAT IS AN ARRAY; THE INDEX IS NOT CHECKED. ^AN ARRAY OF STRUCTURES IS REACHED BY ADDING THE OFFSET OF THE ELEMENT TO THE ADDRESS: ^[FIELD(P+(I*SIZEOF(name)), name.field)^]. ^THE ADDRESS ^[P^] IS ANY EXPRESSION, A POINTER FROM ^[malloc^], AN ADDRESS OF A VARIABLE (^[LOC(X)^]) OR A POINTER THAT ^C RETURNED. ^FOR EXAMPLE, AN ARRAY OF STRUCTURES SORTED BY ^[qsort^] WITH A COMPARISON THAT READS THEIR FIELDS:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
^S^T^R^U^C^T ^R^E^C (^I^N^T ^K^E^Y, ^D^O^U^B^L^E ^W);
^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K ^I^N^T ^B^Y^K^E^Y(^P^T^R, ^P^T^R);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^P^T^R CALLOC(^S^I^Z^E, ^S^I^Z^E);
^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L ^V^O^I^D QSORT(^P^T^R, ^S^I^Z^E, ^S^I^Z^E, ^P^T^R);
^N:=4;
^A:=^C^C^A^L^L CALLOC(^N,^S^I^Z^E^O^F(^R^E^C));
((^F^I^E^L^D(^A+(^I*^S^I^Z^E^O^F(^R^E^C)),^R^E^C.^K^E^Y):=(^N-^I)*10) ^F^O^R ^I ^I^N 0,1,^N-1);
^C^C^A^L^L QSORT(^A,^N,^S^I^Z^E^O^F(^R^E^C),^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K(^B^Y^K^E^Y));
((^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 3,^F^I^E^L^D(^A+(^I*^S^I^Z^E^O^F(^R^E^C)),^R^E^C.^K^E^Y)) ^F^O^R ^I ^I^N 0,1,^N-1);
^P^R^I^N^T /;
^F^I^N^I(0);
^S^U^B^R ^B^Y^K^E^Y(^X,^Y) ^I^S (
  ^F^I^E^L^D(^X,^R^E^C.^K^E^Y)<^F^I^E^L^D(^Y,^R^E^C.^K^E^Y) => ^R^E^T^U^R^N 0-1;
  ^F^I^E^L^D(^X,^R^E^C.^K^E^Y)=^F^I^E^L^D(^Y,^R^E^C.^K^E^Y) => ^R^E^T^U^R^N 0;
  1)
%%
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
PRINTS ^[10 20 30 40^], EACH IN A FIELD OF THREE. ^THE COMPILER REPORTS AT COMPILE TIME A STRUCTURE OR A FIELD THAT IS NOT DECLARED, A NAME DECLARED TWICE, A TYPE THAT IS NOT A TYPE OF ^C OR A STRUCTURE DECLARED EARLIER, AN ARRAY FIELD NAMED WITHOUT AN ELEMENT OR A FIELD THAT IS NOT AN ARRAY NAMED WITH ONE, AND A FIELD OF A STRUCTURE TYPE USED AS A VALUE. ^BIT FIELDS AND UNIONS ARE NOT DECLARED, A STRUCTURE IS NOT PASSED OR RETURNED BY VALUE, AND THE ATTRIBUTES THAT CHANGE A LAYOUT (^[packed^], ^[aligned^]) ARE NOT KNOWN; THE LAYOUT IS WHAT THE PROGRAM DECLARES.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER READS NO ^C HEADER. ^THE TOOL ^[imp72-cimport^] DOES: IT RUNS THE ^C PREPROCESSOR ON A HEADER AND WRITES THE ^[EXTERNAL^] DECLARATIONS OF THE FUNCTIONS, THE ^[STRUCT^] DECLARATIONS OF THE STRUCTURES AND THE ^[LET^] DECLARATIONS OF THE ENUMERATORS THAT IT CAN EXPRESS, AND LISTS THE REST IN A COMMENT WITH THE REASON. ^A DECLARATION HOLDS TO THE END OF ITS FILE, SO THE DECLARATIONS GO IN FRONT OF THE PROGRAM:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
IMP72-CIMPORT -N QSORT -N STRLEN STDLIB.H > DECLS.IMP
CAT DECLS.IMP PROG.IMP > ALL.IMP
IMP72 ALL.IMP
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^[-n NAME^] AND ^[-p PREFIX^] SELECT NAMES, AND ^[-D MACRO^] WRITES THE INTEGER VALUE OF A MACRO, WHICH THE ^C COMPILER EVALUATES. ^THE MANUAL PAGE ^[imp72-cimport(1)^] AND ^[docs/FFI.md^] DESCRIBE THE TOOL.
.SUBTITLE 3: ^HOW TO ^COMPILE AND ^RUN ^I^M^P72 ^PROGRAMS.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^C^O^M^P^I^L^I^N^G ^I^M^P72 ^P^R^O^G^R^A^M^S
.CENTER
^&3: ^HOW TO ^COMPILE AND ^RUN ^I^M^P72 ^PROGRAMS.\&
.SKIP 2
.SUBTITLE 3.1: ^COMPILING ^PROGRAMS.
.CENTER
^&3.1: ^COMPILING ^PROGRAMS.\&
.SKIP
.INDEX ^C^O^M^P^I^L^I^N^G ^I^M^P72 ^P^R^O^G^R^A^M^S
.INDEX ^S^W^I^T^C^H^E^S, ^C^O^M^P^I^L^E^R
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^I^M^P COMPILER IS CALLED BY THE COMMAND ^[imp72^], WHICH TAKES THE OPTIONS OF A ^[C^] COMPILER:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
IMP72 [OPTIONS] FILE.IMP ...
IMP72 -I [-S ^L^E^T^T^E^R^S] [FILE.IMP ...]
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^A FILE NAMED ON THE COMMAND LINE IS A SOURCE FILE (ANY NAME; ^[.imp^] BY CUSTOM), A FILE OF SYNTAX AND DECLARATIONS
(^[.imc^], ^SECTION 3.4) OR AN OBJECT FILE OR LIBRARY (^[.o^], ^[.a^], ^[.so^]) TO LINK.  ^A ^[.imc^] FILE IS READ BEFORE EACH
SOURCE FILE.  ^EACH SOURCE FILE IS COMPILED BY ITSELF, TO AN OBJECT FILE; THE OBJECTS ARE LINKED WITH THE OTHERS
NAMED, AND WITH THE RUNTIME LIBRARY ^[libimp72.a^], TO THE EXECUTABLE PROGRAM.  ^THE OPTIONS ARE
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
-C          ^COMPILE TO AN OBJECT FILE, FILE.O, AND DO NOT LINK.  ^WITH SEVERAL SOURCES
            THERE IS ONE OBJECT FOR EACH, IN THE CURRENT DIRECTORY.
-^S          ^COMPILE TO THE ASSEMBLY OF THE TARGET, FILE.S.
-^SIR        ^COMPILE TO ^L^L^V^M ^I^R TEXT, FILE.LL.
-O ^F^I^L^E     ^NAME THE OUTPUT (A.OUT FOR A PROGRAM).  ^WITH -C, -^S OR -^SIR, ONE SOURCE ONLY.
-S ^L^E^T^T^E^R^S  ^SET THE COMPILER SWITCHES (BELOW).
-^I ^I^M^A^G^E    ^START FROM THE COMPILER SAVED BY /^U OR /^V (^SECTION 3.4).
-I          ^READ COMMANDS FROM THE TERMINAL (^SECTION 3.1.2).
--RUN       ^COMPILE AND RUN THE PROGRAM IN THE PROCESS OF THE COMPILER.
--TARGET=^T^R^I^P^L^E  ^COMPILE FOR ANOTHER SYSTEM (^SECTION 3.1.3).
--SYSROOT=^D^I^R, --RUNTIME=^F^I^L^E   ^WHERE THE FILES OF THAT SYSTEM ARE.
--VERSION   ^PRINT THE VERSION (IMP72 0.10.0).
--HELP      ^PRINT THE OPTIONS.
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER FINDS ITS SYNTAX FILE ^[syntax64.imc^], THE FILE OF THE ^C INTERFACE ^[ffi.imc^] AND ^[libimp72.a^] NEXT TO ITSELF
(^[share/imp72^] AND ^[lib/imp72^] OF ITS INSTALLATION PREFIX); THE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES ^I^M^P72^S^Y^N^T^A^X, ^I^M^P72^F^F^I AND ^I^M^P72^L^I^B NAME
OTHERS.  ^IT MAKES NO TEMPORARY FILE FOR ^[-c^], ^[-S^], ^[-Sir^] OR ^[--run^]; TO LINK, IT HANDS THE LINKER THE OBJECT IN AN UNNAMED MEMORY FILE ON ^L^I^N^U^X, AND ELSEWHERE IN A PRIVATE DIRECTORY (MODE 0700) IN ^T^M^P^D^I^R IF THAT IS AN ABSOLUTE PATH, ELSE IN ^[/tmp^], DELETED AT EXIT, ON AN ERROR AND ON A SIGNAL.  ^THE MESSAGES GO TO THE STANDARD ERROR.
^A COMPILATION WITH ERRORS WRITES NO OUTPUT AND EXITS WITH THE STATUS 1; A WRONG COMMAND LINE EXITS WITH THE STATUS 2.
^THE COMPILER IS MADE OF ^L^L^V^M AND LLD LIBRARIES AND RUNS NO OTHER PROGRAM, EXCEPT ^[ld.lld^] WHERE ITS LLD IS NOT A LIBRARY.
.PARAGRAPH
^IF YOU WANT TO COMPILE THE ^I^M^P PROGRAM ON FILE ^[sum.imp^], THE SIMPLEST WAY IS ^[imp72 sum.imp^], AND ^[./a.out^] RUNS IT.
^[imp72 --run sum.imp^] COMPILES AND RUNS IT IN ONE STEP.  ^[imp72 -c a.imp b.imp^] MAKES ^[a.o^] AND ^[b.o^], AND
^[imp72 main.imp lib.imp -o prog^] COMPILES TWO FILES AND LINKS THEM; A VARIABLE THAT THE TWO SHARE IS DECLARED ^C^O^M^M^O^N IN BOTH
(^SECTION 2.1.4).
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER SWITCHES ARE GIVEN AS THE LETTERS OF THE ARGUMENT OF ^[-s^], AND THE LISTING
AND PRINTOUTS GO TO THE STANDARD ERROR:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
^A  ^PRINT THE CODE THE COMPILER PRODUCED, ONE LINE FOR EACH OPERATION OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE
   (^SECTION 2.3.3.1.2); THIS IS NOT THE ASSEMBLY OF THE MACHINE, WHICH -^S WRITES.
^L  ^PRINT A SOURCE LISTING.
^Y  ^PRINT THE SOURCE PROGRAM AS IT IS COMPILED (AS ^L, ON THE TERMINAL).
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE LETTERS ^B, ^F, ^G, ^I, ^M, ^N, ^P, ^S, ^T, ^X AND ^Z PRINT TABLES OF THE COMPILER (THE ASSEMBLER'S
DEBUGGING OUTPUT, FREE STORAGE, THE PARSE, THE DIRECTORY, THE CODE TREE, THE CONNECTIVITY MATRICES, THE
SEMANTICS ARRAYS, THE SYNTAX GRAPH AND STATISTICS); THEY ARE OF USE TO THE PERSON WHO WORKS ON THE COMPILER.
.SUBTITLE 3.1.1: ^THE ^COMPILER ^LISTING.
.INDEX ^L^I^S^T^I^N^G
.CENTER
^&3.1.1: ^THE ^COMPILER ^LISTING.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SOURCE LISTING OF THE COMPILER HAS A NUMBER AT THE LEFT OF EACH LINE OF CODE.
^THIS INDICATES THE LEVEL OF PARENTHESIS NESTING AT THE BEGINNING OF THE LINE.
^PARENTHESES CONTAINED WITHIN _# OR ' SIGNS ARE NOT COUNTED.  ^IF THE COMPILER DETECTS AN ERROR, THE
LISTING FROM THAT POINT IN THE SOURCE FILE ON IS WRITTEN.
.SUBTITLE 3.1.2: ^INTERACTIVE ^COMMANDS.
.CENTER
^&3.1.2: ^INTERACTIVE ^COMMANDS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^[imp72 -i^] READS COMMANDS FROM THE STANDARD INPUT.  ^IT PRINTS
ITS VERSION AND THE DATE AS A BANNER AND AN ASTERISK AS THE PROMPT.  ^A COMMAND LINE HAS THE FORMAT
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
OBJ,LIST__FILE.EXT/A/B
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^ALL FIELDS ARE OPTIONAL.  ^THE SOURCE IS READ FROM ^[file.ext^]; IF THE EXTENSION IS OMITTED THE COMPILER LOOKS FOR A FILE WITH
NO EXTENSION, THEN ^[file.imp^], THEN ^[file.imc^].  ^THE OBJECT FILE IS WRITTEN ON ^[obj.o^] (IT DEFAULTS TO ^[file.o^]).  ^LISTINGS THAT THE SWITCHES ASK FOR GO ON ^[list.lst^], OR ON ^[file.lst^] IF NO LIST
FILE IS NAMED.  ^THE ^[_^] THAT SEPARATES THE LIST FILE FROM THE SOURCE IS NOT A LETTER IN
A COMMAND.  ^THE SWITCHES /^A, /^C, /^H, /^L, /^U, /^V AND /^Y ARE GIVEN
AFTER THE FILE.
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
/^A  ^PRODUCE A LISTING OF THE CODE THE COMPILER PRODUCED (AS THE SWITCH ^A ABOVE).
/^C  ^CONTINUE AFTER THIS FILE: THE COMPILER PROMPTS FOR ANOTHER COMMAND; WITHOUT /^C IT ENDS.
/^H  ^HELP: LIST THE SWITCHES.
/^L  ^PRODUCE A SOURCE LISTING.
/^U  ^EXIT TO SAVE THE COMPILER: ^N^A^M^E__/^U WRITES THE COMPILER, WITH THE SYNTAX COMPILED SO FAR, ON ^N^A^M^E.IMG.
/^V  ^THE SAME AS /^U.
/^Y  ^LIST THE SOURCE PROGRAM ON THE TERMINAL AS IT IS COMPILED.
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^A COMMAND WITH NO FILE NAME GOES ON WITH THE CURRENT FILE.  ^A FILE THAT CANNOT BE FOUND IS ANSWERED BY ^[? FILE NOT FOUND^].
^[/D^] LISTS THE DEBUGGING SWITCHES.  ^IF THE END OF THE INPUT FILE IS REACHED WITHOUT THE TWO % SIGNS,
THE MESSAGE ^P^R^E^M^A^T^U^R^E ^E^N^D ^O^F ^I^N^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E IS PRINTED (^SECTION 3.2).
.SUBTITLE 3.1.3: ^CROSS ^COMPILING.
.INDEX ^C^R^O^S^S ^C^O^M^P^I^L^I^N^G
.CENTER
^&3.1.3: ^CROSS ^COMPILING.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^[--target=TRIPLE^] (THE NAMES OF ^L^L^V^M, WITH THE VENDOR LEFT OUT IF DESIRED: ^[aarch64-linux-gnu^], ^[riscv64-linux-musl^],
^[i686-linux-gnu^], ^[armv7-linux-gnueabihf^], ^[x86_64-apple-darwin^]) WITH ^[-c^], ^[-S^] OR ^[-Sir^] MAKES THE OBJECT
IN THE FORMAT OF THAT SYSTEM, WITH 32-BIT OR 64-BIT POINTERS AS IT HAS; THE WORD OF THE PROGRAM REMAINS 64 BITS.  ^THE BYTE ORDER OF THE TARGET IS THE ONE THAT ^L^L^V^M'S DATA LAYOUT OF THE TARGET MACHINE GIVES, SO A BIG ENDIAN TARGET
(^[s390x-linux-gnu^], ^[powerpc-linux-gnu^]) IS COMPILED ON A LITTLE ENDIAN COMPILER AND THE REVERSE: A TEXT CONSTANT IS THE WORD OF ITS CHARACTERS IN THE ORDER OF THE MEMORY OF THE TARGET.  ^THE ARCHITECTURE, THE OPERATING SYSTEM, THE ENVIRONMENT AND THE OBJECT FORMAT OF A TRIPLE ARE CHECKED BY ^L^L^V^M'S OWN TRIPLE PARSER, SO THE SET FOLLOWS THE INSTALLED ^L^L^V^M; THE VENDOR IS FREE TEXT.  ^WHERE THE ^L^L^V^M ^C++ HEADERS ARE NOT INSTALLED THE CHECK IS LENIENT: IT LOOKS AT THE CHARACTERS ONLY, AND ^L^L^V^M'S ANSWER FOR THE TARGET DECIDES.  ^A TRIPLE THAT FAILS THE CHECK GIVES THE MESSAGE ^[imp72: unrecognized target "TRIPLE"^] WITH THE PART THAT IS WRONG, THE STATUS IS 1 AND NO FILE IS WRITTEN.  ^TO LINK FOR ANOTHER SYSTEM, THE COMPILER MUST BE TOLD WHERE THE FILES OF THAT
SYSTEM ARE: ^[--sysroot=DIR^] (ITS ROOT, WITH ^[crt1.o^], ^[crti.o^], ^[crtn.o^] AND THE ^[C^] LIBRARY UNDER ^[DIR/usr/lib^], OR FOR MAC^O^S THE ^S^D^K
DIRECTORY, OR FOR ^W^I^N^D^O^W^S A ^[mingw-w64^] TREE WITH ^[crt2.o^] AND ^[libmingw32.a^]) AND ^[--runtime=FILE^] (THE ^[libimp72.a^] BUILT FOR IT), OR THE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES ^I^M^P72^S^Y^S^R^O^O^T AND ^I^M^P72^R^U^N^T^I^M^E.
^THE DYNAMIC LINKER AND THE ^[-m^] EMULATION COME FROM THE TRIPLE, FOR ABOUT 25 ARCHITECTURES OF ^L^I^N^U^X (^[glibc^] AND ^[musl^]), AND FOR ^F^R^E^E^B^S^D, ^N^E^T^B^S^D, ^O^P^E^N^B^S^D, MAC^O^S, ^[mingw-w64^] AND ^C^Y^G^W^I^N.  ^THERE IS NO RUNTIME LIBRARY FOR ^W^I^N^D^O^W^S OR ^C^Y^G^W^I^N: OBJECTS CAN BE MADE, AND A PROGRAM THAT HAS ITS OWN ^[main^] AND NEEDS NO RUNTIME CAN BE LINKED.  ^A TARGET THE DRIVER CANNOT LINK IS REFUSED WITH THE SYSTEM NAMED.
^WITHOUT THEM THE MESSAGE SAYS WHAT IS MISSING (^[no crt1.o under DIR/usr/lib/TRIPLE...^]) AND HOW TO GIVE IT, OR COMPILE WITH ^[-c^] AND LINK ON THE OTHER SYSTEM.
^THE RUNTIME LIBRARY OF A SYSTEM IS BUILT BY THE COMPILER ITSELF, WITH NO ^[C^] COMPILER.  ^[--run^] IS FOR THE SYSTEM THE COMPILER RUNS ON.
.SUBTITLE 3.2: ^COMPILATION ^ERROR ^DIAGNOSTICS.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^E^R^R^O^R ^D^I^A^G^N^O^S^T^I^C^S
.CENTER
^&3.2: ^COMPILATION ^ERROR ^DIAGNOSTICS.\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER REPORTS ON THREE CLASSES OF ERRORS: FATAL ERRORS, ERRORS, AND
ADVISORY ERRORS.  ^ADVISORY ERRORS INDICATE A CONDITION WHICH MAY NOT BE AN ERROR
BUT SHOULD BE BROUGHT TO THE PROGRAMMER'S ATTENTION.  ^THEY ARE LABELED ^A^D^V^I^S^O^R^Y.
^FATAL ERRORS TERMINATE COMPILATION IMMEDIATELY.  ^REGULAR ERRORS DO NOT TERMINATE
COMPILATION, BUT SUBSEQUENT COMPILATION MAY BE AFFECTED.  ^A MESSAGE IS WRITTEN ON THE
STANDARD ERROR AS
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
** ^E^R^R^O^R - ^N^E^S^T^E^D ^S^U^B^R^O^U^T^I^N^E^S.
** ^A^D^V^I^S^O^R^Y - ^P^R^O^D^U^C^T^I^O^N ^D^U^P^L^I^C^A^T^E^S ^O^N^E ^A^L^R^E^A^D^Y ^I^N ^S^Y^N^T^A^X **
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^AFTER AN ERROR NO OBJECT FILE IS WRITTEN AND THE EXIT STATUS IS 1.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE PHILOSOPHY BEHIND THE ERROR DIAGNOSTICS IN ^I^M^P PRESUMES A CERTAIN AMOUNT
OF MATURITY ON THE PART OF THE PROGRAMMER.  ^HE IS LESS RESTRICTED THAN IN A LANGUAGE SUCH AS ^F^O^R^T^R^A^N, BUT HE IS ALSO LESS PROTECTED FROM HIS OWN ERRORS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FOLLOWING ARE THE ERROR DIAGNOSTICS PROVIDED BY THE COMPILER.  ^THEY ARE
REGULAR ERRORS UNLESS OTHERWISE INDICATED.
.LEFT MARGIN 7
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^A^T^T^E^M^P^T ^T^O ^E^X^E^C^U^T^E ^U^N^D^E^F^I^N^E^D ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^O^U^T^I^N^E (^FATAL):  ^A SYNTAX STATEMENT NAMED A SEMANTIC ROUTINE THAT DOES NOT EXIST (YOU ALREADY GOT ^U^N^D^E^F^I^N^E^D ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^O^U^T^I^N^E FOR THAT), AND AN INSTANCE OF THE PRODUCTION HAS BEEN COMPILED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^A^N^T^E^C^E^D^E^N^T ^N^O^T ^A ^H^A^R^D^W^A^R^E ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R ^I^N ^S^Y^N^O^N^Y^M ^D^E^C^L^A^R^A^T^I^O^N:  ^IN A DECLARATION SUCH AS ^A ^I^S ^B, WHICH MAKES ^A A SYNONYM OF THE REGISTER ^B, ^B IS NOT ONE OF 0^R TO 15^R.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^B^A^D ^M^O^D^I^F^I^E^R:  ^A SEMANTIC CONDITION HAS AN IDENTIFIER WITH A BAD MODIFIER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^B^A^D ^R^E^S^U^L^T ^O^F ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S; ^I^G^N^O^R^E^D (^ADVISORY):  ^A SEMANTIC ROUTINE HAS RETURNED A VALUE FOR A SEMANTIC PART WHICH IS NOT AN OBJECT ON THE SEMANTICS ROUTINE STACK.  ^THIS MAY OR MAY NOT CAUSE TROUBLE LATER ON.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^B^A^D ^T^Y^P^E ^I^N ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S (^FATAL):  ^AN ENTRY OF THE SEMANTICS ARRAY HAS A TYPE THAT THE COMPILER DOES NOT KNOW.  ^THIS IS A BUG IN THE COMPILER OR IN A SYNTAX FILE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^L^C^U^L^A^T^E^D ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T ^I^S ^S^U^B^S^C^R^I^P^T^E^D (^ADVISORY):  ^AN EXPRESSION HAS BEEN EVALUATED TO A CONSTANT AND NOW AN ATTEMPT IS BEING MADE TO SUBSCRIPT IT.  ^SINCE THE CALCULATION YIELDS ONLY ONE WORD, THIS DOESN'T MAKE MUCH SENSE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^N^N^O^T ^G^E^T ^C^O^R^E ^T^O ^I^N^I^T^I^A^L^I^Z^E ^F^R^E^E ^S^T^O^R^A^G^E (^FATAL):  ^THE SYSTEM DID NOT GIVE THE COMPILER THE ADDRESS SPACE IT RESERVES FOR ITS TABLES.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^N^T ^U^S^E ^N^O^N^T^E^R^M^I^N^A^L^S ^I^N ^C^A^S^E ^A^R^G ^L^I^S^T:  ^THE PARAMETER LIST OF A ^C^A^S^E CONTAINS SOMETHING THAT IS NOT A CONSTANT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^S^E ^C^O^N^T^A^I^N^S ^S^U^B^E^X^P^R^E^S^S^I^O^N ^W^H^I^C^H ^I^S ^C^A^S^E ^B^U^T ^W^O^N^T ^G^E^N^E^R^A^L^I^Z^E (^ADVISORY):  ^YOU ARE WARNED THAT A SEMANTIC CONDITION IN A ^C^A^S^E DEFINITION CONTAINS A SUBEXPRESSION WHICH IS ALSO AN INSTANCE OF THE PRODUCTION IN THE SYNTAX PART.  ^THE MAIN EXPRESSION WILL GENERALIZE TO OTHER INSTANCES OF THE ^C^A^S^E, BUT THE SUBEXPRESSION WON'T.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^S^E ^D^E^F^I^N^E^D ^T^W^I^C^E ^O^R ^S^A^M^E ^N^A^M^E ^A^S ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^O^U^T^I^N^E:  ^A ^C^A^S^E MAY BE DEFINED IN ONE PLACE ONLY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^A^S^E ^U^N^D^E^F^I^N^E^D (^FATAL):  ^YOU TRIED TO REFERENCE A ^C^A^S^E SEMANTICS, BUT THE CASE WAS NEVER DEFINED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^C^O^D^E ^G^E^N^E^R^A^T^I^O^N ^S^T^A^C^K ^D^I^D ^N^O^T ^R^E^D^U^C^E ^T^O ^O^N^E ^I^T^E^M:  ^THE PROGRAM PARSED CORRECTLY, BUT SEMANTICS FOR SOME OF THE PROGRAM COULD NOT BE FOUND.  ^IF YOU ADDED YOUR OWN SYNTAX IN THE PROGRAM, CHECK IT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^E^R^R^O^R ^C^O^R^R^E^C^T^O^R ^G^I^V^E^S ^U^P. (^FATAL):  ^THE ERROR CORRECTOR WAS UNABLE TO CORRECT A SYNTAX ERROR.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^E^R^R^O^R ^C^O^R^R^E^C^T^O^R ^G^I^V^E^S ^U^P ^A^T % (^FATAL):  ^THE ERROR CORRECTOR WAS UNABLE TO CORRECT A SYNTAX ERROR, AND READ PAST THE % SIGN WHILE TRYING.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^E^X^P^O^N^E^N^T ^O^U^T ^O^F ^R^A^N^G^E:  ^THE POWER OF TEN OF A FLOATING POINT CONSTANT IS TOO LARGE OR TOO SMALL FOR A DOUBLE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^F^R^E^E ^S^T^O^R^A^G^E ^E^X^H^A^U^S^T^E^D (^FATAL):  ^YOUR PROGRAM HAS DEMANDED MORE SPACE TO COMPILE THAN THE COMPILER'S TABLES HOLD.  ^IF YOU HAD SYNTAX ERRORS, YOU MAY BE ABLE TO COMPILE WHEN THE ERRORS ARE REPAIRED.  ^OTHERWISE, MAKE YOUR PROGRAM SMALLER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^F^R^E^E ^S^T^O^R^A^G^E ^U^N^L^I^N^K^E^D (^FATAL):  ^A BUG IN THE COMPILER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^G^O ^T^O NAME, ^W^H^I^C^H ^I^S ^N^O^T ^I^N ^T^H^E ^S^U^B^R^O^U^T^I^N^E:  ^A ^G^O ^T^O NAMES A TAG THAT IS NEITHER IN THE ROUTINE NOR IN THE MAIN PROGRAM.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^I^L^L^E^G^A^L ^C^H^A^R^A^C^T^E^R ^I^N ^F^L^O^A^T^I^N^G ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T:  ^A FLOATING POINT CONSTANT HAS A CHARACTER THAT IS NOT A DIGIT, THE POINT, THE " OR A MINUS SIGN.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^I^N ^A^D^D^C^H^A^R - ^N^A^M^E > 80 ^C^H^A^R^A^C^T^E^R^S:  ^A SYNTACTIC CLASS NAME IS TOO LONG.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^M^O^D^I^F^I^E^R ^O^N ^O^T^H^E^R ^T^H^A^N ^F^I^R^S^T ^O^C^C^U^R^R^E^N^C^E ^O^F ^N^A^M^E ^I^S ^I^G^N^O^R^E^D:  ^IN A SEMANTIC CONDITION THE MODIFIERS BELONG TO THE FIRST OCCURRENCE OF A NAME ONLY.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^M^O^R^E ^T^H^A^N 2047 ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R^S ^I^N ^O^N^E ^C^O^M^P^I^L^A^T^I^O^N (^FATAL):  ^THE CODE OF A SOURCE FILE NAMES MORE THAN 2047 REGISTERS (TEMPORARIES AND THE REGISTERS OF THE PROGRAM).  ^SPLIT THE FILE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N ^N^O^T ^A ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T ^I^N ^N ^L^O^N^G:  ^N MAY BE A CONSTANT EXPRESSION BUT MUST CONTAIN NO VARIABLES (EXCEPT THOSE SYNTACTICALLY DEFINED AS CONSTANTS).
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N^A^M^E ^T^O^O ^L^O^N^G (^FATAL):  ^YOU USED AN IDENTIFIER LONGER THAN 100 CHARACTERS IN A SYNTACTIC CONDITION.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N^E^S^T^E^D ^C^A^S^E ^D^E^F^I^N^I^T^I^O^N^S ^I^L^L^E^G^A^L:  ^A ^C^A^S^E WAS DEFINED INSIDE A ^C^A^S^E.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N^E^S^T^E^D ^S^U^B^R^O^U^T^I^N^E^S:  ^DUE PROBABLY TO MISMATCHED PARENTHESES, A SUBROUTINE DEFINITION HAS BEEN INITIATED BEFORE THE PREVIOUS ONE WAS CLOSED OUT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N^O^N-^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T ^E^X^P^R^E^S^S^I^O^N ^I^N ^D^A^T^A ^S^T^A^T^E^M^E^N^T:  ^THE ITEMS OF A ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT ARE CONSTANTS AND STRINGS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^N^U^M^B^E^R ^M^I^S^S^I^N^G ^O^R ^O^U^T ^O^F ^P^L^A^C^E ^I^N ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S ^I^D^E^N^T^I^F^I^E^R:  ^IN A SEMANTIC CONDITIONAL, YOU HAD A NUMBER IN THE WRONG FORMAT OR WRONG PLACE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^O^P^E^R^A^T^I^O^N ^O^N ^A ^R^E^A^L ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T:  ^THE COMPILER DOES NOT EVALUATE THAT OPERATION ON REAL CONSTANTS; PUT THE VALUE IN A VARIABLE FIRST.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^O^U^T ^O^F ^M^E^M^O^R^Y ^F^O^R ^R^E^L^O^C^A^T^I^O^N ^T^A^B^L^E (^FATAL):  ^THE COMPILER COULD NOT GET MEMORY FOR A TABLE OF THE FREE STORAGE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^P^R^E^M^A^T^U^R^E ^E^N^D ^O^F ^I^N^P^U^T ^F^I^L^E (^FATAL):  ^COMPILER READ THE ENTIRE INPUT FILE BUT DID NOT ENCOUNTER THE %% WHICH TERMINATES ^I^M^P PROGRAMS.  ^PERHAPS YOU: 1. ^ONLY HAD ONE %.  2. ^FAILED TO MATCH _# OR ' WITH ANOTHER ONE, SO THAT THE % SIGN GOT ABSORBED IN A CONSTANT/COMMENT.  ^CHECK THE NUMBERS DOWN THE LEFT MARGIN OF THE LISTING TO SEE WHEN THEY STOPPED FOLLOWING THE PARENTHESIS NESTING OF THE CODE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^P^R^O^D^U^C^T^I^O^N ^D^U^P^L^I^C^A^T^E^S ^O^N^E ^A^L^R^E^A^D^Y ^I^N ^S^Y^N^T^A^X (^ADVISORY):  ^THE PRODUCTION IN A SYNTAX STATEMENT IS IDENTICAL TO ONE ALREADY IN THE SYNTAX.  ^THIS IS NOT AN ERROR, AND IS USED TO ADD SPECIAL SEMANTIC CASES TO ALREADY DEFINED SYNTAX.  ^THE DRIVER OF THE COMPILER DOES NOT PRINT IT.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^P^R^O^P^E^R^T^Y ^D^E^C^L^A^R^E^D ^I^S ^N^O^T ^A^P^P^L^I^C^A^B^L^E ^T^O ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R, ^P^R^O^P^E^R^T^Y ^M^A^Y ^O^N^L^Y ^B^E ^D^E^C^L^A^R^E^D ^F^O^R ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R^S:  ^C^O^M^M^O^N, ^R^E^A^L, ^I^N^T^E^G^E^R OR ^N ^L^O^N^G WAS DECLARED FOR A REGISTER, OR ^R^E^L^E^A^S^E^D FOR A NAME THAT IS NOT A REGISTER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^P^U^S^H^D^O^W^N ^S^T^A^C^K ^U^N^D^E^R^F^L^O^W ^E^R^R^O^R (^FATAL):  ^INDICATES A COMPILER BUG.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^Q^U^O^T^E^D ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S ^N^O^T ^A^N ^I^N^S^T^A^N^C^E ^O^F ^P^R^O^D^U^C^T^I^O^N:  ^A SEMANTIC CONDITION WAS NOT AN INSTANCE OF THE PRODUCTION IN THE SYNTAX STATEMENT IN WHICH IT APPEARED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^C^O^N^D^I^T^I^O^N ^T^O^O ^C^O^M^P^L^E^X (^FATAL), ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^O^U^T^I^N^E^S ^N^E^S^T^E^D ^T^O^O ^D^E^E^P (^FATAL):  ^A SEMANTIC CONDITION OR A NEST OF SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS BEYOND THE LIMITS OF THE COMPILER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S ^S^T^A^C^K ^O^V^E^R^F^L^O^W (^FATAL):  ^THERE IS EXACTLY ONE MAJOR TABLE IN THE COMPILER WHICH IS NOT DYNAMICALLY EXPANDABLE, AND YOUR PROGRAM HAS STUFFED IT FULL.  ^THIS CAN ONLY HAPPEN THROUGH THE USE OF A VERY COMPLEX SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALL IN A SEMANTICS PART OF A SYNTAX STATEMENT, OR MAYBE A BUG IN A SEMANTIC ROUTINE, OR A VERY LONG LIST OF NAMES IN YOUR PROGRAM.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^T^A^C^K ^U^N^D^E^R^F^L^O^W ^I^N ^F^S^U^B (^FATAL):  ^INDICATES A COMPILER BUG.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^U^B^S^C^R^I^P^T^E^D ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R - ^I^G^N^O^R^E^D:  ^THE SUBSCRIPT ON A REGISTER IS DISREGARDED.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^Y^N^T^A^C^T^I^C ^A^M^B^I^G^U^I^T^Y; ^U^N^R^E^S^O^L^V^E^D ^A^T %:  ^THE PARSER HAS FOUND A SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY WHICH IT WAS NOT ABLE TO RESOLVE BY THE END OF YOUR PROGRAM.  ^IF YOUR PROGRAM CONTAINS SYNTAX STATEMENTS, CHECK THEM.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^Y^N^T^A^C^T^I^C ^A^M^B^I^G^U^I^T^Y:  ^THE COMPILER WAS ABLE TO INTERPRET A PORTION OF YOUR
PROGRAM IN TWO CONFLICTING WAYS, AND WAS NOT ABLE TO DECIDE WHICH WAY YOU HAD IN MIND.  ^THE
AMBIGUOUS SEGMENT OF THE PROGRAM IS INDICATED ON THE LISTING (AFTER A FASHION) BY A LIST OF THE
IDENTIFIERS (VARIABLE NAMES AND CONSTANTS) WHICH APPEAR IN THE SEGMENT, PRINTED IN REVERSE ORDER. 
^SOME CONSTRUCTS WHICH MAY PRODUCE THIS ERROR ARE: ^A=>^B=>^C ^E^L^S^E ^D, WHICH IS REALLY AMBIGUOUS
AND SHOULD BE PARENTHESIZED EXPLICITLY, AND (^A ^R^E^L^O^P ^B)=>^C, WHERE ^R^E^L^O^P IS ANY
RELATIONAL OPERATOR, WHICH IS NOT AMBIGUOUS, AND WILL GENERATE CORRECT CODE, BUT THE PARENTHESES MAY
BE REMOVED TO GET RID OF THE ERROR MESSAGE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^E^R^R^O^R:  ^THE PARSER HAS NOT BEEN ABLE TO INTERPRET YOUR PROGRAM AS A LEGAL ^I^M^P
EXPRESSION.  ^IT HAS ATTEMPTED TO CONTINUE THE COMPILATION BY REPLACING PART OF YOUR PROGRAM AT THE
POINT OF THE ERROR BY WHAT IT HOPES IS A CORRECTION OF THE ERROR.  ^IT IS POSSIBLE THAT MORE ERRORS
WILL RESULT FROM THIS CORRECTION LATER ON.  ^THE OFFENDING LINE IS WRITTEN WITH A LINE FEED INSERTED
AT THE POINT AT WHICH THE ERROR WAS DETECTED, FOLLOWED BY THE LINE ** ^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^E^R^R^O^R ** AND
A MARK UNDER THE PLACE, AND THEN THE CORRECTION:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
^X:=1 +
* 2; ^P^R^I^N^T ^I^G^R 0,^X,/; ^F^I^N^I(0)
** ^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^E^R^R^O^R **
          _^
** ^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^E^R^R^O^R - "* " ^I^S ^R^E^P^L^A^C^E^D ^B^Y "0 * "
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 7
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^T^H^E ^A^D^D^R^E^S^S ^O^F ^A ^C^O^M^P^U^T^E^D ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T:  ^L^O^C WAS APPLIED TO A CONSTANT THAT IS NOT A WORD OF THE PROGRAM (A CONSTANT EXPRESSION).
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^T^O^O ^M^A^N^Y ^A^R^G^S ^T^O ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^T^N ^O^R ^P^A^R^A^M ^S^T^A^C^K ^U^N^D^E^R^F^L^O^W (^FATAL):  ^EITHER YOU TRIED TO EXECUTE A SEMANTIC ROUTINE WITH MORE THAN 10 ARGUMENTS, OR ELSE THERE IS A COMPILER BUG.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^T^O^O ^M^A^N^Y ^E^R^R^O^R^S: ^S^T^O^P^P^E^D, ^T^O^O ^M^A^N^Y ^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^E^R^R^O^R^S (^FATAL):  ^A LARGE NUMBER OF ERRORS HAVE BEEN DETECTED IN YOUR PROGRAM, AND THE COMPILER SEES NO POINT IN PROCEEDING.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^T^O^O ^M^A^N^Y ^L^O^C^A^L ^S^Y^M^B^O^L^S ^I^N ^R^E^F^E^R^E^N^C^E^D ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C^S (^FATAL):  ^YOU TRIED TO REFERENCE QUOTED SEMANTICS WITH MORE THAN 10 LOCAL SYMBOLS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^T^W^O ^L^E^N^G^T^H^S ^I^N ^S^A^M^E ^D^E^C^L^A^R^A^T^I^O^N, ^T^W^O ^R^E^G^I^S^T^E^R^S ^I^N ^S^A^M^E ^D^E^C^L^A^R^A^T^I^O^N:  ^A DECLARATION WITH TWO ^N ^L^O^N^G, OR WITH TWO SYNONYMS FOR REGISTERS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^U^N^D^E^F^I^N^E^D ^S^E^M^A^N^T^I^C ^R^O^U^T^I^N^E:  ^A SYNTAX STATEMENT CALLS A SEMANTIC ROUTINE THAT DOES NOT EXIST.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^U^N^K^N^O^W^N ^O^P^E^R^A^T^I^O^N ^I^N ^T^H^E ^C^O^D^E:  ^THE CODE GENERATOR FOUND AN OPERATION IT CANNOT TRANSLATE.  ^THIS IS A BUG IN THE COMPILER OR IN A SYNTAX FILE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -7
^V^A^L^U^E ^N^O^T ^A ^C^O^N^S^T^A^N^T:  ^VALUE IN ^V^A^L^U^E SEMANTICS MUST BE A CONSTANT.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMMANDS ALSO PRINT MESSAGES OF THEIR OWN: ^[imp72: unknown option^], ^[imp72: missing argument to^],
^[imp72: cannot name the output with more than one source to compile^], ^[? FILE NOT FOUND: name^] FOR A SOURCE THAT CANNOT
BE READ, ^[imp72: no target^] FOR A TARGET THAT ^L^L^V^M DOES NOT HAVE, AND THE MESSAGES OF THE LINKER (FOR EXAMPLE
^[undefined symbol: impmain^] WHEN NO FILE HAS A MAIN PROGRAM).
.SUBTITLE 3.3: ^LINKING AND ^RUNNING.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^L^O^A^D^I^N^G ^O^B^J^E^C^T ^P^R^O^G^R^A^M^S
.INDEX ^R^U^N^N^I^N^G ^O^B^J^E^C^T ^P^R^O^G^R^A^M^S
.CENTER
^&3.3: ^LINKING AND ^RUNNING.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER LINKS THE PROGRAM: ITS OBJECT FILES AND THOSE NAMED ON THE COMMAND LINE, THE RUNTIME LIBRARY
^[libimp72.a^] AND THE ^[C^] LIBRARY, WITH LLD IN ITS OWN PROCESS.  ^THE RESULT IS AN EXECUTABLE OF THE SYSTEM, WHICH IS RUN
BY ITS NAME.  ^FOR A PROGRAM OF SEVERAL FILES:
.SKIP
.NOFILL
.LEFT MARGIN 10
IMP72 -C A.IMP B.IMP
IMP72 A.O B.O -O PROG
./prog
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^EXACTLY ONE OF THE FILES HAS A MAIN PROGRAM (STATEMENTS BEFORE ITS FIRST ^S^U^B^R); WITH NONE THE LINK STOPS WITH
^[undefined symbol: impmain^].  ^THE OBJECTS MAY BE LINKED BY ANOTHER LINKER WITH THE ^[C^] LIBRARY AND ^[libimp72.a^]
(^SECTION 2.4.2).  ^IF YOUR PROGRAM DOES ANY ^I/^O, IT SHOULD CALL ^F^I^N^I (SEE ^SECTION 2.2.7) TO COMPLETE THE ^I/^O OPERATIONS.
.PARAGRAPH
^IF YOUR PROGRAM SHOULD FAIL AT RUN TIME, IT IS A ^UNIX PROCESS: AN INTEGER DIVISION BY ZERO OR A REFERENCE TO A
BAD ADDRESS ENDS IT WITH A SIGNAL, AND THE FILES ARE NOT CLOSED.  ^THE PROGRAM HAS NO SYMBOL TABLE OF ITS OWN; ^[lldb^] AND ^[gdb^]
USE THE SYMBOLS OF THE OBJECT, WHICH ARE THE NAMES OF THE ROUTINES AND OF THE ^C^O^M^M^O^N VARIABLES.
.SUBTITLE 3.4: ^EXTENDED ^COMPILERS.
.CENTER
^&3.4: ^EXTENDED ^COMPILERS.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE LANGUAGE IS DEFINED BY THE SYNTAX FILE THE COMPILER READS: NEW SYNTAX IS ADDED BY SYNTAX STATEMENTS (^SECTION 2.3) IN
THREE WAYS.
.SKIP
.LEFT MARGIN 10
.INDENT -3
1. ^IN THE SOURCE PROGRAM ITSELF: A SYNTAX STATEMENT IS A STATEMENT AND TAKES EFFECT FROM WHERE IT IS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^IN A FILE NAMED ^[file.imc^] ON THE COMMAND LINE, WHICH IS READ BEFORE EACH SOURCE FILE: ^[imp72 inv.imc p1.imp^].
^IT ENDS WITH TWO % SIGNS, LIKE A PROGRAM.  ^THE FILE ^[ffi.imc^] OF THE COMPILER, WHICH DEFINES ^C^C^A^L^L, ^E^X^T^E^R^N^A^L, ^C^A^L^L^B^A^C^K, ^F^N^P^T^R AND ^S^T^R^U^C^T, IS SUCH A FILE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^IN AN IMAGE OF THE COMPILER.  ^WITH ^[imp72 -i^], THE COMMAND ^[inv.imc/C^] READS THE FILE AND CONTINUES, AND THE COMMAND
^[myc_/U^] SAVES THE COMPILER WITH THE SYNTAX READ SO FAR ON ^[myc.img^].  ^A PROGRAM IS THEN COMPILED BY THE EXTENDED COMPILER WITH
^[imp72 -I myc.img p1.imp^].
^THE IMAGE HOLDS THE TABLES OF THE COMPILER (THE DIRECTORY, THE SYNTAX, THE DECLARATIONS) AND THE VARIABLES OF ITS MODULES, AND IS FOR THE
COMPILER THAT MADE IT; ANOTHER BUILD REFUSES IT.  ^IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE ADDRESS AT WHICH THE FREE STORAGE OF THE COMPILER LIES: THE
COMPILER ASKS FOR THE ADDRESS THE IMAGE WAS SAVED AT, AND WHEN THE SYSTEM GIVES ANOTHER ONE (AS A SYSTEM THAT RANDOMISES THE ADDRESSES OF
A PROCESS DOES) THE ADDRESSES OF THE TABLES THAT THE MODULES HOLD ARE MOVED WITH THEM.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FILE ^[syntax64.imc^] IS THE DEFINITION OF ^I^M^P72, AND THE COMPILER READS IT FIRST.
^TO MAKE A COMPILER WITH A DIFFERENT LANGUAGE, CHANGE THE FILE (OR POINT ^I^M^P72^S^Y^N^T^A^X AT ANOTHER) AND THE SEMANTIC ROUTINES
IT CALLS.  ^THE COMPILER IS BUILT FROM ITS SOURCES BY ^[make^] IN THE SOURCE TREE, WHICH NEEDS A ^C99 COMPILER AND THE ^L^L^V^M
LIBRARIES (AND LLD, AS A LIBRARY, TO LINK IN THE PROCESS).  ^[make check^] RUNS THE TESTS, AND ^[make install PREFIX=DIR^]
INSTALLS ^[bin/imp72^], ^[share/imp72/syntax64.imc^], ^[share/imp72/ffi.imc^] AND ^[lib/imp72/libimp72.a^].  ^THE SOURCES OF THE
COMPILER ARE IN ^I^M^P72 (DIRECTORY ^[src^]) AND COMPILE WITH THE COMPILER (^SECTION 4).
.SUBTITLE 4: ^INTERNAL ^DOCUMENTATION OF THE ^I^M^P72 ^COMPILER.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^I^N^T^E^R^N^A^L ^D^O^C^U^M^E^N^T^A^T^I^O^N
.CENTER
^&4: ^INTERNAL ^DOCUMENTATION OF THE ^I^M^P72 ^COMPILER.\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS SECTION PROVIDES AN OVERVIEW OF THE ORGANIZATION OF THE ^I^M^P72 COMPILER.
^DOCUMENTATION OF SPECIFIC ROUTINES, DATA BASES, ETC., IS INCLUDED AT THE BEGINNING OF EACH FILE OF SOURCE CODE.  ^THE INTENT OF THE PRESENT SECTION IS TO
PROVIDE A FRAME OF REFERENCE FOR THE READER WHO WISHES TO TINKER WITH THE COMPILER.
^FOR THOSE WITH A MORE INTELLECTUAL INTEREST IN THE COMPILER'S
WORKINGS AND DESIGN, [^BILOFSKY 1973], [^IRONS 1971] AND
[^WEINGART 1973] MAY BE MORE EDIFYING.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER IS PROGRAMMED MODULARLY WHEREVER POSSIBLE, AT SOME COST IN
EFFICIENCY.  (^THIS COST HAS BEEN SUBSTANTIALLY REDUCED BY REPLACING MANY
SUBROUTINE CALLS BY IN-LINE SYNTACTIC MACROS.)  ^THIS PHILOSOPHY
WAS ADOPTED IN ORDER TO ENABLE REPLACEMENT AND MODIFICATION OF PORTIONS OF THE COMPILER WITH MINIMAL REPERCUSSIONS.  ^ACCORDINGLY,
THERE IS NO COMMON DATA BASE IN THE COMPILER.  ^NECESSARY INFORMATION
CONCERNING ANOTHER MODULE'S DATA BASE IS ACQUIRED VIA SUBROUTINE CALLS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE COMPILER MAY BE DIVIDED CONCEPTUALLY INTO THE FOLLOWING SECTIONS:
.RIGHT MARGIN 70
.PAGE
.NOFILL
^S^O^U^R^C^E  ^D^A^T^A ^B^A^S^E                ^F^U^N^C^T^I^O^N^S
^F^I^L^E^S
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
.SKIP
^I. ^HOUSEKEEPING.
^F^R^E^E    ^FREE STORAGE.   ^FREE STORAGE MODULE.  ^MAINTAINS FREE
                        STORAGE, WHICH IS DYNAMICALLY ALLOCA-
                        TED AND USED FOR VIRTUALLY ALL TABLES.
^D^I^R     ^DIRECTORY.      ^DIRECTORY MODULE.
^S^T^A^C^K   ^SEMANTIC ROU-   ^SEMANTIC STACK MODULE.  ^MAINTAINS A
        TINES' STACK    STACK FOR PARAMETERS OF SEMANTIC RTNS.
"       (PROVIDED       ^BIT MATRIX MODULE.  ^CREATES AND ACCES-
        BY CALLER)      SES ARBITRARY BIT MATRICES.
"       ^PUSHDOWN STACK  ^PUSHDOWN STACK MODULE.  ^PROVIDES A ^P^D
                        STACK FOR ANYONE WHO WANTS.
^D^R^I^V^E^R  (NONE)          ^THE COMMAND LINE OF IMP72: THE OPTIONS,
                        THE COMPILATION OF EACH SOURCE FILE AND
                        THE LINK.
^I^M^P^M    (NONE)          ^THE DRIVER PROGRAM OF THE KERNEL: READS
                        THE SYNTAX AND COMPILES THE FILES, OR
                        THE COMMANDS OF ^SECTION 3.1.2.
^I^M^A^G^E                   ^SAVING THE COMPILER (/^U, /^V) AND
                        STARTING FROM THE IMAGE (-^I).
^D^E^B^U^G                   ^DEBUGGING MODULE, THE PRINTOUTS OF THE
                        DEBUGGING SWITCHES.
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
^I^I. ^HOUSEKEEPING _& ^PARSING.
^L^E^X                     ^INPUT AND LEXICAL ANALYZER.
^R^S^Y^N                    ^SYNTAX BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE, AND SEMANTICS
 _& ^R^S^Y^N2                  FOR SYNTAX STATEMENTS.
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
^I^I^I. ^PARSING.
^P^A^R^S^E   ^PARSE ARRAYS.   ^PARSE MODULE. ^CALLS HEAVILY UPON ^G^R^A^P^H
                        AND ^E^R^C^O^R.
^E^R^C^O^R   ^PARSE ARRAYS.   ^ERROR CORRECTION AND SYNTACTIC DISAM-
                        BIGUATION.
^G^R^A^P^H   ^SYNTAX GRAPH _&  ^SYNTAX GRAPH MODULE.  ^CONSTRUCTS AND
        CONNECTIVITY    ACCESSES THE SYNTAX DATA BASE.
        MATRICES.
^I^V. ^SEMANTICS.
^E^N^T^R^E^E  ^CODE GENERATION ^CODE TREE MODULE.  ^BUILDS AND ACCESSES
        TREE.           CODE GENERATION TREE.
^C^O^T^R^E^E  ^CODE            ^CODE GENERATION MODULE.  ^CALLS HEAVILY
        GENERATION      ON CODE TREE MODULE.  ^DOES CODE TREE
        STACK.          MATCHING _& INVOKES SEMANTIC ROUTINES.
                        ^MAINTAINS CODE GENERATION STACK.
^D^O^S^E^M   ^SEMANTICS       ^SEMANTICS MODULE.  ^BUILDS AND ACCESSES
        ARRAY.          SEMANTICS ARRAY.  ^OVERSEES SEMANTICS
                        STACK.
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
^V. ^SEMANTICS _& ^CODE ^GENERATION (DIRECTORY SRC/LLVM-IR).
^C^O^D^E    ^ENTRIES IN      ^SEMANTIC ROUTINES, MOSTLY TARGET-
 _& ^C^O^D^E2  SEMANTICS       MACHINE-DEPENDENT.  ^GENERATE CHAINS OF
 _& ^C^O^D^E3  STACK; CODE     OPERATIONS OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE
        ARRAYS.         (^SECTION 2.3.3.1.2).
^I^M^P^S^E^M  ^CODE AND REG-   ^MACHINE-INDEPENDENT SEMANTIC ROUTINES.
        ISTER ARRAYS.   ^DECLARATIONS AND REGISTERS.
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
^V^I. ^CODE ^OUTPUT (DIRECTORIES SRC AND SRC/LLVM-IR).
^D^E^M^I^T   ^CODE CHAINS.    ^WALKS THE CHAINS OF OPERATIONS AND
 _& ^I^X                     WRITES THE TEXT OF THE ^I^R.
^I^R2^L^L   ^I^R TEXT.        ^WRITES THE ^I^R AS ^L^L^V^M ^I^R.
 _& ^I^R2^L^L^X
^L^L^V^M    ^L^L^V^M MODULE.    ^OBJECT FILE, ASSEMBLY OR ^L^L^V^M ^I^R TEXT
                        BY THE ^L^L^V^M LIBRARIES; THE PROGRAM OF
                        --RUN; THE LINK WITH LLD.
.SKIP
-------------------------------------------------------------
^V^I^I. ^RUNTIME LIBRARY (DIRECTORY LIB).
^I^M^P72^I^O                 ^P^R^I^N^T, ^R^E^A^D, ^F^I^L^E, ^F^I^N^I.
^I^M^P72^R^T                 ^C^A^L^L^I, ^E^X^E^C^U^T^E, BYTE POINTERS, SHIFTS,
                        BLOCK MOVES.
^I^M^P72^M^A^I^N, ^I^M^P72^S^Y^S     ^THE MAIN PROGRAM, THE SYSTEM'S VALUES.
.RIGHT MARGIN 60
.FILL

.SUBTITLE 4.1: ^PARSING.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&4.1: ^PARSING.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE GENERAL FLOW OF CONTROL WITHIN THE COMPILER IS AS FOLLOWS:  ^P^A^R^S^E
OBTAINS A STREAM OF INPUT SYMBOLS (IN THE FORM OF DIRECTORY INDICES) FROM ^L^E^X,
AND PRODUCES A STREAM OF PARSER OUTPUT IN THE FORM OF TERMINAL SYMBOLS (NEGATIVE
DIRECTORY INDICES) AND PRODUCTION NUMBERS (POSITIVE INTEGERS N, CONVENTIONALLY
INDICATED BY [N].  ^IN FACT, N IS AN INDEX IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY, FOR HISTORICAL
REASONS, BUT NEED NOT BE.).  ^THE PARSER OUTPUT IS IN ^POLISH ^POSTFIX FORM.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE PARSER IS A BOTTOM-UP PARSER (^IRONS 1971), DRIVEN THROUGH ONE CYCLE BY
.INDEX PARSER
EACH INPUT SYMBOL.  ^AT THE END OF THE CYCLE, IT HAS A SET OF POSSIBLE PARSES FOR
THE INPUT STRING SO FAR.  ^IT TAKES ONE OF THE FOLLOWING ACTIONS, DEPENDING ON
THE NUMBER OF PARSES IN THE SET.
.LEFT MARGIN 7
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1. ^IF THERE ARE NO PARSES, A SYNTAX ERROR HAS OCCURRED.  ^E^R^C^O^R IS CALLED
TO GENERATE A STRING OF SYMBOLS WHICH MAY BE INSERTED INTO THE PARSE
TO FORM A SYNTACTICALLY CORRECT EXPRESSION.  (^E^R^C^O^R USES THE PHILOSOPHY, BUT NOT THE EXACT METHOD, OF (^IRONS 1963)).
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^IF THERE IS MORE THAN ONE PARSE, ^A^M^B^I^G IS CALLED TO DECIDE IF AN AMBIGUITY HAS GENERATED MULTIPLE PARSES, AND TO DISCARD THE REDUNDANT PARSES IF THIS IS THE CASE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^IF THERE IS ONE UNIQUE PARSE, THE PORTION OF THE PARSER OUTPUT STREAM
GENERATED SINCE THE LAST TIME THERE WAS ONE UNIQUE PARSE IS PASSED TO
THE SEMANTICS PORTION OF THE COMPILER BY CALLING ^C^O^T^R^E^E.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^ALL OTHER FUNCTIONS PERFORMED BY THE COMPILER, INCLUDING ADDITION OF SYNTAX
AND SEMANTICS TO THE COMPILER TABLES, AND INVOKING THE ASSEMBLY PHASE, ARE PERFORMED BY SEMANTIC ROUTINES INVOKED BY THE PRODUCTIONS WHICH ^P^A^R^S^E PASSES TO
^C^O^T^R^E^E FOR INTERPRETATION.  ^THUS THE ENTIRE FLOW OF CONTROL OF THE COMPILER IS
DIRECTED BY THE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS OF THE LANGUAGE.
.SUBTITLE 4.2: ^SEMANTICS.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&4.2: ^SEMANTICS.\&
.PARAGRAPH
^C^O^T^R^E^E MAINTAINS A CODE GENERATION STACK, WHICH STARTS OFF AS THE PARSER
OUTPUT IN POSTFIX FORM, BUT IS COLLAPSED AS THE PRODUCTIONS IN THE STACK ARE
APPLIED TO THEIR ARGUMENTS, BY INVOKING THE APPROPRIATE SEMANTICS FOR THE PRODUCTIONS, TO PRODUCE A SINGLE STACK ENTRY (SEMANTIC OBJECT) WHICH IS INSERTED
BACK IN THE STACK IN PLACE OF THE PRODUCTION AND ITS ARGUMENTS.  ^THIS COULD
ALWAYS BE BE DONE AT THE BOTTOM OF THE STACK (WE WILL CONSIDER THE WORKING END
TO BE THE BOTTOM), AS SOON AS THE PARSER SUPPLIED A PRODUCTION, WERE IT NOT
FOR THE SPECIAL SEMANTIC CASES.  ^THESE REQUIRE THAT A PRODUCTION NOT BE APPLIED
TO ITS ARGUMENTS UNTIL ENOUGH ADDITIONAL PARSER OUTPUT HAS BEEN SUPPLIED TO
ENSURE THAT THE PRODUCTION IS NOT PART OF SOME SPECIAL CASE.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN ORDER TO DO THIS, ALL SPECIAL CASES ARE KEPT IN A CODE GENERATION TREE,
WHICH IS BUILT AND ACCESSED BY ROUTINES IN THE CODE TREE MODULE, ^E^N^T^R^E^E.  ^THE
SPECIAL CASES ARE ENTERED IN THE TREE AS NODES WHICH MAY MATCH EITHER A PARTICULAR CLASS OF OBJECTS (AS SPECIFIED BY THE MODIFIERS IN THE SEMANTIC CONDITION SEE ^SECTION 2.3.3.2), OR A PARTICULAR PRODUCTION.  ^WHEN A NEW SUCCESSOR IS ADDED
TO A NODE, IT IS CAREFULLY INSERTED AMONG EXISTING SUCCESSORS SO THAT THE ORDER
OF SUCCESSORS OF A NODE, FROM LEFT TO RIGHT, IS IN ORDER OF INCREASING GENERALITY.  ^THIS IS DONE BY ^E^N^T^R^E^E, AND BY SUBROUTINES ^I^N^S^N^O AND ^C^O^M^P^N^O.  ^THIS ORDER
INSURES THAT THE MOST SPECIFIC CASE IN THE TREE IS THE ONE TO MATCH A GIVEN
PIECE OF PARSER OUTPUT (SUBJECT TO THE DIFFICULTIES MENTIONED IN ^SECTION 2.3.5),
SINCE NODES ARE SEARCHED LEFT-TO-RIGHT WHILE ATTEMPTING A MATCH.
.INDEX \\SPECIAL CASES, IMPLEMENTATION OF
.PARAGRAPH
^THE DEFINITION OF UNCONDITIONAL SEMANTICS FOR A PRODUCTION CAUSES THE MOST
GENERAL POSSIBLE CASE OF THAT PRODUCTION TO BE ENTERED IN THE CODE GENERATION
TREE, ENSURING THAT IF NO SPECIAL CASE IS FOUND THE SEMANTICS FOR THE GENERAL
CASE WILL BE INVOKED.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE TERMINAL NODES OF THE CODE GENERATION TREE MUST ALWAYS MATCH SOME PRODUCTION.  ^IN ORDER TO AVOID EXCESSIVE FANOUT TO THESE NODES, THE TERMINAL NODES
COMING FROM ANY GIVEN NODE ARE CONDENSED INTO ONE.  ^THE ENTRY FOR EACH PRODUCTION IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY CONTAINS A POINTER TO A LIST FOR THAT PRODUCTION, IN
THE ARRAY ^P^R^O^S^E^M (PART OF THE SEMANTICS MODULE), CONTAINING THE NAMES OF ALL THE
TERMINAL NODES IN THE CODE GENERATION TREE WHERE THAT PRODUCTION MAY MATCH, AND
A POINTER BACK TO THE SEMANTICS ARRAY ENTRY FOR THE SEMANTICS TO BE INVOKED AT
THAT NODE.  ^THE ^P^R^O^S^E^M ENTRY FOR A PRODUCTION ALSO CONTAINS A BIT VECTOR TELLING
AT WHICH LEVELS OF THE CODE GENERATION TREE THE PRODUCTION MAY MATCH SOME NODE,
EITHER TERMINAL OR OTHERWISE.  ^THIS INFORMATION MAKES IT POSSIBLE FOR ^C^O^T^R^E^E TO
ASCERTAIN IN MANY CASES THAT NO MATCH IS POSSIBLE STARTING AT A GIVEN POINT IN
THE CODE GENERATION STACK, WITHOUT HAVING TO SEARCH THE ENTIRE TREE.
.PARAGRAPH
^CERTAIN NON-TERMINAL NODES IN THE CODE GENERATION TREE MAY MATCH A CLASS OF
PRODUCTIONS DEFINED BY A ^V^A^L^U^E KLUDGE.
.PARAGRAPH
^C^O^T^R^E^E PERFORMS THE FOLLOWING ALGORITHM:  ^IT IS CALLED ONCE FROM ^P^A^R^S^E WITH
EACH ITEM OF PARSER OUTPUT.  ^THE ITEM IS ADDED TO THE BOTTOM OF THE CODE GENERATION STACK.  ^IF IT IS A TERMINAL SYMBOL, NO OTHER ACTION IS TAKEN.  ^IF IT IS A
PRODUCTION NUMBER, AN ATTEMPT IS MADE TO MATCH SOME SUBSTRING IN THE STACK WITH
SOME PATTERN IN THE CODE GENERATION TREE.  ^A MATCH IS ATTEMPTED STARTING AT
EACH NODE IN THE STACK, STARTING AT THE TOP.
.LEFT MARGIN 7
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
1. ^IF A MATCH IS FOUND WHICH MIGHT BE CONTINUED PAST THE END OF THE STACK,
NO ACTION IS TAKEN, AND ^C^O^T^R^E^E RETURNS CONTROL TO THE PARSER FOR MORE
PARSER OUTPUT.  ^IN THIS CASE, MORE CONTEXT IS NEEDED TO DETERMINE
WHETHER THIS IS A SPECIAL CASE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
2. ^IF NO MATCH IS FOUND AT ANY POSITION, ANOTHER SYMBOL IS REQUESTED FROM
THE PARSER.  ^THIS IS IN FACT AN ERROR CONDITION, BUT IS DETECTED ONLY
AT THE COMPLETION OF THE PARSE.
.SKIP
.INDENT -3
3. ^IF A MATCH IS FOUND, ALL THE WAY THROUGH TO A TERMINAL NODE OF THE CODE
GENERATION TREE, THE APPROPRIATE SEMANTICS ARE APPLIED.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SEMANTICS ARE APPLIED BY MOVING THE OBJECTS IN THE MATCHED SECTION OF
THE STACK ONTO THE SEMANTICS STACK, AND CALLING THE SEMANTICS MODULE, ^D^O^S^E^M,
WITH THE INDEX IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY OF THE SEMANTICS TO BE INVOKED.  ^D^O^S^E^M
WILL USUALLY SUPPLY A SINGLE OBJECT AS THE RESULT OF THE SEMANTICS.  ^C^O^T^R^E^E THEN
INSERTS THIS OBJECT INTO THE STACK IN PLACE OF ALL THE OBJECTS AND PRODUCTIONS
MATCHED, AND GOES BACK TO LOOK FOR ANOTHER PATTERN MATCH.
.PARAGRAPH
^QUOTED SEMANTICS ARE STORED IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY ESSENTIALLY AS PARSER
OUTPUT, WITH SPECIAL INDICATORS FOR LOCAL SYMBOLS AND FOR PLACES WHERE THE ARGUMENTS ARE TO BE INSERTED (COPYING THE ASSOCIATED CODE, IF ANY, FOR ALL BUT THE
FIRST INSERTION OF EACH ARGUMENT).  ^D^O^S^E^M PERFORMS QUOTED SEMANTICS BY CALLING
^C^O^D^E^N^T, IN THE CODE GENERATION STACK PACKAGE, TO ADD EACH ITEM IN THE QUOTED
SEMANTICS ONTO THE BOTTOM OF THE CODE GENERATION STACK.  ^C^O^D^E^N^T ALSO SETS A FLAG
FOR ^C^O^T^R^E^E, SO THAT WHEN ^D^O^S^E^M RETURNS CONTROL TO ^C^O^T^R^E^E SPECIAL ACTION IS TAKEN
TO REARRANGE THE STACK SO THAT THE ENTIRE RESULT OF THE QUOTED SEMANTICS IS
INSERTED IN PLACE OF THE MATCHED PORTION OF THE STACK.  ^THUS, IT IS QUITE POSSIBLE TO HAVE SEMANTICS GENERATE MORE ITEMS ON THE STACK THAN WERE NECESSARY TO
INVOKE IT.  ^THIS PROCESS IS USED IN THE SEMANTICS FOR ^I^M^P72 CONDITIONALS, WHERE
SEVERAL QUOTED SEMANTIC REWRITES OF A CONDITIONAL EXPRESSION MAY BE USED TO PUT
IT INTO A FORM WHICH GENERATES GOOD OBJECT CODE.
.PARAGRAPH
^THERE IS ALSO A SPECIAL SWITCH IN ^C^O^T^R^E^E WHICH CAUSES IT NOT TO ENTER THE
PARSER OUTPUT IN THE CODE GENERATION STACK AT ALL, BUT RATHER TO STORE IT IN AN
ARRAY FOR LATER INTERPRETATION BY CERTAIN SEMANTIC ROUTINES.  ^THE SEMANTIC ROUTINES ^M^A^X^W^E^L^L AND ^M^A^X^E^N^D TURN THIS SWITCH ON AND OFF REPSECTIVELY, THE PRODUCTION INVOKING THE LATTER MAKING USE OF THE ^P^R^I^O^R^I^T^Y MECHANISM TO AVOID BEING
STORED AND IGNORED ITSELF.  ^THIS MECHANISM IS USED TO IMPLEMENT QUOTED SEMANTICS
AND SEMANTIC CONDITIONALS.
.SKIP 4
.SUBTITLE 4.3: ^SEMANTICS AND ^CODE ^GENERATION.
.CENTER
^&4.3: ^SEMANTICS AND ^CODE ^GENERATION.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^SEMANTIC ROUTINES ARE INVOKED BY ^D^O^S^E^M, WHICH MERELY INTERPRETS THE ENTRIES
IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY.  ^THE ROUTINE IS COMPLETELY EXPLAINED BY THE DATA STRUCTURE, WHICH IS WELL-DOCUMENTED ON THE SOURCE FILE.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SEMANTIC ROUTINES THAT GENERATE CODE (^C^O^D^E, ^C^O^D^E2, ^C^O^D^E3 AND ^I^M^P^S^E^M) BUILD FOR EACH OBJECT A CHAIN
OF RECORDS IN A FREE STORAGE ARRAY.  ^A RECORD IS ONE OPERATION OF THE ABSTRACT MACHINE (^SECTION 2.3.3.1.2) WITH ITS REGISTER AND ITS OPERAND, OR A TAG,
OR A CONSTANT OF A ^D^A^T^A STATEMENT.  ^H^O^O^K JOINS CHAINS.  ^WHEN THE PARSE OF A
STATEMENT OF THE MAIN PROGRAM OR OF A ROUTINE IS COMPLETE, ^A^S^S^E^M^C PASSES THE CHAINS TO ^D^E^M^I^T, WHICH
WALKS THEM AND WRITES THE TEXT OF THE INTERMEDIATE REPRESENTATION: ONE FUNCTION FOR EACH ROUTINE, WITH ITS VARIABLES, ITS CALLS AND ITS
JUMPS.  ^I^R2^L^L TRANSLATES THAT TEXT TO A MODULE OF ^L^L^V^M ^I^R, AND ^L^L^V^M MAKES FROM THE MODULE WHAT THE OPTIONS ASK FOR: AN OBJECT FILE, THE
ASSEMBLY OF THE TARGET, THE ^I^R TEXT, OR A PROGRAM RUN BY ^O^R^C IN THE PROCESS.  ^THE DRIVER LINKS OBJECTS WITH THE RUNTIME LIBRARY.
^REGISTER ALLOCATION, INSTRUCTION SELECTION AND THE OPTIMIZATION OF THE CODE ARE DONE BY ^L^L^V^M.
.PARAGRAPH
(^THE CONFUSING ROUTINES IN THE SEMANTICS PACKAGE, AND THERE ARE VERY CONFUSING ONES INDEED, HAVE TO DO WITH THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX, WHICH IS DISCUSSED
BELOW.)
.SUBTITLE 4.4: ^HOW ^EXTENSIBILITY IS ^IMPLEMENTED.
.PAGE
.INDEX \\EXTENSIBILITY, IMPLEMENTATION OF
.CENTER
^&4.4: ^HOW ^EXTENSIBILITY IS ^IMPLEMENTED.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SYNTAX OF ^I^M^P72 IS STORED IN A SYNTAX GRAPH AND TWO ASSOCIATED CONNECTIVITY MATRICES, AS DESCRIBED IN (^IRONS 1971), EXCEPT THAT THE GRAPH IS NOT
BACK-OPTIMIZED, IN ORDER TO BE ABLE TO ADD NEW PRODUCTIONS WITHOUT GREAT FUSS.
^NEW PRODUCTIONS ARE ADDED TO THE GRAPH, AND THE MATRIX UPDATED, BY SUBROUTINE
^G^R^A^P^H, WHICH IS CALLED WITH ONE PRODUCTION.  ^THE PARSER ACCESSES THE GRAPH VIA
OTHER SUBROUTINES IN THE SYNTAX GRAPH MODULE.
.PARAGRAPH
^INITIALLY, THE GRAPH IS EMPTY.  ^SUBROUTINE ^R^S^Y^N HAS BUILT INTO IT A SYNTAX
AND SEMANTICS FOR VERY SIMPLE SYNTAX STATEMENTS.  ^THE PROCESS OF CREATING AN ^I^M^P
COMPILER BEGINS BY CALLING ^R^S^Y^N (FROM THE DRIVER PROGRAM ^I^M^P^M) TO READ A SIMILAR
SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS FROM THE FIRST PART OF THE SYNTAX FILE, UP TO ITS FIRST % SIGN.
^ONCE THIS IS DONE, THE COMPILER IS BOOTSTRAPPED (IT SAYS ^S^Y^N^T^A^X ^B^O^O^T^S^T^R^A^P^P^E^D WHEN IT IS NOT RUN QUIETLY) AND USES THE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS IN ITS INTERNAL TABLES.  ^THE FIRST
STATEMENTS IT READS ARE THE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS FOR MORE COMPLEX SYNTAX STATEMENTS.  ^AFTER THIS COMES THE SYNTAX FOR THE COMPUTATIONAL PORTION OF THE LANGUAGE.
^THE COMPILER THEN GOES ON TO THE FILES NAMED ON THE COMMAND LINE.  ^THUS, EXCEPT FOR
LEXICAL CONVENTIONS AND THE SYNTAX BUILT INTO ^R^S^Y^N (WHICH IS ONLY USED TO BOOTSTRAP THE COMPILER), THE ENTIRE SYNTAX AND SEMANTICS FOR ^I^M^P72 COMES FROM FILE
^[syntax64.imc^] AND THE SEMANTIC SUBROUTINES REFERRED TO ON IT.  ^THE COMPILER CAN BE SAVED AFTER IT HAS READ ITS SYNTAX (^SECTION 3.4).
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OTHER ROUTINES ON FILES ^R^S^Y^N AND ^R^S^Y^N2 IMPLEMENT THE SEMANTICS FOR SYNTAX STATEMENTS.  ^THEY WERE AT ONCE THE FIRST AND THE MOST COMPLEX SEMANTIC ROUTINES IMPLEMENTED, AND, TO PUT IT KINDLY, ARE RATHER OBSCURE.  ^THE ROUTINES
^S^Y^T^R^M, ^S^Y^N^T, AND ^S^Y^N^T^S BUILD UP A PRODUCTION IN ARRAY ^S^N WHICH IS THEN ENTERED
IN THE SYNTAX GRAPH BY SEMANTIC ROUTINE ^S^Y^N^T^A^X.  ^AT THIS POINT, A LIST OF THE
NAMES OF THE ARGUMENTS OF THE PRODUCTION IS IN ARRAY ^N^A FOR USE IN COMPILING
THE SEMANTICS.
.PARAGRAPH
^BASICALLY, SEMANTICS ARE COMPILED INTO ARRAY ^S^E^M^S IN THE SEMANTICS PACKAGE
VIA CALLS ON ^S^E^T^S^E^M.  ^SEMANTIC ROUTINE CALLS ARE COMPILED BY SEMANTIC ROUTINES
^S^E^M^P AND ^S^E^M^R, WHICH CHECK ^N^A TO SEE IF IDENTIFIERS ARE ARGUMENTS OF THE PRODUCTION.
.PARAGRAPH
^QUOTED SEMANTICS ARE PROCESSED BY SEMANTIC ROUTINE ^Q^U^O^S^E^M, WHICH CONVERTS
PARSER OUTPUT INTO SEMANTICS WHICH IT ENTERS IN THE SEMANTICS ARRAY VIA ^S^E^T^S^E^M.
.PARAGRAPH
^WHEN A SEMANTIC CONDITION IS ENCOUNTERED, SEMANTIC ROUTINE ^Q^U^O^S^E^M ENTERS THE
PATTERN IN THE CODE GENERATION TREE WITH A CALL TO ^E^N^T^R^E^E, WHICH RETURNS A LIST
OF THE FORMAL ARGUMENTS OF THE CONDITION.  ^Q^U^O^S^E^M THEN CALLS ^S^E^M^F^I^X (IN THE
SEMANTIC PACKAGE) TO PROCESS THE SEMANTICS, WHICH HAVE ALREADY BEEN ENTERED IN
THE SEMANTICS ARRAY, TO CONFORM WITH THE NEW LIST OF FORMAL ARGUMENTS.
.PARAGRAPH
^WHEN A MODIFIER FOR A ^V^A^L^U^E KLUDGE IS ENCOUNTERED ON QUOTED SEMANTICS,
SEMANTIC ROUTINE ^R^E^P^V^A^L PERFORMS THE NECESSARY ALTERATION ON THE PARSER OUTPUT
BEFORE ^Q^U^O^S^E^M GETS IT.
.PARAGRAPH
^SEMANTIC PACKAGE ROUTINE ^S^E^M^F^X1 IS CALLED TO PERFORM A SIMILAR ALTERATION ON
DEFAULT CASE SEMANTICS IN THE EVENT THAT A ^V^A^L^U^E KLUDGE APPLIES TO THEM.
.SUBTITLE 5: ^SOURCE AND ^LICENCE.
.PAGE
.INDEX ^S^O^U^R^C^E
.CENTER
^&5: ^SOURCE AND ^LICENCE.\&
.SKIP 2
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SOURCES OF THE COMPILER, OF THE RUNTIME LIBRARY, OF THIS MANUAL AND OF THE TESTS ARE IN ONE TREE.  ^ITS MAIN DIRECTORIES ARE
^[src^] (THE COMPILER: THE KERNEL IN ^[src^], THE CODE GENERATOR IN ^[src/llvm-ir^]), ^[lib^] (THE RUNTIME LIBRARY AND ^[ffi.imc^]),
^[tests^], ^[tools^] (THE TOOLS THAT MAKE THIS MANUAL AMONG THEM), ^[docs^] AND ^[decus^] (THE ORIGINAL COMPILER, AS IT WAS DISTRIBUTED
BY ^D^E^C^U^S, PROGRAM 10-198, FOR THE ^P^D^P-10).  ^THE MANUAL IS ^[docs/manual/imp72.rno^], A ^R^U^N^O^F^F SOURCE;
THE PAGE ^[imp72.7^] AND THE FILE ^[imp72.md^] ARE MADE FROM IT BY THE TOOLS ^[runoff2mdoc^] AND ^[runoff2md^].
.PARAGRAPH
^THE SOFTWARE IS DISTRIBUTED UNDER THE ^I^S^C LICENCE (THE FILE ^[LICENSE^]): PERMISSION TO USE, COPY, MODIFY AND DISTRIBUTE IT FOR ANY
PURPOSE, WITH OR WITHOUT FEE, IS GRANTED PROVIDED THAT THE COPYRIGHT NOTICE AND THE PERMISSION NOTICE APPEAR IN ALL COPIES; THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ORIGINAL COMPILER WAS DESIGNED AND IMPLEMENTED BY ^WALT ^BILOFSKY AT THE ^YALE ^UNIVERSITY ^DEPARTMENT OF ^COMPUTER ^SCIENCE,
BASED LARGELY ON PREVIOUS WORK OF ^E. ^T. ^IRONS, WITH SUBSTANTIAL CONTRIBUTIONS BY ^STEVEN ^WEINGART AND ^TERRY ^LYONS.
.SUBTITLE ^APPENDIX ^I. ^RUNTIME ^LIBRARY ^ROUTINES.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&^APPENDIX ^I. ^RUNTIME ^LIBRARY ^ROUTINES.\&
.SKIP
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^I^M^P RUN-TIME LIBRARY, FILE ^[libimp72.a^], CONTAINS THE ROUTINES THAT THE CODE OF A PROGRAM CALLS (^P^R^I^N^T AND ^R^E^A^D AMONG THEM)
AND THE ROUTINES BELOW, WHICH A PROGRAM MAY CALL.  ^A PROGRAM CALLS THE ^[C^] LIBRARY THROUGH ^C^C^A^L^L (^SECTION 2.4.1).  ^THE ARGUMENTS OF AN ^I^M^P
ROUTINE ARE ADDRESSES, SO THAT IN A CALL WITH ^C^C^A^L^L THE ADDRESS OF A VARIABLE IS ITS ARGUMENT.  ^NAMES ARE CASE SENSITIVE.
.LEFT MARGIN 5
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^F^I^N^I(^I): CLOSES ALL THE FILES AND RETURNS THE OUTPUT AND THE INPUT TO THE STANDARD FILES; EXITS FROM THE PROGRAM IF ^I IS 0 (^SECTION 2.2.7).
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^I^F^I^X(^X): THE INTEGER PART OF THE ^R^E^A^L ^X, TRUNCATED TOWARD ZERO.  ^A VALUE BEYOND THE RANGE IS THE LEAST INTEGER.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
IMPARGC(0): THE NUMBER OF ARGUMENTS OF THE COMMAND LINE OF THE PROGRAM.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
IMPARGVP(0): THE ADDRESS OF THE ARRAY OF POINTERS TO THE ARGUMENTS.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^I^M^P^A^R^G^E^T(^O^U^T,^V,^I): STORES ELEMENT ^I OF THE ARRAY OF POINTERS ^V (A ^[char *[]^]) IN THE WORD AT THE ADDRESS ^O^U^T.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^I^M^P^A^R^G^S^E^T(^V,^I,^P): STORES THE POINTER ^P AS ELEMENT ^I OF THE ARRAY ^V.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
^I^M^P^S^T^D^I^N(0), ^I^M^P^S^T^D^O^U^T(0): THE ^[FILE *^] OF THE STANDARD INPUT AND THE STANDARD OUTPUT OF THE ^[C^] LIBRARY, TO GIVE TO ^[fputs^], ^[fgets^] AND THE LIKE WITH ^C^C^A^L^L.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
IMPCALLI(^C^O^D^E,^A^C): THE ROUTINE THAT ^C^A^L^L^I(^C^O^D^E,^A^C) CALLS (^SECTION 2.2.9).
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
IMPBLK(^A,^B,^N): THE ROUTINE THAT ^M^O^V^E CALLS: COPIES ^N+1 WORDS FROM ^B TO ^A.
.SKIP
.INDENT -5
IMPMAIN: THE FUNCTION THAT THE STATEMENTS BEFORE THE FIRST ^S^U^B^R OF A FILE MAKE.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OTHER SYMBOLS OF THE LIBRARY ARE LOWER CASE NAMES THAT BEGIN WITH ^[imp^] (^[imppr^], ^[imprd^], ^[impxct^], ^[impibp^], ^[impldb^], ^[impdpb^],
^[implsh^], ^[impash^], ^[improt^], ^[impblt^], ^[impac^], ^[impca^], ^[impbpp^], ^[impbpv^], ^[impxw^]) OR UPPER CASE NAMES THAT BEGIN WITH ^[RTL^], ^[SC^]
OR ^[IMPCORE^]: THE COMPILER CALLS THEM FOR ^P^R^I^N^T, ^R^E^A^D, BYTE POINTERS, SHIFTS BY A VARIABLE COUNT, ^E^X^E^C^U^T^E AND THE LIKE, AND THE
LIBRARY USES THEM ITSELF.  ^A PROGRAM SHOULD NOT DEFINE A NAME OF THAT KIND.
.SUBTITLE ^APPENDIX ^I^I: ^SYNTAX OF ^^IMP72\\.
.PAGE
.CENTER
^&^APPENDIX ^I^I: ^SYNTAX OF ^^IMP\\72.\&
.RIGHT MARGIN 80
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP 2
.TAB STOPS 11
.NOFILL
.INDEX SYNTAX OF ^^IMP\\
^^<NAM>	::= NAM"\\
^^<SYM>	::= SYM"\\
